"{\"schemaVersion\":1,\"thread\":{\"id\":\"01a03544-ddce-77a1-b5af-44df07c54f4b\",\"kind\":\"codex\",\"hostId\":\"remote-ssh-discovered:black-chatgpt-2\",\"title\":\"Operator MFP L=2, general t\",\"preview\":\"can you read about MFP (mean field peeling) from this project?&#x20;\",\"status\":{\"type\":\"notLoaded\"},\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"createdAt\":1787600100,\"updatedAt\":1787600100},\"page\":{\"order\":\"newest_first\",\"limit\":10,\"nextCursor\":\"01a03fe8-946d-7331-8e04-0201fad2a2cd\",\"hasMore\":true},\"turns\":[{\"id\":\"01a0497c-1770-78d0-b74b-84b633de4dcd\",\"status\":\"interrupted\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1787939264,\"completedAt\":1787941138,\"durationMs\":1874505,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1973\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"can you lift your negative ODE result to a negative PDE result for the same exact linear model? meaning, everything and statement the same but allow partial derivatives wrt to each other and not only time  to exist int he equations, and resolve this fully and completely,  and  then apply the same level of rigor on it , fully independent and adversarial audits, seeing if passes rigorous and validity tests. if issues come up you can fix, fix them and pass it through the same strict and rigorous and independent audits, do this enough times so that the final super strict and rigorous and independent and adversarial test with isolated independent diversified agents pass. your audits MUST include very very robust and careful checks of any invoked theorems, checking their source, obligations, assertions, if they match, and in fact reading the proof of that invoked theorem, to see if you are convinced the proof is correct and theorem obligations are valid, this must be done at all steps of your auditing (perhaps delegated to a sub agent specialized for this), this might have to even go one level deeper if you deem appropriate, unless the stated invoked theorem is a super classical result not a research level result, which you are 100% absolutely positively certain it is correct and there's no need to double check it. all else demands further inspection and auditing\\n\\nYou have to continue until full resolution, which means the proven result is false or true, you have to prove either case and cannot stop your rounds otherwise. In either case, output the fully self contained rigorous and cohesive report detailing your argument\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1974\",\"summary\":[\"**Formalizing PDE interpretation for ODE lifting**\",\"**Constructing positive PDE counterexample**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1975\",\"text\":\"I’m using the rigorous-math workflow because the first issue is logical, not computational: “PDE” must be formalized tightly enough to avoid either a vacuous impossibility or a trivial encoding theorem. I’ll test both directions independently—whether the graph-ladder obstruction survives finite jets, and whether one field with a spatial coordinate can already encode the full hierarchy.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_pUvQxUm7KyXtXGj89APpjBXL\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-6543-7790-b508-1baed56f9705\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_no_go_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_rzbT04OpVm9JA7MrFBDrZsdo\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-7a20-70e1-8014-49e68e54da29\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_encoding_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_n2I10MSWhbCUPJANRzRGt1BF\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-8e2a-7722-8559-0006b47448aa\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_scope_adversary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1976\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing PDE embedding of ODEs**\",\"**Assessing PDE construction for universal negative claim**\",\"**Extending no-go proof under finite-jet PDE constraints**\",\"**Assessing PDE construction for universal negative claim**\",\"**Extending no-go proof under finite-jet PDE constraints**\",\"**Assessing PDE construction for universal negative claim**\",\"**Extending no-go proof under finite-jet PDE constraints**\",\"**Constructing state-universal PDE counterexample with jet encoding**\",\"**Assessing PDE construction for universal negative claim**\",\"**Extending no-go proof under finite-jet PDE constraints**\",\"**Constructing state-universal PDE counterexample with jet encoding**\",\"**Formalizing PDE no-go under finite graph contraction fields**\",\"**Assessing PDE construction for universal negative claim**\",\"**Extending no-go proof under finite-jet PDE constraints**\",\"**Constructing state-universal PDE counterexample with jet encoding**\",\"**Formalizing PDE no-go under finite graph contraction fields**\",\"**Detailing formal PDE no-go framework with bounded contraction graphs**\",\"**Assessing PDE construction for universal negative claim**\",\"**Extending no-go proof under finite-jet PDE constraints**\",\"**Constructing state-universal PDE counterexample with jet encoding**\",\"**Formalizing PDE no-go under finite graph contraction fields**\",\"**Detailing formal PDE no-go framework with bounded contraction graphs**\",\"**Extending inductive PDE no-go via jet differential polynomial degrees**\",\"**Assessing PDE construction for universal negative claim**\",\"**Extending no-go proof under finite-jet PDE constraints**\",\"**Constructing state-universal PDE counterexample with jet encoding**\",\"**Formalizing PDE no-go under finite graph contraction fields**\",\"**Detailing formal PDE no-go framework with bounded contraction graphs**\",\"**Extending inductive PDE no-go via jet differential polynomial degrees**\",\"**Assessing PDE construction for universal negative claim**\",\"**Extending no-go proof under finite-jet PDE constraints**\",\"**Constructing state-universal PDE counterexample with jet encoding**\",\"**Formalizing PDE no-go under finite graph contraction fields**\",\"**Detailing formal PDE no-go framework with bounded contraction graphs**\",\"**Extending inductive PDE no-go via jet differential polynomial degrees**\",\"**Clarifying PDE no-go boundary and universal counterexample limits**\",\"**Assessing PDE construction for universal negative claim**\",\"**Extending no-go proof under finite-jet PDE constraints**\",\"**Constructing state-universal PDE counterexample with jet encoding**\",\"**Formalizing PDE no-go under finite graph contraction fields**\",\"**Detailing formal PDE no-go framework with bounded contraction graphs**\",\"**Extending inductive PDE no-go via jet differential polynomial degrees**\",\"**Clarifying PDE no-go boundary and universal counterexample limits**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1977\",\"text\":\"The key boundary is emerging sharply. If every PDE field is itself a uniformly bounded-degree contraction expression—as “everything else unchanged” suggests—then spatial differentiation cannot create a new connected graph type, and the old ladder proof does extend. But if only the PDE’s formula has bounded degree while its initial field profile may contain infinitely many modes, a single linear transport equation already stores the whole trajectory. I’m proving both statements so the quantifier boundary is explicit rather than hidden.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0497c-657f-7d51-8ec6-e3ea863cfde7\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-6543-7790-b508-1baed56f9705\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_no_go_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1978\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing connected component contradictions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0497c-8e65-75a3-b692-868cc56ff42e\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-8e2a-7722-8559-0006b47448aa\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_scope_adversary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1979\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying operator action on basis**\",\"**Verifying operator action on basis**\",\"**Analyzing PDE well-posedness and function spaces**\",\"**Formulating physical-time no-go proof**\",\"**Formulating physical-time no-go proof**\",\"**Verifying positivity and independence in graph coefficients**\",\"**Analyzing analyticity and scaling in jet evaluations**\",\"**Examining injective-index polynomial uniqueness**\",\"**Analyzing analyticity and scaling in jet evaluations**\",\"**Examining injective-index polynomial uniqueness**\",\"**Analyzing analyticity and scaling in jet evaluations**\",\"**Examining injective-index polynomial uniqueness**\",\"**Establishing analytic flow and PDE representation**\",\"**Analyzing analyticity and scaling in jet evaluations**\",\"**Examining injective-index polynomial uniqueness**\",\"**Establishing analytic flow and PDE representation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1980\",\"text\":\"The resolution is now definite rather than conditional:\\n\\n- The genuine lift is true for fixed finite-order local PDEs whose field profiles remain uniformly bounded-degree contraction expressions.\\n- The unrestricted claim “no \\\\(O(1)\\\\)-field PDE” is false—even one scalar field satisfying \\\\(U_t=U_s\\\\) can encode the complete restartable future-observable profile.\\n- The old connected-path ladder gives a quantitative necessity: any exact PDE outside the no-go class must hide graph types of unbounded degree in progressively higher spatial jets.\\n\\nI’m writing this as a sharp theorem with both directions, including the physical-loss clock.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-32f87200-9a8c-406a-984e-3bcb15fac31b\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Three hidden linear layers: the exact PDE closure boundary\\n\\n## 1. Verdict\\n\\nThere are two distinct statements that must not be conflated.\\n\\n1. The finite-contraction ODE no-go **does extend** to every fixed local PDE\\n   of finite differential order whose fields, uniformly over their spatial\\n   variables, are bounded-degree complete contractions of the current network\\n   tensors. Mixed spatial partial derivatives do not evade the obstruction.\\n\\n2. The unrestricted assertion that no fixed finite number of scalar PDE\\n   fields can represent the dynamics is **false**. One scalar field governed\\n   by the linear transport equation stores the entire ordered-word hierarchy\\n   in its spatial profile. Field count is not numerical state dimension.\\n\\nThe theorem below proves both claims and identifies the exact missing\\nhypothesis: finite contraction provenance of the whole spatial jet.\\n\\nNo external research theorem is used.\\n\\n## 2. Exact model and feature derivation\\n\\nAt width \\\\(n\\\\), let\\n\\n\\\\[\\na,x\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n,qquad B,R\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n},qquad\\nf_n=a^{\\\\mathsf T}RBx.\\n\\\\tag{2.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe feature-ascent vector field is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\na'&=RBx,&R'&=a(Bx)^{\\\\mathsf T},\\\\\\\\\\nB'&=(R^{\\\\mathsf T}a)x^{\\\\mathsf T},&\\nx'&=B^{\\\\mathsf T}R^{\\\\mathsf T}a.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{2.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWrite \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n\\\\) for the derivation on polynomials in the entries of\\n\\\\((a,R,B,x)\\\\) induced by (2.2). For squared loss \\\\((y-f_n)^2\\\\), physical\\ngradient flow is generated by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr X_n=2\\\\eta(y-f_n)\\\\mathscr D_n.\\n\\\\tag{2.3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll no-go statements below are state-universal: their identities must hold\\nfor every state in a nonempty open set and every sufficiently large width,\\nnot merely along the Gaussian initialization orbit.\\n\\n## 3. The PDE class\\n\\nFix independently of \\\\(n\\\\):\\n\\n- an open spatial domain \\\\(\\\\Omega\\\\subset\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\);\\n- a number \\\\(q\\\\) of scalar fields;\\n- finite differential orders \\\\(r,\\\\rho\\\\);\\n- a tensor-degree bound \\\\(D\\\\);\\n- finitely many readout points \\\\(\\\\zeta_1,\\\\ldots,\\\\zeta_s\\\\in\\\\Omega\\\\).\\n\\nA typed complete-contraction graph is a scalar tensor network made from\\ncopies of \\\\(a,x,B,R\\\\), with every layer index paired and summed. Its tensor\\ndegree is the number of tensor copies. Let \\\\(P_G^{(n)}\\\\) denote the resulting\\npolynomial.\\n\\nA **uniform bounded-contraction encoder** is a family\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_n=(U_{1,n},\\\\ldots,U_{q,n}),qquad\\nU_{\\\\alpha,n}:\\\\Omega\\\\times\\\\Theta_n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\mathbb R,\\n\\\\tag{3.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nof the form\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_{\\\\alpha,n}(\\\\zeta;\\\\theta)\\n=\\\\sum_{G\\\\in\\\\mathcal G_D}c_{\\\\alpha,G,n}(\\\\zeta)P_G^{(n)}(\\\\theta).\\n\\\\tag{3.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere \\\\(\\\\mathcal G_D\\\\) is the finite set of typed graphs of degree at most\\n\\\\(D\\\\), and the coefficient functions are smooth. They may depend on \\\\(n\\\\);\\nthis only strengthens the no-go theorem. Finiteness of \\\\(\\\\mathcal G_D\\\\)\\nfollows because there are finitely many tensor occurrences, typed index\\nvertices, and pairings at bounded degree.\\n\\nA fixed local differential-polynomial PDE closure consists of polynomials\\n\\\\(F_\\\\alpha\\\\), independent of \\\\(n\\\\) and the state, such that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr D_nU_{\\\\alpha,n}(\\\\zeta;\\\\theta)\\n=F_\\\\alpha\\\\!\\\\left(\\n\\\\zeta,\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\partial_\\\\zeta^\\\\beta U_{\\\\gamma,n}(\\\\zeta;\\\\theta)\\\\bigr)_\\n{1\\\\le\\\\gamma\\\\le q,\\\\ |\\\\beta|\\\\le r}\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{3.3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe coefficients of \\\\(F_\\\\alpha\\\\) may be arbitrary fixed smooth functions of\\n\\\\(\\\\zeta\\\\). All mixed partial derivatives in the \\\\(d\\\\) spatial variables are\\nallowed. The output is required to be a fixed polynomial readout of finitely\\nmany finite jets,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_n=G\\\\!\\\\left(\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\partial_\\\\zeta^\\\\beta U_{\\\\alpha,n}(\\\\zeta_j;\\\\theta)\\\\bigr)_\\n{\\\\alpha,j,\\\\ |\\\\beta|\\\\le\\\\rho}\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{3.4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTaking \\\\(U_{1,n}(\\\\zeta_1)=f_n\\\\) is the special case corresponding exactly\\nto the earlier ODE theorem.\\n\\nThe phrase “bounded-degree PDE” is otherwise ambiguous. In (3.2) the bound\\nconcerns dependence of the encoded fields on the network tensors; in (3.3)\\nit concerns the local differential expression. Both are needed for a\\nfinite-information theorem.\\n\\n## 4. Main theorem\\n\\n### Theorem 1\\n\\nFor the network (2.1)--(2.2):\\n\\n1. No encoder and local PDE satisfying (3.1)--(3.4) exists for all\\n   sufficiently large \\\\(n\\\\).\\n\\n2. The same is true if the differential polynomials and readout are replaced\\n   by real-analytic germs and the identities hold on a full neighborhood of\\n   the zero network state.\\n\\n3. The same two conclusions hold for the physical derivation (2.3), for\\n   every label \\\\(y\\\\in\\\\mathbb R\\\\).\\n\\n4. If condition (3.2) is deleted, the corresponding universal no-PDE claim\\n   is false. There is a state-universal, autonomous, restartable realization\\n   using one scalar field, one spatial variable, and the fixed degree-one,\\n   first-order PDE\\n\\n   \\\\[\\n   \\\\partial_tU=\\\\partial_sU.\\n   \\\\tag{4.1}\\n   \\\\]\\n\\nThus the theorem rules out finite bounded-contraction PDE closures, not\\nPDEs whose spatial profiles store infinitely many moments.\\n\\n## 5. Stable independence of contraction graphs\\n\\nWe first prove the coefficient-comparison fact used below.\\n\\n### Lemma 2\\n\\nEvery fixed finite family of pairwise nonisomorphic typed complete-\\ncontraction graphs has linearly independent contraction polynomials in all\\nsufficiently large widths.\\n\\n### Proof\\n\\nTreat the tensor entries as independent commuting indeterminates. For a graph\\n\\\\(G\\\\), let \\\\(I_G^{(n)}\\\\) be its injective contraction: distinct index vertices\\nof the same type must receive distinct indices. Splitting unrestricted index\\nmaps according to their equality partition gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_G^{(n)}=\\\\sum_\\\\pi I_{G/\\\\pi}^{(n)},\\n\\\\tag{5.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\pi\\\\) ranges over type-compatible partitions. Ordered by the number\\nof index vertices, (5.1) is triangular with diagonal coefficient one. Finite\\nMöbius inversion on the partition poset therefore expresses every \\\\(I_G\\\\) as\\na linear combination of unrestricted contractions.\\n\\nSuppose a finite linear combination of injective contractions vanishes.\\nChoose a graph \\\\(G_0\\\\) with nonzero coefficient and a maximal number of typed\\nindex vertices. At widths at least those vertex counts, label all vertices of\\n\\\\(G_0\\\\) injectively. The resulting tensor-entry monomial records every tensor\\ntype and the two incident typed indices of every tensor occurrence. Hence the\\nsame monomial can occur in \\\\(I_H\\\\) only if \\\\(H\\\\) has the same vertices and\\nincidences, namely only if \\\\(H\\\\) is type-preservingly isomorphic to \\\\(G_0\\\\).\\nIts coefficient cannot cancel. This contradiction proves independence of the\\n\\\\(I_G\\\\), and the invertible triangular transformation proves independence of\\nthe \\\\(P_G\\\\). \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n\\nThe stable-width qualification is necessary: fixed-dimensional\\nCayley--Hamilton identities relate sufficiently long words.\\n\\n## 6. The connected-path ladder\\n\\nPut\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA=RB,qquad C=A^{\\\\mathsf T}A=B^{\\\\mathsf T}R^{\\\\mathsf T}RB.\\n\\\\tag{6.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}\\\\) retain only endpoint differentiation:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}a=Ax,qquad\\n\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}x=A^{\\\\mathsf T}a,qquad\\n\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}A=0.\\n\\\\tag{6.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor \\\\(j\\\\ge0\\\\), define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_j=a^{\\\\mathsf T}AC^jx,quad\\nX_j=x^{\\\\mathsf T}C^{j+1}x,quad\\nY_j=a^{\\\\mathsf T}(AA^{\\\\mathsf T})^{j+1}a.\\n\\\\tag{6.3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUsing\\n\\\\(AC^jA^{\\\\mathsf T}=(AA^{\\\\mathsf T})^{j+1}\\\\) and\\n\\\\((AA^{\\\\mathsf T})^{j+1}A=AC^{j+1}\\\\), the product rule gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}U_j=X_j+Y_j,qquad\\n\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}X_j=\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}Y_j=2U_{j+1}.\\n\\\\tag{6.4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince \\\\(U_0=f_n\\\\), induction yields\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}^{,2m-1}f_n\\n=4^{m-1}\\\\left[\\nx^{\\\\mathsf T}C^mx+a^{\\\\mathsf T}(AA^{\\\\mathsf T})^ma\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\tag{6.5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEvery coordinate of (2.2) is a monomial with coefficient \\\\(+1\\\\). Therefore\\nevery product-rule history in the full derivation has a nonnegative integer\\ncoefficient. The endpoint-only histories form a subset of those histories.\\nConsequently\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr D_n^{,2m-1}f_n\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{contains with positive coefficient}\\\\quad\\n4^{m-1}\\\\Gamma_m,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Gamma_m=x^{\\\\mathsf T}C^mx.\\n\\\\tag{6.6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe graph \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\) is connected, has tensor degree \\\\(4m+2\\\\), and the\\n\\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\) are pairwise nonisomorphic.\\n\\n## 7. Why finite spatial derivatives do not help\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathcal S\\\\) be the finite set of connected graph types that occur in\\nthe fields (3.2). Define\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_{\\\\mathcal S}^{(n)}\\n=\\\\operatorname{span}\\\\{P_H^{(n)}:\\n\\\\text{every connected component of }H\\\\text{ belongs to }\\\\mathcal S\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{7.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is a unital algebra because multiplication of contraction polynomials is\\ndisjoint union of their graphs.\\n\\nSpatial differentiation in (3.2) differentiates only the coefficient\\nfunctions. Hence, for every multi-index \\\\(\\\\beta\\\\), every field jet\\n\\\\(\\\\partial_\\\\zeta^\\\\beta U_{\\\\alpha,n}\\\\) belongs to\\n\\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{\\\\mathcal S}^{(n)}\\\\). This remains true at arbitrarily high\\nspatial order; the key point is the common finite graph alphabet, not a\\nfinite truncation of the jet.\\n\\nFor completeness, introduce formal jet variables \\\\(u_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta}\\\\). The\\nevolutionary prolongation of (3.3) acts on a differential polynomial \\\\(H\\\\) by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbf T_FH\\n=\\\\sum_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta}\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial H}{\\\\partial u_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta}}\\n\\\\,\\\\partial_\\\\zeta^\\\\beta F_\\\\alpha.\\n\\\\tag{7.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOnly finitely many terms occur for any fixed \\\\(H\\\\). Since the state\\nderivation and spatial differentiation act on different variables, they\\ncommute. The ordinary chain and product rules therefore give\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr D_n H(j^\\\\infty U_n)\\n=(\\\\mathbf T_FH)(j^\\\\infty U_n).\\n\\\\tag{7.3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nApplying (7.3) repeatedly to the readout polynomial gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr D_n^k f_n\\n=(\\\\mathbf T_F^kG)(j^\\\\infty U_n).\\n\\\\tag{7.4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor each fixed \\\\(k\\\\), the right-hand side uses only finitely many jets and is\\na finite polynomial in them. It consequently belongs to\\n\\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{\\\\mathcal S}^{(n)}\\\\).\\n\\nChoose \\\\(m\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\notin\\\\mathcal S\\\\). Equation (7.4) says that\\n\\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n^{2m-1}f_n\\\\) is a linear combination of graphs whose connected\\ncomponents lie in \\\\(\\\\mathcal S\\\\), while (6.6) says its coefficient of the new\\nconnected graph \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\) is positive. Lemma 2 makes these graph\\ncoefficients independent in sufficiently large width. This contradiction\\nproves Theorem 1(1).\\n\\nNotice that iteration may increase the spatial derivative order without\\nbound. It still cannot help: every such derivative remains in the same\\nfinite graph alphabet.\\n\\n## 8. Analytic differential expressions\\n\\nSuppose instead that the PDE and readout are analytic germs near the zero\\nnetwork state. Scale all four tensors by \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\). Every field jet is then\\na polynomial in \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\) of degree at most \\\\(D\\\\). Subtract its value at\\n\\\\(\\\\lambda=0\\\\); every remaining nonzero jet has strictly positive\\n\\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\)-order.\\n\\nAt any prescribed power \\\\(\\\\lambda^N\\\\), only finitely many monomials in the\\nconvergent Taylor expansions of the analytic PDE and readout can contribute:\\neach nonconstant factor has order at least one. Each contribution is a\\nproduct of field-jet coefficients and hence has connected components only\\nfrom \\\\(\\\\mathcal S\\\\).\\n\\nThe derivation \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n\\\\) maps a homogeneous tensor polynomial of\\ndegree \\\\(d\\\\) to one of degree \\\\(d+2\\\\). Thus\\n\\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n^{2m-1}f_n\\\\) is homogeneous of degree \\\\(4m+2\\\\). Comparing the\\ncoefficient of \\\\(\\\\lambda^{4m+2}\\\\) in the analytic version of (7.4) again\\nplaces it in \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{\\\\mathcal S}^{(n)}\\\\), contradicting (6.6). This\\nproves Theorem 1(2).\\n\\n## 9. Physical gradient flow\\n\\nFor \\\\(y\\\\ne0\\\\), split\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr X_n=2\\\\eta y\\\\mathscr D_n-2\\\\eta f_n\\\\mathscr D_n.\\n\\\\tag{9.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first summand raises tensor degree by two; the second raises it by six.\\nHence the lowest-degree homogeneous component of\\n\\\\(\\\\mathscr X_n^k f_n\\\\) is uniquely\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(2\\\\eta y)^k\\\\mathscr D_n^kf_n.\\n\\\\tag{9.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAt \\\\(k=2m-1\\\\), its \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\) coefficient is nonzero, so the preceding\\nargument applies degree by degree.\\n\\nFor \\\\(y=0\\\\), write\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathscr X_n^kf_n=(-2\\\\eta)^kQ_k,qquad\\nQ_0=f_n,qquad Q_{k+1}=f_n\\\\mathscr D_nQ_k.\\n\\\\tag{9.3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nInduction shows that \\\\(Q_k\\\\) contains\\n\\\\(f_n^k\\\\mathscr D_n^kf_n\\\\) with positive coefficient: applying\\n\\\\(f_n\\\\mathscr D_n\\\\) to that term produces\\n\\\\(f_n^{k+1}\\\\mathscr D_n^{k+1}f_n\\\\), and all other graph coefficients remain\\nnonnegative. For \\\\(k=2m-1\\\\), one connected component of this term is\\n\\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\). Choosing \\\\(m\\\\) beyond \\\\(\\\\mathcal S\\\\) gives the same stable\\ngraph contradiction. This proves Theorem 1(3).\\n\\n## 10. The counterexample to a broad PDE no-go\\n\\nThe failure without (3.2) is elementary and exact. Let \\\\(\\\\Phi_n^s\\\\) be the\\nlocal feature flow generated by (2.2). For a current state \\\\(\\\\theta\\\\), define\\none scalar spatial profile\\n\\n\\\\[\\nE_n(\\\\theta)(s)=f_n(\\\\Phi_n^s\\\\theta).\\n\\\\tag{10.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nStarting from \\\\(\\\\theta_0\\\\), set\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_n(t,s)=E_n(\\\\Phi_n^t\\\\theta_0)(s)\\n=f_n(\\\\Phi_n^{t+s}\\\\theta_0).\\n\\\\tag{10.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe flow property gives, wherever \\\\(t,s,t+s\\\\) lie in the existence interval,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\partial_tU_n=\\\\partial_sU_n,qquad\\nU_n(t,0)=f_n(\\\\Phi_n^t\\\\theta_0).\\n\\\\tag{10.3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is autonomous and restartable:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nE_n(\\\\Phi_n^t\\\\theta)(s)=E_n(\\\\theta)(s+t).\\n\\\\tag{10.4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt is also a well-posed local PDE. For a \\\\(C^1\\\\) initial profile \\\\(u_0\\\\) on\\na half-line, the unique \\\\(C^1\\\\) solution of \\\\(u_t=u_s\\\\) is\\n\\\\(u(t,s)=u_0(t+s)\\\\). Indeed the chain rule verifies the formula; conversely,\\nalong each characteristic \\\\(\\\\tau\\\\mapsto(t-\\\\tau,s+\\\\tau)\\\\), the derivative of\\n\\\\(u\\\\) is \\\\(-u_t+u_s=0\\\\), proving uniqueness.\\n\\nThe spatial domain, one-field count, differential order, and degree of the\\nPDE are all independent of \\\\(n\\\\). A common local spacetime interval can also\\nbe chosen on a common operator-norm neighborhood. In cyclic form (2.2) is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal C'=(\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T})^3.\\n\\\\tag{10.5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOn \\\\(\\\\|\\\\mathcal C\\\\|,\\\\|\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathcal C}\\\\|\\\\le2\\\\varepsilon\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T3}\\\\|\\\\le8\\\\varepsilon^3,qquad\\n\\\\|\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T3}-\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathcal C}^{\\\\mathsf T3}\\\\|\\n\\\\le12\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\|\\\\mathcal C-\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathcal C}\\\\|.\\n\\\\tag{10.6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese estimates make the integral map\\n\\\\(\\\\mathcal C(t)=\\\\mathcal C(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\n(\\\\mathcal C(u)^{\\\\mathsf T})^3du\\\\) invariant and contractive on a ball for,\\nfor example, \\\\(|t|\\\\le(24\\\\varepsilon^2)^{-1}\\\\), uniformly in dimension. Thus\\n(10.1)--(10.3) are not merely formal.\\n\\nFinally,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\partial_s^kE_n(\\\\theta)(0)=\\\\mathscr D_n^kf_n(\\\\theta).\\n\\\\tag{10.7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBy (6.6), the \\\\((2m-1)\\\\)-st spatial jet contains \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\). The one field\\nhas hidden the entire unbounded graph alphabet in its infinite Taylor jet;\\nit therefore violates exactly (3.2).\\n\\nThe same transport construction applies to physical gradient flow and to\\nthe operator IDE width limit: replace \\\\(\\\\Phi_n\\\\) by the relevant autonomous\\nflow. Hence autonomy, restartability, locality, finite PDE order, and a fixed\\nfield count do not by themselves imply finite information.\\n\\n## 11. A quantitative necessity theorem\\n\\nThe proof gives more than a dichotomy. Suppose every individual spatial jet\\nis a finite contraction polynomial, but there is no common degree bound, and\\nsuppose an order-\\\\(r\\\\) PDE with a readout of order \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\) is exact. Formula\\n(7.4) shows that \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n^k f_n\\\\) uses jets only through some finite\\norder no larger than \\\\(\\\\rho+kr\\\\).\\n\\nAt \\\\(k=2m-1\\\\), the connected graph \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\) cannot be created as a\\nproduct of smaller connected components. Stable independence therefore\\nforces at least one participating spatial jet of order at most\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\rho+(2m-1)r\\n\\\\tag{11.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nto contain \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\) already. Its contraction degree is at least\\n\\\\(4m+2\\\\). Thus every exact PDE must pay for the ladder through unbounded\\ncontraction complexity in its spatial jet.\\n\\nFor \\\\(r=0\\\\), (11.1) says no new spatial jet is ever accessible and recovers\\nthe finite-scalar ODE no-go.\\n\\n## 12. Final logical boundary\\n\\nThe rigorous resolution is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\\\text{no fixed finite-jet local PDE over a finite bounded-degree\\ncontraction alphabet}}\\n\\\\tag{12.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nbut\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\\\text{one fixed scalar transport PDE suffices if arbitrary spatial\\nprofiles are allowed}.}\\n\\\\tag{12.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAccordingly, the sentence “the three-hidden-layer linear model has no PDE\\nwith \\\\(O(1)\\\\) scalar fields” is false. The correct negative theorem is\\n(12.1). It is sharp: the spatial jet, not the number of displayed fields, is\\nwhere the missing infinitely many ordered-word coordinates can reside.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1981\",\"summary\":[\"**Identifying cyclic notation error for fix**\",\"**Verifying jet order growth and analytic identities**\",\"**Analyzing heat equation counterexample growth**\",\"**Analyzing heat equation counterexample growth**\",\"**Verifying Laplacian identity and analytic convergence**\",\"**Fixing notation and clarifying polynomial identity**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-6d139ba2-578e-4291-bb9d-74d6f3db0402\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -322,2 +322,9 @@\\n \\n+If (3.3)--(3.4) were initially assumed only on a nonempty open set of network\\n+states, both sides after substitution would be polynomials in the tensor\\n+entries. A real polynomial that vanishes on an open set has every coefficient\\n+zero (restrict successively to one-dimensional coordinate lines). Hence the\\n+identities are polynomial identities, and the same coefficient comparison is\\n+valid.\\n+\\n Notice that iteration may increase the spatial derivative order without\\n@@ -436,4 +443,4 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-\\\\|\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T3}\\\\|\\\\le8\\\\varepsilon^3,qquad\\n-\\\\|\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T3}-\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathcal C}^{\\\\mathsf T3}\\\\|\\n+\\\\|(\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T})^3\\\\|\\\\le8\\\\varepsilon^3,\\\\qquad\\n+\\\\|(\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T})^3-(\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathcal C}^{\\\\mathsf T})^3\\\\|\\n \\\\le12\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\|\\\\mathcal C-\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathcal C}\\\\|.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1982\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning heat equation subsection**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_xaYbWpEW3cgkY39PqdjpPPtr\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-7a20-70e1-8014-49e68e54da29\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_encoding_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1983\",\"summary\":[\"**Identifying backslash loss in patches**\",\"**Planning String.raw usage for patches**\",\"**Identifying backslash loss in patches**\",\"**Planning String.raw usage for patches**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1984\",\"text\":\"My first formatting pass exposed a purely mechanical problem in the draft: several LaTeX spacing commands lost their backslashes during file creation, and two lines were duplicated. I’m repairing the source before any audit begins; no mathematical claim is being changed.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-1a049ddb-3ad1-4af0-ade7-ad78b5a00432\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -26,3 +26,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-a,x\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n,qquad B,R\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n},qquad\\n+a,x\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n,\\\\qquad B,R\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n},\\\\qquad\\n f_n=a^{\\\\mathsf T}RBx.\\n@@ -73,3 +73,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-U_n=(U_{1,n},\\\\ldots,U_{q,n}),qquad\\n+U_n=(U_{1,n},\\\\ldots,U_{q,n}),\\\\qquad\\n U_{\\\\alpha,n}:\\\\Omega\\\\times\\\\Theta_n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\mathbb R,\\n@@ -201,3 +201,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-A=RB,qquad C=A^{\\\\mathsf T}A=B^{\\\\mathsf T}R^{\\\\mathsf T}RB.\\n+A=RB,\\\\qquad C=A^{\\\\mathsf T}A=B^{\\\\mathsf T}R^{\\\\mathsf T}RB.\\n \\\\tag{6.1}\\n@@ -208,4 +208,4 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}a=Ax,qquad\\n-\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}x=A^{\\\\mathsf T}a,qquad\\n+\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}a=Ax,\\\\qquad\\n+\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}x=A^{\\\\mathsf T}a,\\\\qquad\\n \\\\mathscr D_{0,n}A=0.\\n@@ -217,4 +217,4 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-U_j=a^{\\\\mathsf T}AC^jx,quad\\n-X_j=x^{\\\\mathsf T}C^{j+1}x,quad\\n+U_j=a^{\\\\mathsf T}AC^jx,\\\\quad\\n+X_j=x^{\\\\mathsf T}C^{j+1}x,\\\\quad\\n Y_j=a^{\\\\mathsf T}(AA^{\\\\mathsf T})^{j+1}a.\\n@@ -228,3 +228,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}U_j=X_j+Y_j,qquad\\n+\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}U_j=X_j+Y_j,\\\\qquad\\n \\\\mathscr D_{0,n}X_j=\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}Y_j=2U_{j+1}.\\n@@ -236,3 +236,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}^{,2m-1}f_n\\n+\\\\mathscr D_{0,n}^{\\\\,2m-1}f_n\\n =4^{m-1}\\\\left[\\n@@ -249,3 +249,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-\\\\mathscr D_n^{,2m-1}f_n\\n+\\\\mathscr D_n^{\\\\,2m-1}f_n\\n \\\\quad\\\\text{contains with positive coefficient}\\\\quad\\n@@ -378,4 +378,4 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-\\\\mathscr X_n^kf_n=(-2\\\\eta)^kQ_k,qquad\\n-Q_0=f_n,qquad Q_{k+1}=f_n\\\\mathscr D_nQ_k.\\n+\\\\mathscr X_n^kf_n=(-2\\\\eta)^kQ_k,\\\\qquad\\n+Q_0=f_n,\\\\qquad Q_{k+1}=f_n\\\\mathscr D_nQ_k.\\n \\\\tag{9.3}\\n@@ -413,3 +413,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-\\\\partial_tU_n=\\\\partial_sU_n,qquad\\n+\\\\partial_tU_n=\\\\partial_sU_n,\\\\qquad\\n U_n(t,0)=f_n(\\\\Phi_n^t\\\\theta_0).\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0497c-7a5b-7140-8bba-4134a9ebe89d\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-7a20-70e1-8014-49e68e54da29\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_encoding_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1985\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying polynomial positivity in induction**\",\"**Analyzing PDE operator norms and local well-posedness**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_6OJBkK4jFZUv8zeLXVsE71wL\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-6543-7790-b508-1baed56f9705\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_no_go_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_T8Vta917bVYMqtSojlJ7FDkk\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-8e2a-7722-8559-0006b47448aa\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_scope_adversary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_lLP2SYAdAABd8ucaytwJFViT\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-7a20-70e1-8014-49e68e54da29\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_encoding_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1986\",\"summary\":[\"**Auditing mathematical assumptions on coefficients and identities**\",\"**Verifying analytic PDE coefficient dependencies and local polynomial structure**\",\"**Auditing mathematical assumptions on coefficients and identities**\",\"**Verifying analytic PDE coefficient dependencies and local polynomial structure**\",\"**Auditing mathematical assumptions on coefficients and identities**\",\"**Verifying analytic PDE coefficient dependencies and local polynomial structure**\",\"**Confirming graph independence and PDE uniqueness with local flows**\",\"**Auditing mathematical assumptions on coefficients and identities**\",\"**Verifying analytic PDE coefficient dependencies and local polynomial structure**\",\"**Confirming graph independence and PDE uniqueness with local flows**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1987\",\"text\":\"The draft theorem is now under three blind attacks. One is checking the differential-algebra prolongation and graph independence; one is trying to break the transport counterexample’s autonomy and uniformity; the third is rederiving the analytic and physical-loss variants. No research theorem is used—the only analytic input for the counterexample is proved by an explicit contraction estimate.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0498a-4307-73b1-8cfe-f94a194e0e7c\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-7a20-70e1-8014-49e68e54da29\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_encoding_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1988\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning robust counterexample search**\",\"**Evaluating local-germ PDE counterexample options**\",\"**Planning robust counterexample search**\",\"**Evaluating local-germ PDE counterexample options**\",\"**Designing fixed-domain transport counterexample**\",\"**Planning robust counterexample search**\",\"**Evaluating local-germ PDE counterexample options**\",\"**Designing fixed-domain transport counterexample**\",\"**Planning robust counterexample search**\",\"**Evaluating local-germ PDE counterexample options**\",\"**Designing fixed-domain transport counterexample**\",\"**Refining PDE domain and readout boundary setup**\",\"**Planning robust counterexample search**\",\"**Evaluating local-germ PDE counterexample options**\",\"**Designing fixed-domain transport counterexample**\",\"**Refining PDE domain and readout boundary setup**\",\"**Establishing local-germ PDE counterexample with global physical flow**\",\"**Planning robust counterexample search**\",\"**Evaluating local-germ PDE counterexample options**\",\"**Designing fixed-domain transport counterexample**\",\"**Refining PDE domain and readout boundary setup**\",\"**Establishing local-germ PDE counterexample with global physical flow**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1989\",\"text\":\"The first adversarial round found a real quantifier defect: the physical-flow statement must assume \\\\(\\\\eta>0\\\\). It also correctly rejected my claim that a locally defined feature trajectory automatically gives a fixed-half-line transport semigroup. I’m repairing this in two parts: a local analytic-germ counterexample for feature time, and a genuinely global fixed-domain transport semigroup for physical gradient flow, after proving the latter’s finite-width global existence by the loss/trace-norm energy estimate.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-b597efa8-6f5e-490c-a47e-250b7582378e\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -13,4 +13,5 @@\\n    fields can represent the dynamics is **false**. One scalar field governed\\n-   by the linear transport equation stores the entire ordered-word hierarchy\\n-   in its spatial profile. Field count is not numerical state dimension.\\n+   by the linear transport equation stores the unbounded output\\n+   Lie-derivative hierarchy in its spatial profile. Field count is not\\n+   numerical state dimension.\\n \\n@@ -43,4 +44,4 @@\\n Write \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n\\\\) for the derivation on polynomials in the entries of\\n-\\\\((a,R,B,x)\\\\) induced by (2.2). For squared loss \\\\((y-f_n)^2\\\\), physical\\n-gradient flow is generated by\\n+\\\\((a,R,B,x)\\\\) induced by (2.2). For squared loss \\\\((y-f_n)^2\\\\), fix\\n+\\\\(\\\\eta>0\\\\). Physical gradient flow is generated by\\n \\n@@ -59,3 +60,4 @@\\n \\n-- an open spatial domain \\\\(\\\\Omega\\\\subset\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\);\\n+- a spatial domain \\\\(\\\\Omega\\\\subset\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), possibly with a smooth\\n+  boundary, with coefficient functions smooth up to that boundary;\\n - a number \\\\(q\\\\) of scalar fields;\\n@@ -140,6 +142,7 @@\\n 3. The same two conclusions hold for the physical derivation (2.3), for\\n-   every label \\\\(y\\\\in\\\\mathbb R\\\\).\\n+   every label \\\\(y\\\\in\\\\mathbb R\\\\) and every fixed \\\\(\\\\eta>0\\\\).\\n \\n 4. If condition (3.2) is deleted, the corresponding universal no-PDE claim\\n-   is false. There is a state-universal, autonomous, restartable realization\\n+   is false. Feature time has a state-universal local-germ realization, and\\n+   physical time has a global forward, autonomous, restartable realization,\\n    using one scalar field, one spatial variable, and the fixed degree-one,\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-78f17d4a-2f74-4db2-90a8-5b1e6a52c938\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -394,7 +394,8 @@\\n \\n-## 10. The counterexample to a broad PDE no-go\\n+## 10. Counterexamples to a broad PDE no-go\\n+\\n+### 10.1 Feature time: an exact local-germ PDE\\n \\n-The failure without (3.2) is elementary and exact. Let \\\\(\\\\Phi_n^s\\\\) be the\\n-local feature flow generated by (2.2). For a current state \\\\(\\\\theta\\\\), define\\n-one scalar spatial profile\\n+Let \\\\(\\\\Phi_n^s\\\\) be the local feature flow generated by (2.2). For a current\\n+state \\\\(\\\\theta\\\\), define the germ at \\\\(s=0\\\\) of one scalar profile\\n \\n@@ -413,3 +414,4 @@\\n \\n-The flow property gives, wherever \\\\(t,s,t+s\\\\) lie in the existence interval,\\n+Wherever \\\\(t,s,t+s\\\\) lie in the local existence interval, the flow property\\n+gives\\n \\n@@ -417,3 +419,3 @@\\n \\\\partial_tU_n=\\\\partial_sU_n,\\\\qquad\\n-U_n(t,0)=f_n(\\\\Phi_n^t\\\\theta_0).\\n+U_n(t,0)=f_n(\\\\Phi_n^t\\\\theta_0),\\n \\\\tag{10.3}\\n@@ -421,3 +423,3 @@\\n \\n-This is autonomous and restartable:\\n+and\\n \\n@@ -428,11 +430,8 @@\\n \\n-It is also a well-posed local PDE. For a \\\\(C^1\\\\) initial profile \\\\(u_0\\\\) on\\n-a half-line, the unique \\\\(C^1\\\\) solution of \\\\(u_t=u_s\\\\) is\\n-\\\\(u(t,s)=u_0(t+s)\\\\). Indeed the chain rule verifies the formula; conversely,\\n-along each characteristic \\\\(\\\\tau\\\\mapsto(t-\\\\tau,s+\\\\tau)\\\\), the derivative of\\n-\\\\(u\\\\) is \\\\(-u_t+u_s=0\\\\), proving uniqueness.\\n+Thus translation of germs is autonomous and restartable for every\\n+sufficiently small time for which the translated germ is defined.\\n \\n-The spatial domain, one-field count, differential order, and degree of the\\n-PDE are all independent of \\\\(n\\\\). A common local spacetime interval can also\\n-be chosen on a common operator-norm neighborhood. In cyclic form (2.2) is\\n+The one-field count, differential order, and degree are independent of\\n+\\\\(n\\\\). A common germ radius exists on a common operator-norm neighborhood.\\n+In cyclic form (2.2) is\\n \\n@@ -452,9 +451,10 @@\\n \\n-These estimates make the integral map\\n-\\\\(\\\\mathcal C(t)=\\\\mathcal C(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\n-(\\\\mathcal C(u)^{\\\\mathsf T})^3du\\\\) invariant and contractive on a ball for,\\n-for example, \\\\(|t|\\\\le(24\\\\varepsilon^2)^{-1}\\\\), uniformly in dimension. Thus\\n-(10.1)--(10.3) are not merely formal.\\n+For initial norm at most \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\) and\\n+\\\\(|t|\\\\le(24\\\\varepsilon^2)^{-1}\\\\), the Picard integral map stays inside the\\n+\\\\(2\\\\varepsilon\\\\)-ball because\\n+\\\\(\\\\varepsilon+8\\\\varepsilon^3|t|\\\\le4\\\\varepsilon/3\\\\), and its contraction\\n+constant is at most \\\\(12\\\\varepsilon^2|t|\\\\le1/2\\\\). This proves the asserted\\n+uniform local flow directly.\\n \\n-Finally,\\n+Moreover,\\n \\n@@ -466,9 +466,78 @@\\n By (6.6), the \\\\((2m-1)\\\\)-st spatial jet contains \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\). The one field\\n-has hidden the entire unbounded graph alphabet in its infinite Taylor jet;\\n-it therefore violates exactly (3.2).\\n+has hidden an unbounded graph alphabet in its infinite Taylor jet and\\n+therefore violates exactly (3.2). This is a local-germ representation, not a\\n+claim that feature flow is complete.\\n \\n-The same transport construction applies to physical gradient flow and to\\n-the operator IDE width limit: replace \\\\(\\\\Phi_n\\\\) by the relevant autonomous\\n-flow. Hence autonomy, restartability, locality, finite PDE order, and a fixed\\n-field count do not by themselves imply finite information.\\n+### 10.2 Physical time: a global fixed-domain PDE\\n+\\n+For physical time the counterexample is a genuine forward semigroup on a\\n+fixed field space, because the finite-width physical flow is global. With\\n+\\\\(\\\\mathcal C\\\\) as in (10.5), put\\n+\\n+\\\\[\\n+K=\\\\|(\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T})^3\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{HS}}^2,\\\\qquad\\n+e=y-\\\\tfrac14\\\\operatorname{Tr}\\\\mathcal C^4.\\n+\\\\tag{10.8}\\n+\\\\]\\n+\\n+Direct differentiation of the four parameter blocks gives\\n+\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\dot e=-2\\\\eta eK,\\\\qquad\\n+\\\\frac d{dt}e^2=-4\\\\eta e^2K.\\n+\\\\tag{10.9}\\n+\\\\]\\n+\\n+Every length-three path around the four-cycle contains an endpoint rank-one\\n+block, so \\\\(\\\\operatorname{rank}\\\\mathcal C^3\\\\le4\\\\). Consequently\\n+\\\\(\\\\|(\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T})^3\\\\|_1\\\\le2\\\\sqrt K\\\\). For every finite \\\\(T\\\\),\\n+\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\begin{aligned}\\n+\\\\|\\\\mathcal C(T)-\\\\mathcal C(0)\\\\|_1\\n+&\\\\le4\\\\eta\\\\int_0^T|e|\\\\sqrt K\\\\,dt\\\\\\\\\\n+&\\\\le4\\\\eta\\\\sqrt T\\n+\\\\left(\\\\int_0^Te^2K\\\\,dt\\\\right)^{1/2}\\\\\\\\\\n+&\\\\le2|e(0)|\\\\sqrt{\\\\eta T}.\\n+\\\\end{aligned}\\n+\\\\tag{10.10}\\n+\\\\]\\n+\\n+The last inequality uses (10.9). Thus the finite-dimensional state remains\\n+bounded on every finite interval. The locally Lipschitz polynomial ODE\\n+therefore extends for all \\\\(t\\\\ge0\\\\): if a maximal finite endpoint existed,\\n+the state would remain in a compact ball on which the vector field is\\n+bounded and Lipschitz, so one more local Picard step would extend it.\\n+\\n+Let \\\\(\\\\Psi_n^t\\\\) denote this global physical flow and define, on the fixed\\n+half-line,\\n+\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\widehat E_n(\\\\theta)(s)=f_n(\\\\Psi_n^s\\\\theta),\\n+\\\\qquad s\\\\in[0,\\\\infty).\\n+\\\\tag{10.11}\\n+\\\\]\\n+\\n+On \\\\(C([0,\\\\infty))\\\\) with the compact-open topology, right translation\\n+\\\\(S_tu(s)=u(s+t)\\\\) is a continuous semigroup: on every compact \\\\(s\\\\)-interval,\\n+uniform continuity on the slightly larger compact interval gives continuity\\n+in \\\\(t\\\\). Along the physical flow,\\n+\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\widehat E_n(\\\\Psi_n^t\\\\theta)=S_t\\\\widehat E_n(\\\\theta),\\\\qquad\\n+\\\\partial_tU=\\\\partial_sU,\\\\qquad\\n+U(t,0)=f_n(\\\\Psi_n^t\\\\theta).\\n+\\\\tag{10.12}\\n+\\\\]\\n+\\n+For \\\\(C^1\\\\) profiles this PDE is uniquely solved by\\n+\\\\(U(t,s)=U(0,t+s)\\\\). Indeed, along\\n+\\\\(\\\\tau\\\\mapsto(t-\\\\tau,s+\\\\tau)\\\\), the derivative is\\n+\\\\(-U_t+U_s=0\\\\). Hence (10.12) is an autonomous, restartable, globally\\n+well-posed forward PDE on a fixed domain.\\n+\\n+Together, the local feature-germ construction and the global physical\\n+construction show that locality, finite differential order, and fixed field\\n+count do not by themselves imply finite information. Neither construction\\n+uses the width-limit IDE or an external theorem.\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0498a-30bf-7b80-9031-9a91b27c637a\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-8e2a-7722-8559-0006b47448aa\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_scope_adversary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1990\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying boundedness and well-posedness of PDE**\",\"**Defining missing C block and refining output semantics**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0498a-20e1-7363-84eb-47ed706ec7e2\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-6543-7790-b508-1baed56f9705\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_no_go_route\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-ac584900-a997-447e-b509-555b58e0d737\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -145,6 +145,7 @@\\n 4. If condition (3.2) is deleted, the corresponding universal no-PDE claim\\n-   is fa