\(G_0\\\\) with nonzero coefficient and a maximal number of typed\\n-index vertices. At widths at least those vertex counts, label all vertices of\\n-\\\\(G_0\\\\) injectively. The resulting tensor-entry monomial records every tensor\\n-type and the two incident typed indices of every tensor occurrence. Hence the\\n-same monomial can occur in \\\\(I_H\\\\) only if \\\\(H\\\\) has the same vertices and\\n-incidences, namely only if \\\\(H\\\\) is type-preservingly isomorphic to \\\\(G_0\\\\).\\n-Its coefficient cannot cancel. This contradiction proves independence of the\\n-\\\\(I_G\\\\), and the invertible triangular transformation proves independence of\\n-the \\\\(P_G\\\\). \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n+Injective contractions of pairwise nonisomorphic auxiliary networks are\\n+independent in sufficiently large width. Indeed, label every index vertex of\\n+one network injectively. The resulting tensor-entry monomial records each\\n+tensor type and all incident typed indices, so the same monomial can occur\\n+for another injective network only if the two incidence networks are\\n+type-preservingly isomorphic.\\n+\\n+Now suppose \\\\(\\\\sum_G c_GP_G^{(n)}=0\\\\) for the original complete-contraction\\n+graphs. Choose among the graphs with \\\\(c_G\\\\ne0\\\\) one having a maximal number\\n+of index vertices. In the expansion (5.1), the discrete partition contributes\\n+\\\\(I_G\\\\). A nontrivial quotient has fewer index vertices, and no graph with\\n+nonzero coefficient has more vertices than \\\\(G\\\\). Hence no other term can\\n+cancel the coefficient \\\\(c_G\\\\) of \\\\(I_G\\\\). Therefore \\\\(c_G=0\\\\), a\\n+contradiction. Descending in the number of vertices proves that every\\n+coefficient vanishes. \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-fc9181b6-a1c7-44ff-922a-9c420529b5e7\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -296,15 +296,31 @@\\n For completeness, introduce formal jet variables \\\\(u_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta}\\\\). The\\n-evolutionary prolongation of (3.3) acts on a differential polynomial \\\\(H\\\\) by\\n+total spatial derivatives on the jet algebra are\\n \\n \\\\[\\n+D_i^{\\\\mathrm{tot}}\\n+=\\\\frac{\\\\partial}{\\\\partial\\\\zeta_i}\\n++\\\\sum_{\\\\gamma,\\\\delta}\\n+u_{\\\\gamma,\\\\delta+e_i}\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\partial}{\\\\partial u_{\\\\gamma,\\\\delta}},\\n+\\\\qquad\\n+D_{\\\\mathrm{tot}}^\\\\beta\\n+=\\\\prod_{i=1}^d(D_i^{\\\\mathrm{tot}})^{\\\\beta_i}.\\n+\\\\tag{7.2}\\n+\\\\]\\n+\\n+For the finitely many readout points, use independent jet copies\\n+\\\\(u_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta}^{(j)}\\\\). The evolutionary prolongation of (3.3) is\\n+\\n+\\\\[\\n \\\\mathbf T_FH\\n-=\\\\sum_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta}\\n-\\\\frac{\\\\partial H}{\\\\partial u_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta}}\\n-\\\\,\\\\partial_\\\\zeta^\\\\beta F_\\\\alpha.\\n-\\\\tag{7.2}\\n+=\\\\sum_{j,\\\\alpha,\\\\beta}\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\partial H}{\\\\partial u_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta}^{(j)}}\\n+\\\\left(D_{\\\\mathrm{tot}}^\\\\beta F_\\\\alpha\\\\right)\\n+(\\\\zeta_j,u^{(j)}).\\n+\\\\tag{7.3}\\n \\\\]\\n \\n-Only finitely many terms occur for any fixed \\\\(H\\\\). Since the state\\n-derivation and spatial differentiation act on different variables, they\\n-commute. The ordinary chain and product rules therefore give\\n+Only finitely many terms occur for any fixed differential polynomial \\\\(H\\\\).\\n+The state derivation and spatial differentiation act on different variables\\n+and therefore commute. The chain and product rules give\\n \\n@@ -313,3 +329,3 @@\\n =(\\\\mathbf T_FH)(j^\\\\infty U_n).\\n-\\\\tag{7.3}\\n+\\\\tag{7.4}\\n \\\\]\\n@@ -321,3 +337,3 @@\\n =(\\\\mathbf T_F^kG)(j^\\\\infty U_n).\\n-\\\\tag{7.4}\\n+\\\\tag{7.5}\\n \\\\]\\n@@ -328,3 +344,3 @@\\n \\n-Choose \\\\(m\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\notin\\\\mathcal S\\\\). Equation (7.4) says that\\n+Choose \\\\(m\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\notin\\\\mathcal S\\\\). Equation (7.5) says that\\n \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n^{2m-1}f_n\\\\) is a linear combination of graphs whose connected\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-402e4e10-c2be-429c-8612-b4386e6243bf\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -379,3 +379,3 @@\\n \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n^{2m-1}f_n\\\\) is homogeneous of degree \\\\(4m+2\\\\). Comparing the\\n-coefficient of \\\\(\\\\lambda^{4m+2}\\\\) in the analytic version of (7.4) again\\n+coefficient of \\\\(\\\\lambda^{4m+2}\\\\) in the analytic version of (7.5) again\\n places it in \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{\\\\mathcal S}^{(n)}\\\\), contradicting (6.6). This\\n@@ -587,3 +587,3 @@\\n suppose an order-\\\\(r\\\\) PDE with a readout of order \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\) is exact. Formula\\n-(7.4) shows that \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n^k f_n\\\\) uses jets only through some finite\\n+(7.5) shows that \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n^k f_n\\\\) uses jets only through some finite\\n order no larger than \\\\(\\\\rho+kr\\\\).\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1992\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning analytic theorem patch placement**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-2132606f-1860-4668-84f8-640845ed58b5\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -123,2 +123,9 @@\\n \\n+In the analytic variant, \\\\(F_\\\\alpha\\\\) and \\\\(G\\\\) are fixed functions analytic\\n+in their finite jet arguments on open sets containing every realized\\n+zero-network jet tuple to which they are applied. The closure identities are\\n+required on a network-state neighborhood on which those compositions remain\\n+inside the analytic domains. This specifies the jet-space basepoints; they\\n+need not be the origin because (3.2) may contain an empty-graph term.\\n+\\n The phrase “bounded-degree PDE” is otherwise ambiguous. In (3.2) the bound\\n@@ -364,13 +371,20 @@\\n \\n-Suppose instead that the PDE and readout are analytic germs near the zero\\n-network state. Scale all four tensors by \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\). Every field jet is then\\n-a polynomial in \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\) of degree at most \\\\(D\\\\). Subtract its value at\\n-\\\\(\\\\lambda=0\\\\); every remaining nonzero jet has strictly positive\\n+Suppose instead that the PDE and readout satisfy the analytic-domain\\n+condition stated after (3.4). Scale all four tensors by \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\). Every\\n+field jet is then a polynomial in \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\) of degree at most \\\\(D\\\\).\\n+For every jet variable, recenter at its complete zero-state value,\\n+\\\\[\\n+v_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta,n}(\\\\zeta;\\\\lambda)\\n+=\\\\partial^\\\\beta U_{\\\\alpha,n}(\\\\zeta;\\\\lambda\\\\theta)\\n+-\\\\partial^\\\\beta U_{\\\\alpha,n}(\\\\zeta;0).\\n+\\\\tag{8.1}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Every nonzero \\\\(v_{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta,n}\\\\) has strictly positive\\n \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\)-order.\\n \\n-At any prescribed power \\\\(\\\\lambda^N\\\\), only finitely many monomials in the\\n-convergent Taylor expansions of the analytic PDE and readout can contribute:\\n-each nonconstant factor has order at least one. Each contribution is a\\n-product of field-jet coefficients and hence has connected components only\\n-from \\\\(\\\\mathcal S\\\\).\\n+Taylor-expand each analytic function at the corresponding zero-state jet\\n+tuple. At any prescribed power \\\\(\\\\lambda^N\\\\), only finitely many Taylor\\n+monomials can contribute because each nonconstant centered factor has order\\n+at least one. Each contribution is a product of field-jet coefficients and\\n+hence has connected components only from \\\\(\\\\mathcal S\\\\).\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-e0fa109a-15d2-4909-b75b-3a51cae154b0\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -598,11 +598,16 @@\\n \\n-The proof gives more than a dichotomy. Suppose every individual spatial jet\\n-is a finite contraction polynomial, but there is no common degree bound, and\\n-suppose an order-\\\\(r\\\\) PDE with a readout of order \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\) is exact. Formula\\n-(7.5) shows that \\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n^k f_n\\\\) uses jets only through some finite\\n-order no larger than \\\\(\\\\rho+kr\\\\).\\n+The proof gives more than a dichotomy. Suppose an order-\\\\(r\\\\) PDE with a\\n+readout of order \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\) is exact and assume this width-uniform local\\n+finiteness condition: for every fixed jet cutoff \\\\(J\\\\), the union over all\\n+widths, fields, and \\\\(|\\\\beta|\\\\le J\\\\) of the graph supports of\\n+\\\\(\\\\partial^\\\\beta U_{\\\\alpha,n}\\\\) is finite. This permits the support and its\\n+maximum degree to grow with \\\\(J\\\\), but prevents a fixed low-order jet from\\n+hiding width-dependent arbitrarily long words. Formula (7.5) shows that\\n+\\\\(\\\\mathscr D_n^k f_n\\\\) uses jets only through order at most \\\\(\\\\rho+kr\\\\).\\n \\n-At \\\\(k=2m-1\\\\), the connected graph \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\) cannot be created as a\\n-product of smaller connected components. Stable independence therefore\\n-forces at least one participating spatial jet of order at most\\n+At \\\\(k=2m-1\\\\), the relevant finite jet cutoff has one graph alphabet\\n+independent of width, so Lemma 2 applies in its stable range. The connected\\n+graph \\\\(\\\\Gamma_m\\\\) cannot be created as a product of smaller connected\\n+components. Stable independence therefore forces at least one participating\\n+spatial jet of order at most\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1993\",\"summary\":[\"**Fixing sed range and notation errors**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-c9732dd1-355a-4d12-b83f-3bcdc4098942\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -339,3 +339,3 @@\\n \\n-Applying (7.3) repeatedly to the readout polynomial gives\\n+Applying (7.4) repeatedly to the readout polynomial gives\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1994\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying analytic composition properties**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-3e437271-e2e8-4e62-8cfb-edbe388500a0\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_deep_linear_pde_no_go/PDE_CLOSURE_BOUNDARY.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -527,3 +527,10 @@\\n fixed field space, because the finite-width physical flow is global. With\\n-\\\\(\\\\mathcal C\\\\) as above, put\\n+\\\\(\\\\mathcal C\\\\) as above, the four diagonal blocks of \\\\(\\\\mathcal C^4\\\\) all\\n+have trace \\\\(f_n\\\\), and (2.3) gives\\n+\\\\[\\n+f_n=\\\\tfrac14\\\\operatorname{Tr}\\\\mathcal C^4,\\\\qquad\\n+\\\\dot{\\\\mathcal C}=2\\\\eta e(\\\\mathcal C^{\\\\mathsf T})^3.\\n+\\\\tag{10.7a}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Put\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1995\",\"text\":\"The repaired report now makes the boundary sharper:\\n\\n- \\\\(\\\\eta>0\\\\) is explicit.\\n- The graph-independence proof uses the necessary quotient-closed auxiliary incidence class.\\n- PDE prolongation now uses total derivatives and separate jet copies at each readout point.\\n- The analytic theorem has explicit jet-space basepoints.\\n- Feature time is claimed only as a local germ; physical time gets the stronger global fixed-half-line semigroup, proved from the exact energy estimate.\\n\\nI’m freezing this revision for the second blind round.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_76l0u1VFFQ4zS0LHyzpRXeXb\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-6543-7790-b508-1baed56f9705\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_no_go_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_LCykUaDr1rkvcZ59ZEQTyrxf\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-8e2a-7722-8559-0006b47448aa\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_scope_adversary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_NwjG63pgEhBfjNHJ8JzMjib3\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-7a20-70e1-8014-49e68e54da29\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_encoding_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1996\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing C³ rank and trace properties**\",\"**Bounding energy integral and verifying analytic PDE identities**\",\"**Analyzing C³ rank and trace properties**\",\"**Bounding energy integral and verifying analytic PDE identities**\",\"**Analyzing C³ rank and trace properties**\",\"**Bounding energy integral and verifying analytic PDE identities**\",\"**Verifying graph-based Taylor expansions and coefficient independence**\",\"**Analyzing C³ rank and trace properties**\",\"**Bounding energy integral and verifying analytic PDE identities**\",\"**Verifying graph-based Taylor expansions and coefficient independence**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04990-df44-7fa3-8f68-9d4095a423e8\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-7a20-70e1-8014-49e68e54da29\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_encoding_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04990-ce7e-7680-ba6c-6e558b09736e\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0497c-8e2a-7722-8559-0006b47448aa\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/pde_scope_adversary\"},{