shing pathwise convergence and constructing initial action law**\",\"**Constructing functional spaces and proving solution uniqueness**\",\"**Analyzing initial operator dynamics and kernel expansions**\",\"**Confirming global loss descent and joint convergence for finite times**\",\"**Clarifying operator norm convergence and action state equivalence**\",\"**Deriving full L2 proof with notation**\",\"**Formulating continuous gradient flow equations and bounds**\",\"**Establishing Lipschitz stability and Euler error bounds**\",\"**Establishing pathwise convergence and constructing initial action law**\",\"**Constructing functional spaces and proving solution uniqueness**\",\"**Analyzing initial operator dynamics and kernel expansions**\",\"**Confirming global loss descent and joint convergence for finite times**\",\"**Clarifying operator norm convergence and action state equivalence**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1897\",\"text\":\"The key structural difference from the failed \\\\(L=3\\\\) argument is now clear: for \\\\(L=2\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\), the coordinate \\\\(X=A+A^3/3\\\\) exactly cancels the sole unbounded first-layer derivative factor. The remaining vector field is dimension-uniformly Lipschitz in normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\) plus the middle-operator norm on bounded trajectories. I’m checking whether that observation really closes every width/mesh/GD and action-law bridge—not assuming that it does.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1898\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying Lipschitz properties and boundedness**\",\"**Analyzing polynomial bounds and convergence conditions**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz properties and boundedness**\",\"**Analyzing polynomial bounds and convergence conditions**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz properties and boundedness**\",\"**Analyzing polynomial bounds and convergence conditions**\",\"**Confirming empirical convergence without mean theorem**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz properties and boundedness**\",\"**Analyzing polynomial bounds and convergence conditions**\",\"**Confirming empirical convergence without mean theorem**\",\"**Establishing operator norm bounds with Gaussian concentration**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz properties and boundedness**\",\"**Analyzing polynomial bounds and convergence conditions**\",\"**Confirming empirical convergence without mean theorem**\",\"**Establishing operator norm bounds with Gaussian concentration**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz properties and boundedness**\",\"**Analyzing polynomial bounds and convergence conditions**\",\"**Confirming empirical convergence without mean theorem**\",\"**Establishing operator norm bounds with Gaussian concentration**\",\"**Deriving rigorous expansions and differentiability proofs**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz properties and boundedness**\",\"**Analyzing polynomial bounds and convergence conditions**\",\"**Confirming empirical convergence without mean theorem**\",\"**Establishing operator norm bounds with Gaussian concentration**\",\"**Deriving rigorous expansions and differentiability proofs**\",\"**Identifying proof gap in Appendix A.2**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a048ce-a78e-7391-8cb5-8bfae1349de7\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a048cd-51f2-7a81-9813-2e887e11b5ce\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l2_activity_algebra_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a048ce-852b-74d0-a9d5-e5c7fef52cc8\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a048cd-28a6-7ca0-9c31-79530cb9e7ba\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l2_tp_primary_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1899\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying Lipschitz conditions and probabilistic bounds**\",\"**Assessing operator adjoint and coupling consistency**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz conditions and probabilistic bounds**\",\"**Assessing operator adjoint and coupling consistency**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz conditions and probabilistic bounds**\",\"**Assessing operator adjoint and coupling consistency**\",\"**Analyzing velocity field continuity and convergence challenges**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz conditions and probabilistic bounds**\",\"**Assessing operator adjoint and coupling consistency**\",\"**Analyzing velocity field continuity and convergence challenges**\",\"**Establishing uniform integrability via double-limit truncation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_qKJOW21v3jyRlnC069Hlqq48\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a048cd-3d26-7002-8697-43cb31dd0a03\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l2_continuum_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1900\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing Euler local error bounds**\",\"**Analyzing Euler local error bounds**\",\"**Establishing Euler step convergence rates**\",\"**Analyzing Euler local error bounds**\",\"**Establishing Euler step convergence rates**\",\"**Refining convergence with time-integrated energies**\",\"**Analyzing Euler local error bounds**\",\"**Establishing Euler step convergence rates**\",\"**Refining convergence with time-integrated energies**\",\"**Establishing uniform tail integrability for convergence**\",\"**Analyzing Euler local error bounds**\",\"**Establishing Euler step convergence rates**\",\"**Refining convergence with time-integrated energies**\",\"**Establishing uniform tail integrability for convergence**\",\"**Verifying L2 boundedness and equicontinuity**\",\"**Planning detailed report structure**\",\"**Detailing external theorem and audit plan**\",\"**Planning detailed report structure**\",\"**Detailing external theorem and audit plan**\",\"**Planning detailed report structure**\",\"**Detailing external theorem and audit plan**\",\"**Establishing empirical-oracle coupling convergence**\",\"**Verifying discrete velocity approximations**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1901\",\"text\":\"The continuum audit found—and repaired—one subtle point the earlier proof had glossed over: \\\\(q(A)P\\\\) is not naively Lipschitz in \\\\(L^2\\\\). The repair uses uniform \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence of the joint law of \\\\((A,P)\\\\), whose second coordinate supplies exactly the required square-tail control. This avoids both fourth-moment assumptions and any growing-length tensor-program theorem. I’m incorporating that correction into the standalone proof before blind review.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-98c9e04f-a6cb-4e5c-a435-7ed981f942b4\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# A rigorous joint mean-field/gradient-flow limit for the two-hidden-layer arctangent network\\n\\n## Theorem\\n\\nFor every width \\\\(n\\\\), consider the one-sample network\\n\\\\[\\nA_i=W_i^{(1)},\\\\qquad H_i=\\\\phi(A_i),\\\\qquad\\nS_j=\\\\sum_{i=1}^nB_{ji}H_i,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nG_j=\\\\phi(S_j),\\\\qquad f_n=\\\\sum_{j=1}^nc_jG_j,\\n\\\\qquad L_n=(f_n-1)^2,                              \\\\tag{T1}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\phi(x)=\\\\arctan x\\\\).  Initialize all variables independently by\\n\\\\[\\nA_i(0)\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\qquad B_{ji}(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-1}),\\n\\\\qquad c_j(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-4}).                     \\\\tag{T2}\\n\\\\]\\nUse exact full-batch GD with base step and raw layer multipliers\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n=n^{-2},\\\\qquad\\n(\\\\lambda_A,\\\\lambda_B,\\\\lambda_c)=(n,1,n^{-1}),       \\\\tag{T3}\\n\\\\]\\nand use polygonal interpolation on the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\).\\n\\nThen, along the full sequence \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), the exact GD processes converge in probability, uniformly on every compact time interval, to a unique autonomous and restartable action-law gradient flow.  The empirical path laws of \\\\(A\\\\) and \\\\(S\\\\) converge in \\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\).  Outputs, losses, all three scaled kernel blocks, and both hidden velocity energies converge in the modes stated below.\\n\\nThere exists \\\\(T_*>0\\\\) such that, for every \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_*\\\\):\\n\\n1. both hidden marginal laws have finite strictly positive variance;\\n2. both hidden layers have finite positive integrated mean-square velocity and nonzero mean-square displacement;\\n3. all three parameter blocks have finite positive integrated scaled kernel;\\n4. the affine-regression residual of \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) is positive in both hidden layers;\\n5. \\\\(K(t)\\\\) is nonconstant and \\\\(L(T)<L(0)=1\\\\).\\n\\nIn particular, this is a genuinely nonlinear feature-learning limit, not a frozen-feature or affine limit.  Moreover\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\lambda_A=n^{-1},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\lambda_B=n^{-2},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\lambda_c=n^{-3},                            \\\\tag{T4}\\n\\\\]\\nso every raw parameter step tends to zero.\\n\\nThe proof has five bridges: exact normalization; a dimension-free regularized flow; fixed-mesh width identification by a finite population-oracle tensor program; a same-width Euler sandwich yielding the continuous action law; and a direct comparison of exact GD with that flow.  The final section proves strict activity and nonlinearity.\\n\\n## 1. Exact finite-width equations\\n\\nPut\\n\\\\[\\nC=nc,qquad \\\\langle u,v\\\\rangle_n=n^{-1}u^\\\\top v,\\n\\\\qquad (u\\\\otimes_n v)z=u\\\\langle v,z\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\nand write\\n\\\\[\\nq(x)=\\\\phi'(x)=\\\\frac1{1+x^2},\\\\qquad\\nD=C\\\\odot q(S),\\\\qquad P=B^\\\\top D,qquad e=f_n-1.     \\\\tag{1.1}\\n\\\\]\\nThen \\\\(f_n=\\\\langle C,G\\\\rangle_n\\\\).  Direct differentiation gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial A_i}=\\\\frac1nq(A_i)P_i,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial B_{ji}}=\\\\frac1nD_jH_i,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial c_j}=G_j.             \\\\tag{1.2}\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplying the loss gradients by (T3) proves that the associated finite-width gradient flow is exactly\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot A=-2e\\\\,q(A)\\\\odot P,qquad\\n\\\\dot B=-2e\\\\,D\\\\otimes_nH,qquad\\n\\\\dot C=-2e\\\\,G.                                     \\\\tag{1.3}\\n\\\\]\\nThe three scaled tangent-kernel blocks are\\n\\\\[\\nK^A_n=\\\\|q(A)P\\\\|_n^2,qquad\\nK^B_n=\\\\|H\\\\|_n^2\\\\|D\\\\|_n^2,qquad\\nK^C_n=\\\\|G\\\\|_n^2,                                  \\\\tag{1.4}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_n^2=\\\\langle v,v\\\\rangle_n\\\\).  Thus, for \\\\(K_n=K_n^A+K_n^B+K_n^C\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f_n=-2eK_n,qquad \\\\dot L_n=-4e^2K_n.          \\\\tag{1.5}\\n\\\\]\\nIn particular, \\\\(|e(t)|\\\\le |e(0)|\\\\) along the flow.\\n\\nDifferentiating \\\\(S=BH\\\\) will also be useful:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot S=-2e\\\\left\\\\{\\\\|H\\\\|_n^2D+B[q(A)^2\\\\odot P]\\\\right\\\\}. \\\\tag{1.6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n## 2. The regularizing coordinate and dimension-free flow estimates\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\nF(a)=a+\\\\frac{a^3}{3},\\\\qquad X=F(A),\\\\qquad J=F^{-1},\\n\\\\qquad h(x)=\\\\arctan J(x).                           \\\\tag{2.1}\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(a)=q(a)^{-1}\\\\), equation (1.3) becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nA&=J(X),& H&=h(X),& S&=BH,\\\\\\\\\\nG&=\\\\arctan S,&D&=Cq(S),&e&=\\\\langle C,G\\\\rangle_n-1,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot X&=-2eB^\\\\top D,&\\n\\\\dot B&=-2eD\\\\otimes_nH,&\\n\\\\dot C&=-2eG.\\n\\\\end{aligned}}                                    \\\\tag{2.2}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is exact, not a linearization.  Also\\n\\\\[\\nJ'(x)=q(J(x)),\\\\qquad h'(x)=q(J(x))^2,               \\\\tag{2.3}\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(J,h,q\\\\circ J\\\\) are globally Lipschitz; \\\\(|H|,|G|\\\\le a_0:=\\\\pi/2\\\\).\\n\\nFor two states define\\n\\\\[\\nd_n(\\\\theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\theta)\\n=\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}+\\n \\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n.                            \\\\tag{2.4}\\n\\\\]\\nOn a set on which \\\\(\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}},\\\\|\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le M_B\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\|C\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le M_C\\\\), the vector field in (2.2) is Lipschitz in (2.4), with a constant independent of \\\\(n\\\\).  Indeed,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|H-\\\\widetilde H\\\\|_n\\\\le\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_n,     \\\\tag{2.5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le a_0\\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n  +M_B\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_n,                       \\\\tag{2.6}\\n\\\\]\\nand\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|D-\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le \\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n\\n +M_C\\\\operatorname{Lip}(q)\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_n.  \\\\tag{2.7}\\n\\\\]\\nFurthermore\\n\\\\[\\n|e-\\\\widetilde e|\\n\\\\le a_0\\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n\\n +\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_n,        \\\\tag{2.8}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|B^\\\\top D-\\\\widetilde B^\\\\top\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le \\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\|D\\\\|_n\\n +M_B\\\\|D-\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_n.                        \\\\tag{2.9}\\n\\\\]\\nFinally \\\\(\\\\|u\\\\otimes_nv\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}=\\\\|u\\\\|_n\\\\|v\\\\|_n\\\\).  Substitution of (2.5)--(2.9) into (2.2) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|V_n(\\\\theta)-V_n(\\\\widetilde\\\\theta)\\\\|_{d_n}\\n\\\\le L(M_B,M_C,\\\\|C\\\\|_n,\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n)d_n(\\\\theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\theta). \\\\tag{2.10}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe required bounded set is invariant on each compact interval, uniformly in width on a high-probability initialization event.  From (1.5), \\\\(|e(t)|\\\\le |e(0)|\\\\), and hence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|C(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|C(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+2a_0|e(0)|t.  \\\\tag{2.11}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (2.2) then bounds \\\\(\\\\|B(t)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\), followed by \\\\(\\\\|X(t)\\\\|_n\\\\).  Explicitly,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\le2a_0|e(0)|\\\\|C\\\\|_n,\\n\\\\quad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le2|e(0)|\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\|C\\\\|_n.             \\\\tag{2.12}\\n\\\\]\\nNow \\\\(\\\\|C_n(0)\\\\|_n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\), \\\\(\\\\|C_n(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty=o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\|X_n(0)\\\\|_n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\).  A self-contained \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net argument gives a constant \\\\(M_0\\\\) with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|B_n(0)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}>M_0)\\\\le2e^{-cn}. \\\\tag{2.13}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed, two unit-sphere \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points each,\\n\\\\(\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le2\\\\max_{u,v}|v^\\\\top Bu|\\\\), and every fixed bilinear form is \\\\(N(0,n^{-1})\\\\); a union bound proves (2.13) for any sufficiently large fixed \\\\(M_0\\\\).\\n\\nConsequently (2.2) has a unique global finite-width solution.  On every \\\\([0,T]\\\\), explicit Euler with mesh \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\), polygonally interpolated, satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^\\\\delta(t),\\\\theta_n(t))\\n\\\\le C_T\\\\delta                                             \\\\tag{2.14}\\n\\\\]\\non an event of probability tending to one.  To verify (2.14), the local defect is at most \\\\(\\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\delta^2\\\\), where \\\\(M_T\\\\) bounds the vector field; the recurrence\\n\\\\(r_{k+1}\\\\le(1+L_T\\\\delta)r_k+\\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\delta^2\\\\) and discrete Gronwall give the assertion.  A stopped bootstrap keeps the Euler iterates in the slightly enlarged bounded set.\\n\\n## 3. The only nonclassical external theorem\\n\\nWe use the following exact specialization of Greg Yang, *Tensor Programs III: Neural Matrix Laws*, arXiv:2009.10685v3, Setup 2.2 and Theorem 2.10.\\n\\n**Fixed-program reused-Gaussian theorem.**  Fix a finite program whose initial matrix has iid \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\) entries, whose initial vector coordinates are iid copies of a fixed jointly Gaussian vector (the covariance may be singular), and whose instructions are coordinatewise polynomially bounded maps and finitely many applications of \\\\(B_0\\\\) and \\\\(B_0^\\\\top\\\\).  For every fixed finite list of program vectors and every polynomially bounded test \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_{i=1}^n\\\\psi(Z_{n,i}^1,\\\\ldots,Z_{n,i}^r)\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\psi(Z^1,\\\\ldots,Z^r)\\\\quad\\\\text{almost surely}. \\\\tag{3.1}\\n\\\\]\\nThe limiting variables retain every reused-transpose response term; a transpose call is not replaced by an independent multiplication.\\n\\nThe full primary proof was checked.  Its Gaussian-conditioning step conditions the matrix simultaneously on all preceding \\\\(BY\\\\) and \\\\(B^\\\\top V\\\\) calls.  A simultaneous induction proves empirical-moment convergence and constructs a full-support core set.  At singular covariance, rank stability and zero stability show that a zero limiting innovation is eventually an exact finite-width linear dependence; a positive innovation is a high-rank projected Gaussian for which the proof establishes the required strong law.  This closes the singular-covariance case without assuming rank stability.  The theorem is only for one fixed finite parameterless program.\\n\\nWe do **not** use the paper's Appendix A.1 conditional almost-sure strengthening or Appendix A.2 mean-convergence proof.  Independent inspection found that the former is false for arbitrary varying conditioning sigma-fields and that the printed proof of the latter omits a uniform-integrability step.  Neither is needed here.  Nor do we import the paper's operator-norm citation; (2.13) was proved directly.\\n\\n## 4. Fixed-mesh width limit of the actual Euler network\\n\\nFix \\\\(\\\\delta>0\\\\) and \\\\(N=\\\\lceil T/\\\\delta\\\\rceil<\\\\infty\\\\).  Unrolling the learned matrix gives the exact formulas\\n\\\\[\\nB_k=B_0-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_rD_r\\\\otimes_nH_r,       \\\\tag{4.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nB_kv=B_0v-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_rD_r\\\\langle H_r,v\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nB_k^\\\\top u=B_0^\\\\top u-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_rH_r\\\\langle D_r,u\\\\rangle_n. \\\\tag{4.2}\\n\\\\]\\nConstruct a deterministic population oracle recursively.  Set its readout initialization to the exactly zero Gaussian vector.  At every step replace \\\\(e_r\\\\) and every normalized inner product in (4.2) by the deterministic expectation prescribed by the previously constructed oracle variables.  Encode its Euler update in the \\\\(A\\\\)-coordinate.  Because \\\\(|q|\\\\le1\\\\), all dynamic coordinate maps are polynomially bounded; the sole matrix instructions are finitely many \\\\(B_0,B_0^\\\\top\\\\) calls.  The theorem in Section 3 therefore applies, including every response caused by reuse of \\\\(B_0^\\\\top\\\\).\\n\\nThis does not yet identify the actual scalar-feedback network.  Couple it and the oracle using the same \\\\(A_0,B_0\\\\).  Their initial \\\\(C\\\\)-difference tends to zero in normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\), and both \\\\(C\\\\)-vectors remain uniformly bounded coordinatewise for the fixed number of steps.  Let \\\\(r_k\\\\) be the maximum normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\) difference of constructed vector probes together with the operator norm of the difference of the learned rank memories.  Applying (2.5)--(2.10) and (4.2) node by node gives\\n\\\\[\\nr_{k+1}\\\\le(1+C_T\\\\delta)r_k+C_T\\\\delta\\\\,\\\\zeta_{n,k}, \\\\tag{4.3}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\zeta_{n,k}\\\\) is a finite sum of oracle empirical averages minus their population expectations.  By (3.1), \\\\(\\\\max_{k<N}\\\\zeta_{n,k}\\\\to0\\\\) almost surely.  Since \\\\(N\\\\) is fixed, discrete Gronwall gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{k\\\\le N}r_k\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.                       \\\\tag{4.4}\\n\\\\]\\nThus every fixed finite collection of actual Euler probes, empirical Gram quantities, kernel terms, output, and loss has the oracle limit.  Nothing here lets \\\\(N\\\\) grow with \\\\(n\\\\).\\n\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a048ce-985d-72e3-ac2c-30a4d142e2f5\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a048cd-3d26-7002-8697-43cb31dd0a03\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l2_continuum_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1902\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying Euler scheme distinctions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-04024a8c-f9f3-410a-8f3b-e4df2a34bf68\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -206,3 +206,19 @@\\n \\\\]\\n-Construct a deterministic population oracle recursively.  Set its readout initialization to the exactly zero Gaussian vector.  At every step replace \\\\(e_r\\\\) and every normalized inner product in (4.2) by the deterministic expectation prescribed by the previously constructed oracle variables.  Encode its Euler update in the \\\\(A\\\\)-coordinate.  Because \\\\(|q|\\\\le1\\\\), all dynamic coordinate maps are polynomially bounded; the sole matrix instructions are finitely many \\\\(B_0,B_0^\\\\top\\\\) calls.  The theorem in Section 3 therefore applies, including every response caused by reuse of \\\\(B_0^\\\\top\\\\).\\n+Construct a deterministic population oracle recursively.  Set its readout initialization to the exactly zero Gaussian vector and put \\\\(\\\\bar X_0=F(A_0)\\\\).  If \\\\(\\\\bar H_k=h(\\\\bar X_k)\\\\), define\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\begin{aligned}\\n+\\\\bar S_k={}&B_0\\\\bar H_k\\n+-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\bar e_r\\\\bar D_r\\\\,\\n+             \\\\mathbb E[\\\\bar H_r\\\\bar H_k],\\\\\\\\\\n+\\\\bar G_k={}&\\\\arctan\\\\bar S_k,qquad\\n+\\\\bar D_k=\\\\bar C_kq(\\\\bar S_k),\\\\\\\\\\n+\\\\bar e_k={}&\\\\mathbb E[\\\\bar C_k\\\\bar G_k]-1,\\\\\\\\\\n+\\\\bar P_k={}&B_0^\\\\top\\\\bar D_k\\n+-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\bar e_r\\\\bar H_r\\\\,\\n+             \\\\mathbb E[\\\\bar D_r\\\\bar D_k],\\\\\\\\\\n+\\\\bar X_{k+1}={}&\\\\bar X_k-2\\\\delta\\\\bar e_k\\\\bar P_k,qquad\\n+\\\\bar C_{k+1}=\\\\bar C_k-2\\\\delta\\\\bar e_k\\\\bar G_k.\\n+\\\\end{aligned}                                                   \\\\tag{4.3}\\n+\\\\]\\n+The learned operator is represented by (4.1); hence (4.3) is exactly the Euler scheme for (2.2), not a new model.  Its cubic initialization map \\\\(F\\\\), and every later coordinate map, are polynomially bounded; the sole matrix instructions are finitely many \\\\(B_0,B_0^\\\\top\\\\) calls.  Theorem (3.1) therefore applies, including every response caused by reuse of \\\\(B_0^\\\\top\\\\).  We need only its almost-sure empirical-moment conclusion, so the cubic \\\\(F\\\\) creates no uniform-integrability issue.\\n \\n@@ -210,3 +226,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-r_{k+1}\\\\le(1+C_T\\\\delta)r_k+C_T\\\\delta\\\\,\\\\zeta_{n,k}, \\\\tag{4.3}\\n+r_{k+1}\\\\le(1+C_T\\\\delta)r_k+C_T\\\\delta\\\\,\\\\zeta_{n,k}, \\\\tag{4.4}\\n \\\\]\\n@@ -215,5 +231,4 @@\\n \\\\max_{k\\\\le N}r_k\\\\longrightarrow0\\n-\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.                       \\\\tag{4.4}\\n+\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.                       \\\\tag{4.5}\\n \\\\]\\n Thus every fixed finite collection of actual Euler probes, empirical Gram quantities, kernel terms, output, and loss has the oracle limit.  Nothing here lets \\\\(N\\\\) grow with \\\\(n\\\\).\\n-\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-c6153b52-6385-4427-b961-ce5cde9fc4ef\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -234 +234,138 @@\\n Thus every fixed finite collection of actual Euler probes, empirical Gram quantities, kernel terms, output, and loss has the oracle limit.  Nothing here lets \\\\(N\\\\) grow with \\\\(n\\\\).\\n+\\n+## 5. Continuous action law by a same-width Euler sandwich\\n+\\n+For each finite \\\\(n\\\\), compare two auxiliary Euler schemes, of meshes \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\delta'\\\\), through the same exact finite-width ODE (2.2).  Equation (2.14) gives\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^\\\\delta(t),\\\\theta_n^{\\\\delta'}(t))\\n+\\\\le C_T(\\\\delta+\\\\delta')                              \\\\tag{5.1}\\n+\\\\]\\n+with probability tending to one.  We never compare matrices of different dimensions.\\n+\\n+To define the law-level state precisely, enumerate a countable two-sorted probe grammar.  Its right probes begin with \\\\(X\\\\), its left probes with \\\\(C\\\\), and it is closed under rational linear combinations, the coordinate maps occurring in (2.2), normalized inner products, and applications of the current \\\\(B\\\\) and \\\\(B^*\\\\).  Record every finite joint coordinate law of same-side probes and every inner product.  A metric is obtained by summing \\\\(2^{-m}\\\\) times truncated \\\\(W_2\\\\) distances over an enumeration of these finite records.  This is the **action-law topology**.\\n+\\n+For any fixed finite probe, (5.1) and the coordinate coupling give a \\\\(C_{T,P}(\\\\delta+\\\\delta')\\\\) bound between the two probe laws.  Take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) using Section 4.  The deterministic fixed-mesh action laws are therefore Cauchy as \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\downarrow0\\\\); the weighted metric turns the finite-probe statements into convergence of the complete countable state.  Denote its limit by \\\\(\\\\mathfrak S(t)\\\\).\\n+\\n+Here is a concrete realization, needed for well-posedness rather than merely for notation.  Positivity of every finite empirical Gram matrix passes to the limit.  Quotient the rational right and left probe spans by their zero seminorms and complete them to \\\\(\\\\mathcal H_R\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\mathcal H_L\\\\).  By (2.13) and Borel--Cantelli, along the product coupling used in (3.1), \\\\(\\\\|B_0\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le M_0\\\\) eventually almost surely.  Hence\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\|B_0v\\\\|_{\\\\mathcal H_L}\\\\le M_0\\\\|v\\\\|_{\\\\mathcal H_R} \\\\tag{5.2}\\n+\\\\]\\n+for every formal probe and then by completion.  The finite identity\\n+\\\\(\\\\langle u,B_0v\\\\rangle_n=\\\\langle B_0^\\\\top u,v\\\\rangle_n\\\\) proves that the two actions extend as bounded adjoints.  The current operator is\\n+\\\\[\\n+B(t)=B_0-2\\\\int_0^te(s)D(s)\\\\otimes H(s)\\\\,ds,         \\\\tag{5.3}\\n+\\\\]\\n+a Bochner integral of bounded rank-one operators, so it too is bounded with the displayed adjoint.\\n+\\n+The recorded finite joint laws generate commutative coordinate probability spaces on both sides; the preceding Hilbert spaces are their \\\\(L^2\\\\) closures.  Coordinatewise Lipschitz maps act continuously on \\\\(L^2\\\\).  The product \\\\(Cq(S)\\\\) is legitimate because \\\\(C(t)\\\\in L^\\\\infty\\\\), by the limiting version of (2.11).  Therefore (2.2) is a genuine integral equation for \\\\((X,B,C)\\\\) on this two-sorted realization.\\n+\\n+The estimate (2.10), integrated in time and followed by Gronwall, proves uniqueness among solutions with the a-priori bounds (2.11)--(2.12).  Two realizations of the same complete action law are identified first on the formal probes and then by completion; the equations are equivariant under that identification.  Thus uniqueness descends to the equivalence class.  Equation (2.2) at time \\\\(t_0\\\\) uses only the current \\\\((X(t_0),B(t_0),C(t_0))\\\\), not \\\\(B_0\\\\) or the preceding trajectory.  The solution can therefore be restarted and satisfies\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\Phi_{t+s}=\\\\Phi_t\\\\circ\\\\Phi_s.                       \\\\tag{5.4}\\n+\\\\]\\n+This proves autonomy and restartability.\\n+\\n+Combining (2.14), Section 4, and the Cauchy construction gives, for every finite list of action probes,\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_{\\\\mathrm{probe}}\\n+  (\\\\theta_n(t),\\\\mathfrak S(t))\\\\longrightarrow0\\n+\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.                        \\\\tag{5.5}\\n+\\\\]\\n+\\n+## 6. Path laws, kernels, and velocities\\n+\\n+The raw preactivations are \\\\(A=J(X)\\\\) and \\\\(S=Bh(X)\\\\).  From (1.3), (1.6), and the bounds of Section 2,\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\sup_n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\bigl(\\\\|\\\\dot A(t)\\\\|_n+\\n+\\\\|\\\\dot S(t)\\\\|_n\\\\bigr)<\\\\infty                       \\\\tag{6.1}\\n+\\\\]\\n+on the high-probability initialization event.  For either \\\\(Z=A\\\\) or \\\\(Z=S\\\\), and an interval \\\\(I\\\\) of length \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\),\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\sup_{s,t\\\\in I}|Z_i(t)-Z_i(s)|^2\\n+\\\\le\\\\delta\\\\int_I\\\\|\\\\dot Z(r)\\\\|_n^2\\\\,dr.            \\\\tag{6.2}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Hence each empirical path law is within \\\\(C_T\\\\sqrt\\\\delta\\\\) in \\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\) of its grid projection.  At a fixed grid, weak convergence and convergence of the squared grid maximum follow from (3.1), since that maximum is a polynomially bounded test of finitely many vectors.  First let \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\downarrow0\\\\) in (6.2).  This proves\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{A_i(\\\\cdot)}\\\\to\\\\mu_A,qquad\\n+\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_j\\\\delta_{S_j(\\\\cdot)}\\\\to\\\\mu_S\\n+\\\\quad\\\\text{in }W_2(C([0,T]))                       \\\\tag{6.3}\\n+\\\\]\\n+in probability, as well as uniform integrability of the squared path suprema.\\n+\\n+One further argument is necessary for the gated backpropagated fields; they are not naively Lipschitz as maps \\\\(L^2\\\\times L^2\\\\to L^2\\\\).  Put again \\\\(P=B^*D\\\\).  Equations (2.5)--(2.9) show that\\n+\\\\[\\n+(X,B,C)\\\\longmapsto(A,S,D,P)                         \\\\tag{6.4}\\n+\\\\]\\n+is Lipschitz from the bounded regularized state into normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\).  Also\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\dot P=\\\\dot B^*D+B^*\\\\dot D,qquad\\n+\\\\dot D=\\\\dot Cq(S)+Cq'(S)\\\\dot S,                    \\\\tag{6.5}\\n+\\\\]\\n+and (1.6) implies a uniform \\\\(L^2\\\\) bound on the right sides.  Consequently the joint empirical laws of \\\\((A,P)\\\\) converge uniformly in time in \\\\(W_2(\\\\mathbb R^2)\\\\), and the resulting family is compact in that topology.\\n+\\n+We use the following elementary lemma.\\n+\\n+**Dominated pushforward lemma.**  If \\\\(\\\\mu_r\\\\to\\\\mu\\\\) in \\\\(W_2(\\\\mathbb R^2)\\\\), with coordinates \\\\((a,p)\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\Psi\\\\) is continuous with \\\\(|\\\\Psi(a,p)|\\\\le |p|\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\Psi_\\\\#\\\\mu_r\\\\to\\\\Psi_\\\\#\\\\mu\\\\) in \\\\(W_2(\\\\mathbb R)\\\\).  The assertion is uniform over a compact \\\\(W_2\\\\)-family.\\n+\\n+**Proof.**  \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence is weak convergence plus uniform integrability of \\\\(a^2+p^2\\\\).  On \\\\(|p|\\\\le M\\\\), truncate further to a compact set and approximate \\\\(\\\\Psi\\\\) by bounded continuous functions.  Outside it,\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\int|\\\\Psi|^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_r\\n+\\\\le\\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_r,              \\\\tag{6.6}\\n+\\\\]\\n+which tends to zero uniformly in \\\\(r\\\\) as \\\\(M\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).  This proves weak convergence and convergence of second moments of the pushforwards, hence \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence.  Compactness gives the uniform form. \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n+\\n+Apply the lemma to\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\Psi_1(a,p)=q(a)p,qquad \\\\Psi_2(a,p)=q(a)^2p.       \\\\tag{6.7}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Both are bounded in magnitude by \\\\(|p|\\\\).  It follows that\\n+\\\\(-2e\\\\Psi_1(A,P)=\\\\dot A\\\\), its squared norm \\\\(4e^2K^A\\\\), and \\\\(Q=\\\\Psi_2(A,P)\\\\) converge uniformly in time in the needed law/scalar modes.  For the remaining term in (1.6), truncate \\\\(Q\\\\) and use\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\|B(Q-Q_M)\\\\|_n\\\\le M_B\\\\|Q-Q_M\\\\|_n.                 \\\\tag{6.8}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Thus \\\\(\\\\dot S\\\\) converges in time-integrated \\\\(L^2\\\\), its squared norm converges in \\\\(L^1([0,T])\\\\), and both velocity integrals pass to the limit.  Compactness of the limiting \\\\((A,P)\\\\)-path and (6.6) make the truncation uniform in time.  The blocks \\\\(K^B,K^C\\\\) use only bounded activations and \\\\(D\\\\), so (2.5)--(2.9) handle them directly.  Consequently\\n+\\\\[\\n+K_n^r\\\\to K^r\\\\quad(r=A,B,C),qquad K_n\\\\to K         \\\\tag{6.9}\\n+\\\\]\\n+uniformly on \\\\([0,T]\\\\), and \\\\(f_n,L_n\\\\) converge uniformly as well.\\n+\\n+## 7. Exact GD versus the finite-width flow\\n+\\n+It remains to replace the auxiliary Euler schemes by the exact GD specified in the theorem.  Let\\n+\\\\[\\n+R=-2eB^*D.                                          \\\\tag{7.1}\\n+\\\\]\\n+One raw GD step is \\\\(A^+=A+\\\\eta q(A)R\\\\); the \\\\(B,C\\\\) updates are exactly the Euler updates of (2.2).  Since \\\\(F\\\\) is cubic,\\n+\\\\[\\n+F(A+\\\\eta q(A)R)=F(A)+\\\\eta R\\n+ +\\\\eta^2Aq(A)^2R^2+\\\\frac{\\\\eta^3}{3}q(A)^3R^3       \\\\tag{7.2}\\n+\\\\]\\n+coordinatewise, with no remainder.  Now\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\sup_a|a|q(a)^2<\\\\infty,qquad\\n+\\\\|R\\\\|_n\\\\le C_T,qquad\\n+\\\\|R\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\sqrt n\\\\|R\\\\|_n.                  \\\\tag{7.3}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Therefore the one-step defect relative to Euler in the \\\\(X\\\\)-coordinate is at most\\n+\\\\[\\n+C_T(\\\\eta^2\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta^3n).                         \\\\tag{7.4}\\n+\\\\]\\n+The ordinary ODE local defect is \\\\(O_T(\\\\eta^2)\\\\).  Applying the dimension-free stability estimate (2.10) step by step gives\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\\\theta_n(t))\\n+\\\\le C_T\\\\bigl(\\\\eta+\\\\eta\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta^2n\\\\bigr).       \\\\tag{7.5}\\n+\\\\]\\n+The proof is first stopped on the enlarged bounded set from Section 2; the right side tends to zero, so the first-exit event is impossible for large \\\\(n\\\\).  With \\\\(\\\\eta=\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\), (7.5) tends to zero.\\n+\\n+For completeness, polygonal GD also has the correct hidden velocity energies.  Its first-layer slope is exactly \\\\(-2e_kq(A_k)P_k\\\\).  If \\\\(V_{A,k}\\\\) and \\\\(V_{B,k}\\\\) are the raw \\\\(A,B\\\\) slopes, then\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\begin{aligned}\\n+\\\\frac{S_{k+1}-S_k}{\\\\eta}\\n+={}&V_{B,k}H_k\\n++B_k\\\\frac{\\\\arctan(A_k+\\\\eta V_{A,k})-\\\\arctan A_k}{\\\\eta}\\\\\\\\\\n+&+V_{B,k}\\\\{\\\\arctan(A_k+\\\\eta V_{A,k})-\\\\arctan A_k\\\\}. \\\\tag{7.6}\\n+\\\\end{aligned}\\n+\\\\]\\n+On \\\\(|P_k|\\\\le M\\\\), the middle quotient differs from \\\\(q(A_k)V_{A,k}\\\\) by \\\\(O_T(\\\\eta M^2)\\\\).  On the complement, both are bounded by \\\\(C_T|P_k|\\\\), whose square tails are controlled by the compact \\\\(W_2\\\\) family in Section 6.  First take the width limit, then \\\\(M\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).  The last line of (7.6) tends to zero because \\\\(\\\\|V_{B,k}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le C_T\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\|\\\\Delta H_k\\\\|_n\\\\le\\\\eta\\\\|V_{A,k}\\\\|_n\\\\).  Thus both discrete hidden-velocity energies converge to their continuous counterparts.\\n+\\n+Finally, the actual \\\\(C_n(0)\\\\) can be replaced by zero uniformly on compact time intervals: its normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\) norm is \\\\(O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\), its sup norm is \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\), and (2.10) applies.  Sections 4--7 prove the full joint diagonal convergence asserted in the theorem.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-2a652b66-0624-48c3-b62b-d44a359b951b\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -371 +371,149 @@\\n Finally, the actual \\\\(C_n(0)\\\\) can be replaced by zero uniformly on compact time intervals: its normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\) norm is \\\\(O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\), its sup norm is \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\), and (2.10) applies.  Sections 4--7 prove the full joint diagonal convergence asserted in the theorem.\\n+\\n+## 8. Exact initial action law and reused-adjoint response\\n+\\n+Let\\n+\\\\[\\n+A_0\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\qquad H_0=\\\\arctan A_0,qquad\\n+m=\\\\mathbb E H_0^2>0.                               \\\\tag{8.1}\\n+\\\\]\\n+The initial second preactivation has law\\n+\\\\[\\n+Z\\\\sim N(0,m).                                       \\\\tag{8.2}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Put\\n+\\\\[\\n+G_0=\\\\arctan Z,qquad\\n+u(Z)=G_0q(Z)=\\\\frac{\\\\arctan Z}{1+Z^2},qquad\\n+\\\\nu=\\\\mathbb Eu(Z)^2>0,qquad\\n+\\\\kappa=\\\\mathbb Eu'(Z).                              \\\\tag{8.3}\\n+\\\\]\\n+The reused adjoint \\\\(\\\\Xi=B_0^*u(Z)\\\\) contains both an innovation and a response.  We prove its law directly.\\n+\\n+Condition on \\\\(H=(H_i)\\\\).  For row \\\\(b_j\\\\) of \\\\(B_0\\\\), let \\\\(s_j=b_j^\\\\top H\\\\) and \\\\(m_n=\\\\|H\\\\|_n^2\\\\).  Gaussian regression gives\\n+\\\\[\\n+b_j=\\\\frac{s_j}{nm_n}H+\\\\widetilde b_j,qquad\\n+\\\\widetilde b_j\\\\mid(H,s_j)\\\\sim\\n+N\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1nP_H^\\\\perp\\\\right),                 \\\\tag{8.4}\\n+\\\\]\\n+independently over \\\\(j\\\\).  Consequently, conditionally on \\\\((H,s)\\\\),\\n+\\\\[\\n+B_0^\\\\top u(s)=a_nH+\\\\sqrt{\\\\nu_n}\\\\,P_H^\\\\perp\\\\Gamma, \\\\tag{8.5}\\n+\\\\]\\n+where\\n+\\\\[\\n+a_n=\\\\frac1{nm_n}\\\\sum_js_ju(s_j),qquad\\n+\\\\nu_n=\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_ju(s_j)^2,qquad\\n+\\\\Gamma\\\\sim N(0,I_n).                               \\\\tag{8.6}\\n+\\\\]\\n+The laws of large numbers give \\\\(\\\\nu_n\\\\to\\\\nu\\\\), and Gaussian integration by parts for \\\\(Z\\\\sim N(0,m)\\\\) gives\\n+\\\\[\\n+a_n\\\\to\\\\frac{\\\\mathbb E[Zu(Z)]}{m}=\\\\mathbb Eu'(Z)=\\\\kappa. \\\\tag{8.7}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Moreover \\\\(\\\\|P_H^\\\\perp\\\\Gamma-\\\\Gamma\\\\|_n\\\\to0\\\\) in probability.  Hence the joint empirical limit is\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\Xi=\\\\sqrt\\\\nu\\\\,\\\\Gamma+\\\\kappa H_0,qquad\\n+\\\\Gamma\\\\sim N(0,1),quad \\\\Gamma\\\\perp A_0.           \\\\tag{8.8}\\n+\\\\]\\n+This is precisely the innovation plus the Onsager/response term; omitting the second summand would be wrong.\\n+\\n+Define\\n+\\\\[\\n+d=\\\\mathbb E[q(A_0)^2\\\\Xi^2]\\n+=\\\\nu\\\\mathbb E q(A_0)^2\\n+\\\\kappa^2\\\\mathbb E[q(A_0)^2H_0^2]>0.                \\\\tag{8.9}\\n+\\\\]\\n+On the left Hilbert space define the positive operator\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\mathcal R=mI+B_0M_{q(A_0)^2}B_0^*,                \\\\tag{8.10}\\n+\\\\]\\n+where \\\\(M_{q(A_0)^2}\\\\) is coordinate multiplication.  Then\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\langle u,\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\rangle\\n+=m\\\\nu+\\\\langle B_0^*u,q(A_0)^2B_0^*u\\\\rangle\\n+=m\\\\nu+d>0.                                         \\\\tag{8.11}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Thus \\\\(\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\ne0\\\\).\\n+\\n+## 9. Small-time motion and completion of the theorem\\n+\\n+At \\\\(t=0\\\\), \\\\(C=0\\\\), \\\\(e=-1\\\\), and hence \\\\(D=P=0\\\\).  The integral equations, in the strong \\\\(L^2\\\\) and operator topologies constructed above, give node by node\\n+\\\\[\\n+C(t)=2tG_0+O_{L^2}(t^2),qquad\\n+D(t)=2t\\\\,u+O_{L^2}(t^2),                            \\\\tag{9.1}\\n+\\\\]\\n+\\\\[\\n+A(t)-A_0=2t^2q(A_0)\\\\Xi+o_{L^2}(t^2),               \\\\tag{9.2}\\n+\\\\]\\n+\\\\[\\n+B(t)-B_0=2t^2u\\\\otimes H_0+o_{\\\\mathrm{op}}(t^2),   \\\\tag{9.3}\\n+\\\\]\\n+and\\n+\\\\[\\n+S(t)-Z=2t^2\\\\mathcal Ru+o_{L^2}(t^2).               \\\\tag{9.4}\\n+\\\\]\\n+For example, \\\\(\\\\dot D(0)=2u\\\\), so\\n+\\\\(A''(0)=4q(A_0)B_0^*u\\\\), \\\\(B''(0)=4u\\\\otimes H_0\\\\); differentiating \\\\(S=BH\\\\) then gives\\n+\\\\[\\n+S''(0)=4\\\\{mu+B_0[q(A_0)^2B_0^*u]\\\\}=4\\\\mathcal Ru. \\\\tag{9.5}\\n+\\\\]\\n+These derivatives exist because the action flow is strongly continuous, all scalar derivatives of \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) are bounded, and the relevant operator actions are bounded.  Alternatively, (9.1)--(9.4) follow directly by dividing the integral equations by the indicated powers of \\\\(t\\\\) and using dominated convergence.\\n+\\n+The two hidden velocity energies therefore satisfy\\n+\\\\[\\n+V_A(t)^2=16dt^2+o(t^2),qquad\\n+V_S(t)^2=16\\\\|\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\|_2^2t^2+o(t^2),          \\\\tag{9.6}\\n+\\\\]\\n+and the displacements satisfy\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\mathbb E|A(t)-A_0|^2=4dt^4+o(t^4),qquad\\n+\\\\mathbb E|S(t)-Z|^2=4\\\\|\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\|_2^2t^4+o(t^4). \\\\tag{9.7}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Both leading coefficients are strictly positive by (8.9)--(8.11).  Hence both hidden layers have finite positive integrated velocity and nonzero displacement on every sufficiently short nonzero interval.\\n+\\n+Let\\n+\\\\[\\n+k_3=\\\\mathbb E(\\\\arctan Z)^2>0.                       \\\\tag{9.8}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Equations (9.1)--(9.4) yield\\n+\\\\[\\n+K^A(t)=4dt^2+o(t^2),qquad\\n+K^B(t)=4m\\\\nu t^2+o(t^2),                            \\\\tag{9.9}\\n+\\\\]\\n+and, since\\n+\\\\(G(t)=G_0+2t^2q(Z)\\\\mathcal Ru+o_{L^2}(t^2)\\\\),\\n+\\\\[\\n+K^C(t)=k_3+4(m\\\\nu+d)t^2+o(t^2).                    \\\\tag{9.10}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Indeed \\\\(\\\\langle G_0,q(Z)\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\rangle=\\\\langle u,\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\rangle=m\\\\nu+d\\\\).  Therefore\\n+\\\\[\\n+K(t)=k_3+8(m\\\\nu+d)t^2+o(t^2),                      \\\\tag{9.11}\\n+\\\\]\\n+so every block has a finite positive time integral and the total kernel is nonconstant.\\n+\\n+Also\\n+\\\\[\\n+f(t)=2k_3t+o(t),qquad\\n+L(t)=1-4k_3t+o(t),                                  \\\\tag{9.12}\\n+\\\\]\\n+so \\\\(f(0)=0\\\\ne1\\\\) and \\\\(L(T)<L(0)\\\\) for all sufficiently small \\\\(T>0\\\\).\\n+\\n+It remains only to verify hidden variance and surviving nonlinearity.  At zero the laws are \\\\(N(0,1)\\\\) and \\\\(N(0,m)\\\\).  Their variances are positive and, by (6.3), continuous in time; hence they stay positive on a short interval.  For a law \\\\(\\\\mu\\\\) with positive variance, the squared distance of \\\\(\\\\arctan z\\\\) from affine functions is\\n+\\\\[\\n+\\\\mathcal N(\\\\mu)=\\n+\\\\operatorname{Var}_\\\\mu(\\\\arctan Z)\\n+-\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{Cov}_\\\\mu(Z,\\\\arctan Z)^2}\\n+       {\\\\operatorname{Var}_\\\\mu(Z)}.                \\\\tag{9.13}\\n+\\\\]\\n+It is strictly positive for either initial Gaussian: equality would make \\\\(\\\\arctan z\\\\) affine on a full-support measure and, by continuity, on \\\\(\\\\mathbb R\\\\).  The moments in (9.13) are continuous under \\\\(W_2\\\\); for the only unbounded mixed moment, an optimal coupling gives\\n+\\\\[\\n+|\\\\mathbb E[X\\\\arctan X-Y\\\\arctan Y]|\\n+\\\\le a_0\\\\|X-Y\\\\|_2+\\\\|Y\\\\|_2\\\\|X-Y\\\\|_2.              \\\\tag{9.14}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Thus both nonlinear residuals remain positive and have positive time integrals after reducing \\\\(T_*\\\\) if necessary.\\n+\\n+All assertions in the theorem have now been proved. \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n+\\n+## Audit note on external dependencies\\n+\\n+The only research-level theorem used is the fixed finite, parameterless reused-Gaussian Master Theorem (3.1).  Its full primary proof, including the singular-covariance branch, was inspected.  Its hypotheses are met only by the deterministic population oracle of Section 4; empirical feedback, the width-dependent small readout initialization, continuous time, and exact GD were handled separately in Sections 2 and 4--7.  No growing-program, conditional-expectation, mean-convergence, DMFT, or operator-norm theorem is assumed.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1903\",\"summary\":[\"**Fixing control variables and inspecting equations**\",\"**Fixing control variables and inspecting equations**\",\"**Verifying convergence and continuity properties**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-7e5b8005-e914-4653-a7ca-70f3db495c39\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -278,3 +278,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-\\\\sup_n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\bigl(\\\\|\\\\dot A(t)\\\\|_n+\\n+\\\\sup_n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\bigl(\\\\|\\\\dot A(t)\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|\\\\dot S(t)\\\\|_n\\\\bigr)<\\\\infty                       \\\\tag{6.1}\\n@@ -415,3 +415,3 @@\\n \\\\Xi=\\\\sqrt\\\\nu\\\\,\\\\Gamma+\\\\kappa H_0,qquad\\n-\\\\Gamma\\\\sim N(0,1),quad \\\\Gamma\\\\perp A_0.           \\\\tag{8.8}\\n+\\\\Gamma\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad \\\\Gamma\\\\perp A_0.          \\\\tag{8.8}\\n \\\\]\\n@@ -457,3 +457,3 @@\\n \\\\[\\n-S''(0)=4\\\\{mu+B_0[q(A_0)^2B_0^*u]\\\\}=4\\\\mathcal Ru. \\\\tag{9.5}\\n+S''(0)=4\\\\{m u+B_0[q(A_0)^2B_0^*u]\\\\}=4\\\\mathcal Ru. \\\\tag{9.5}\\n \\\\]\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1904\",\"text\":\"The first audit round now supports an affirmative result, but only after three corrections: population-oracle rather than direct scalar-feedback TP, same-width Euler sandwich, and \\\\(W_2\\\\)-truncation for gated backpropagation. I have assembled a standalone proof with those repairs. The next round is fully blind: each referee receives only that manuscript, with one assigned to re-read the entire primary tensor-program proof.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_3zahZnqIYsBItJndqBQ1CVwH\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a048dc-09e8-7ae3-90f0-3fdb00092365\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l2_blind_primary_r1\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_wKycLZCQI9uv45LFExnapRaU\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a048dc-26a1-7833-b270-61bb2aadfe9b\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l2_blind_continuum_r1\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_TlEppq4DlJ440KRGK9rtNmno\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a048dc-3a0b-7752-92a1-5af209e6d7a8\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l2_blind_activity_r1\"},{\"type\":\"contextCompaction\",\"id\":\"item-1905\"},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1906\",\"text\":\"The standalone proof is now in a genuinely blind audit round: each reviewer received only that manuscript, and the theorem-source reviewer was separately instructed to inspect the primary paper and its proof rather than trust our summary. I’ll treat any unclosed objection as a failure, patch the argument only when there is a real proof, and rerun fresh reviewers after every substantive repair.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1907\",\"summary\":[\"**Identifying and planning to fix gradient typo**\",\"**Identifying and planning to fix gradient typo**\",\"**Verifying gradient norms and scaling consistency**\",\"**Identifying and planning to fix gradient typo**\",\"**Verifying gradient norms and scaling consistency**\",\"**Evaluating continuum limit and action law convergence**\",\"**Identifying and planning to fix gradient typo**\",\"**Verifying gradient norms and scaling consistency**\",\"**Evaluating continuum limit and action law convergence**\",\"**Confirming uniform convergence and gradient step stability**\",\"**Identifying and planning to fix gradient typo**\",\"**Verifying gradient norms and scaling consistency**\",\"**Evaluating continuum limit and action law convergence**\",\"**Confirming uniform convergence and gradient step stability**\",\"**Identifying and planning to fix gradient typo**\",\"**Verifying gradient norms and scaling consistency**\",\"**Evaluating continuum limit and action law convergence**\",\"**Confirming uniform convergence and gradient step stability**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1908\",\"text\":\"A direct algebra pass has already caught one transcription defect in the draft—the displayed definition of \\\\(d\\\\) lost a “\\\\(+\\\\)” between its two Gaussian contributions. That is typographical, not conceptual, but it illustrates why this round is line-by-line. I am also checking that the continuous-time argument does not quietly turn a fixed-program tensor theorem into a growing-step theorem; the proof must route through a same-width Euler estimate before taking the mesh to zero.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-55cfda67-2b6a-42a1-a8d7-8821f003af4a\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10685\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10685\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1909\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing theorem validity and rank stability**\",\"**Analyzing action-law uniqueness and operator norms**\",\"**Assessing theorem validity and rank stability**\",\"**Analyzing action-law uniqueness and operator norms**\",\"**Clarifying self-contained proof requirements**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a048dc-3a46-79b3-b4d6-b5811d176efb\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a048dc-3a0b-7752-92a1-5af209e6d7a8\",