orrected theorem and definitions**\",\"**Defining and verifying covariance matrices for backprop fields**\",\"**Assessing full-history map coverage**\",\"**Verifying bounded derivatives and cutoffs**\",\"**Analyzing Gaussian concentration with correlated sources**\",\"**Concluding conditional validity with external theorem**\",\"**Reconstructing corrected theorem and definitions**\",\"**Defining and verifying covariance matrices for backprop fields**\",\"**Assessing full-history map coverage**\",\"**Verifying bounded derivatives and cutoffs**\",\"**Analyzing Gaussian concentration with correlated sources**\",\"**Concluding conditional validity with external theorem**\",\"**Reconstructing corrected theorem and definitions**\",\"**Defining and verifying covariance matrices for backprop fields**\",\"**Confirming loss decay and audit summary**\",\"**Assessing full-history map coverage**\",\"**Verifying bounded derivatives and cutoffs**\",\"**Analyzing Gaussian concentration with correlated sources**\",\"**Concluding conditional validity with external theorem**\",\"**Reconstructing corrected theorem and definitions**\",\"**Defining and verifying covariance matrices for backprop fields**\",\"**Confirming loss decay and audit summary**\",\"**Confirming dual convergence proofs and external gap**\",\"**Assessing full-history map coverage**\",\"**Verifying bounded derivatives and cutoffs**\",\"**Analyzing Gaussian concentration with correlated sources**\",\"**Concluding conditional validity with external theorem**\",\"**Reconstructing corrected theorem and definitions**\",\"**Defining and verifying covariance matrices for backprop fields**\",\"**Confirming loss decay and audit summary**\",\"**Confirming dual convergence proofs and external gap**\",\"**Finalizing corrected theorem with audit summary**\",\"**Assessing full-history map coverage**\",\"**Verifying bounded derivatives and cutoffs**\",\"**Analyzing Gaussian concentration with correlated sources**\",\"**Concluding conditional validity with external theorem**\",\"**Reconstructing corrected theorem and definitions**\",\"**Defining and verifying covariance matrices for backprop fields**\",\"**Confirming loss decay and audit summary**\",\"**Confirming dual convergence proofs and external gap**\",\"**Finalizing corrected theorem with audit summary**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_J7hAae3VgNdmHFoPcomybc0J\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044a6-7e94-7280-8eea-fc1053738f0d\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/audit_algebra_activity\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1438\",\"summary\":[\"**Reconstructing cavity with Gaussian conditioning**\",\"**Reconstructing cavity with Gaussian conditioning**\",\"**Deriving backward covariances and response coefficients**\",\"**Reconstructing cavity with Gaussian conditioning**\",\"**Deriving backward covariances and response coefficients**\",\"**Confirming covariance independence and SPD properties**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a044bc-1c2c-77d2-849b-520f54d2c029\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044a6-7e94-7280-8eea-fc1053738f0d\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/audit_algebra_activity\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1439\",\"summary\":[\"**Reconstructing cavity with Gaussian conditioning**\",\"**Deriving backward covariances and response coefficients**\",\"**Confirming covariance independence and SPD properties**\",\"**Tracing gradient and covariance relationships**\",\"**Tracing gradient and covariance relationships**\",\"**Proving SPD properties of covariance matrices**\",\"**Tracing gradient and covariance relationships**\",\"**Proving SPD properties of covariance matrices**\",\"**Clarifying state construction and citation caveats**\",\"**Tracing gradient and covariance relationships**\",\"**Proving SPD properties of covariance matrices**\",\"**Clarifying state construction and citation caveats**\",\"**Formulating rigorous corrected theorem statement**\",\"**Tracing gradient and covariance relationships**\",\"**Proving SPD properties of covariance matrices**\",\"**Clarifying state construction and citation caveats**\",\"**Formulating rigorous corrected theorem statement**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1440\",\"text\":\"The audit found that the proof as originally stated was not valid. Two central arguments were unjustified. After replacing them, three independent audit rounds found no further internal gap.\\n\\nThe precise verdict is:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\\\text{The corrected \\\\(L=3\\\\) theorem is valid if Han's formally stated fixed-stage theorem is accepted.}}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt is not a completely self-contained proof from first principles; the final external-theorem caveat is stated below.\\n\\n## Corrected theorem\\n\\nFor every fixed finite dataset\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_1,\\\\dots,x_p\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|^2}{d}=1,\\\\qquad\\nx_a\\\\neq x_b\\\\;(a\\\\neq b),\\n\\\\qquad y_a\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\},\\n\\\\]\\n\\ntake\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi(x)=\\\\sin x+\\\\cos x.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor three hidden layers, use\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(W^{(1)}_{ir})=\\\\frac1d,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(W^{(2)}_{ji})\\n=\\\\operatorname{Var}(W^{(3)}_{kj})=\\\\frac1n,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(W^{(4)}_k)=\\\\frac1{n^4},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand learning-rate multipliers\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}=\\\\frac nd,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(2)}=\\\\lambda_n^{(3)}=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(4)}=\\\\frac1n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOne admissible diagonal step size is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_{0,n}=e^{-e^n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen, on a nonzero interval \\\\([0,T_*]\\\\), exact GD converges along the full sequence to an autonomous operator-valued mean-field gradient flow. The hidden path laws converge in \\\\(W_2(C([0,T_*]))\\\\); every parameter block is active; all three hidden layers move macroscopically; the nonlinear regression residual stays positive; the NTK changes; and the loss strictly decreases.\\n\\nHere \\\\(T_*\\\\) may depend on the fixed dataset. There is no dataset-uniform positive lower bound as oppositely labelled inputs approach each other.\\n\\n## Exact normalized flow\\n\\nSet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nR_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d},\\\\qquad\\nC=nW^{(4)},\\\\qquad \\\\gamma=\\\\frac2p,\\n\\\\qquad e_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand define\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nU_i^a&=W_i^{(1)\\\\top}x_a,\\n&\\nH^{1,a}&=\\\\phi(U^a),\\\\\\\\\\nS^a&=BH^{1,a},\\n&\\nH^{2,a}&=\\\\phi(S^a),\\\\\\\\\\nT^a&=AH^{2,a},\\n&\\nH^{3,a}&=\\\\phi(T^a),\\\\\\\\\\nf_a&=\\\\langle C,H^{3,a}\\\\rangle_n,\\n&\\nD^{3,a}&=C\\\\odot\\\\phi'(T^a),\\\\\\\\\\nP^{2,a}&=A^\\\\top D^{3,a},\\n&\\nD^{2,a}&=\\\\phi'(S^a)\\\\odot P^{2,a},\\\\\\\\\\nP^{1,a}&=B^\\\\top D^{2,a}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe exact finite-width gradient flow is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot U^a\\n&=-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_b R_{ab}e_b\\\\,\\n  \\\\phi'(U^b)\\\\odot P^{1,b},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot B\\n&=-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_b e_b\\\\,D^{2,b}\\\\otimes_n H^{1,b},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot A\\n&=-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_b e_b\\\\,D^{3,b}\\\\otimes_n H^{2,b},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot C\\n&=-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_b e_b\\\\,H^{3,b}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEvery factor of \\\\(n\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\), including the first-layer block, was independently recomputed and passed audit.\\n\\n## What was wrong and how it was fixed\\n\\n### 1. The growing-stage invocation was invalid\\n\\nThe earlier proof attempted to use a fixed-stage state-evolution theorem with approximately \\\\(T/h_n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) stages. The cited theorem does not justify that.\\n\\nThe repaired argument fixes \\\\(R\\\\) and \\\\(h\\\\), applies the theorem to the resulting finite program, and takes limits in the order\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(R,h)\\\\text{ fixed}\\n\\\\longrightarrow n\\\\to\\\\infty\\n\\\\longrightarrow h\\\\downarrow0\\n\\\\longrightarrow R\\\\uparrow\\\\infty.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nA three-\\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\) argument then gives convergence along the original full diagonal sequence.\\n\\n### 2. The actual reused-matrix program was not completely serialized\\n\\nThe correct single Gaussian matrix is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb A_0=[B_0,A_0^\\\\top]\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times2n},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhose entries are iid \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). At each physical step the four required identities are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_kH^1\\n=\\nB_0H^1-\\\\gamma h\\\\sum_{\\\\ell<k,b}\\ne_{\\\\ell b}D_{\\\\ell b}^2\\n\\\\langle H_{\\\\ell b}^1,H^1\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA_kH^2\\n=\\nA_0H^2-\\\\gamma h\\\\sum_{\\\\ell<k,b}\\ne_{\\\\ell b}D_{\\\\ell b}^3\\n\\\\langle H_{\\\\ell b}^2,H^2\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA_k^\\\\top D^3\\n=\\nA_0^\\\\top D^3-\\\\gamma h\\\\sum_{\\\\ell<k,b}\\ne_{\\\\ell b}H_{\\\\ell b}^2\\n\\\\langle D_{\\\\ell b}^3,D^3\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_k^\\\\top D^2\\n=\\nB_0^\\\\top D^2-\\\\gamma h\\\\sum_{\\\\ell<k,b}\\ne_{\\\\ell b}H_{\\\\ell b}^1\\n\\\\langle D_{\\\\ell b}^2,D^2\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe direct-sum program also produces the unused coordinates\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_k^\\\\top H^2,\\\\qquad A_kD^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTheir overlaps must remain in the deterministic oracle ledger even though subsequent physical decoders have zero derivative with respect to them. Omitting them, as the earlier proof effectively did, leaves the GFOM representation incomplete.\\n\\n### 3. Actual empirical scalars were separated from deterministic oracle scalars\\n\\nThe external theorem applies to deterministic row-separate maps, not maps containing random empirical outputs and overlaps. The corrected proof first replaces every such scalar by its state-evolution value and then compares the actual and oracle programs.\\n\\nIncluding both learned rank memories, the comparison error satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_{k+1}\\n\\\\le\\n(1+C_Rh)\\\\delta_k+C_Rh\\\\zeta_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\zeta_n\\\\to0\\\\) contains all output and overlap discrepancies, including the two unused-component overlaps. Discrete Grönwall gives actual-oracle convergence.\\n\\n### 4. The averaged theorem is no longer used\\n\\nThe source states an averaged asymmetric theorem but omits its proof. The correction instead uses only the fixed-coordinate theorem.\\n\\nFor an empirical probe\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA_n=\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\psi_i(X_i),\\n\\\\]\\n\\napply the entrywise theorem to one coordinate and to pairs \\\\(i\\\\neq j\\\\). The state-evolution rows are independent, so\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(A_n\\\\mid\\\\mathcal M)\\n\\\\le \\\\frac Cn+C\\\\varepsilon_n,\\n\\\\qquad \\\\varepsilon_n\\\\to0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\mathcal M\\\\) is the collection of independent side marks. Conditional Chebyshev, followed by the side-mark law of large numbers, proves empirical convergence.\\n\\nA separate audit obtained the same result from spectral clipping and Gaussian concentration:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Pr\\\\!\\\\left(\\n|A_n-\\\\mathbb E[A_n\\\\mid\\\\mathcal M]|>u\\n\\\\,\\\\middle|\\\\,\\\\mathcal M\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\le\\n2e^{-cnu^2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nPolynomially growing probes follow by smooth truncation and uniform high-moment bounds.\\n\\n### 5. The source-tail proof had a circular bootstrap\\n\\nThe earlier proof used a bound on historical response mass while simultaneously deriving that bound. The repair orders the microstages lexicographically:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS^a\\\\to H^{2,a}\\\\to T^a\\\\to H^{3,a}\\\\to D^{3,a}\\n\\\\to P^{2,a}\\\\to D^{2,a}\\\\to P^{1,a}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus \\\\(P^{2,a}\\\\) is bounded before \\\\(P^{1,a}\\\\), and neither appears on its own right-hand side.\\n\\nFor a Gaussian source \\\\(q\\\\) born in cycle \\\\(\\\\ell\\\\), its later influence satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\nI_{\\\\ell+1,q}\\\\le ChX_\\\\ell^2,\\n\\\\qquad\\nI_{k+1,q}\\\\le(1+ChX_k)I_{kq},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nX_k=1+\\\\sum_a\\\\bigl(|P_k^{1,a}|+|P_k^{2,a}|\\\\bigr).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nI_{kq}\\n\\\\le\\nChX_\\\\ell^2\\n\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\nCh\\\\sum_{m=\\\\ell+1}^{k-1}X_m\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEvery cross-cycle source path contains an \\\\(h\\\\)-factor: it passes through a slow update, a learned-rank update, or a response coefficient whose derivative has already crossed one of those edges.\\n\\nGeneralized Hölder, without any independence assumption, gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{k,a}\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\|P_k^{1,a}\\\\|_{\\\\psi_2}\\n+\\n\\\\|P_k^{2,a}\\\\|_{\\\\psi_2}\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\le CT\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand historical response mass \\\\(\\\\Gamma_k\\\\le CT\\\\), uniformly in \\\\(h,R\\\\). Hence\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\n|P_k^{r,a}|^2\\n\\\\mathbf 1_{\\\\{|P_k^{r,a}|>u\\\\}}\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\le\\nCT^2\\n\\\\left(1+\\\\frac{u^2}{T^2}\\\\right)\\ne^{-cu^2/T^2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis remains valid at singular Gaussian covariances because only formal source derivatives are used; no covariance inverse appears.\\n\\n### 6. Cutoff removal and exact GD now close\\n\\nFor the cutoff and uncut flows,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nD_R'(t)\\n\\\\le\\nC(1+R)D_R(t)\\n+\\nC\\\\sum_{\\\\ell=1}^2\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\nP_R^\\\\ell\\\\mathbf1_{\\\\{|P_R^\\\\ell|>R/2\\\\}}\\n\\\\right\\\\|_2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe preceding tail estimate yields\\n\\n\\\\[\\nD_R(T)\\n\\\\le\\nC\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\nC(1+R)T-\\\\frac{cR^2}{T^2}\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nExact GD is compared with the finite-width ODE only after a polynomial-width localization. For some fixed \\\\(q\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{k\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\le T}\\nd(\\\\theta_{n,k}^{\\\\rm GD},\\\\theta_n(k\\\\eta_{0,n}))\\n\\\\le\\nC_T\\\\eta_{0,n}n^q e^{n^qT}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe double-exponential step size is deliberately conservative but admissible.\\n\\n## Activity and non-laziness audit\\n\\nAt initialization,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_1=e^{-1+R},\\\\qquad\\nQ_2=e^{-1+Q_1},\\\\qquad\\nQ_3=e^{-1+Q_2},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nentrywise. Pairwise distinct inputs make \\\\(Q_1\\\\) strictly positive definite; then \\\\(Q_2,Q_3\\\\) are strictly positive definite as well.\\n\\nThe exact Gaussian cavity energies can be defined as follows. For \\\\(T\\\\sim N(0,Q_2)\\\\), let\\n\\n\\\\[\\ng(T)=\\\\sum_b y_b\\\\phi(T_b),\\\\qquad\\n(v_3)_a=y_ag(T)\\\\phi'(T_a),\\n\\\\qquad\\nM_3=\\\\mathbb E[v_3v_3^\\\\top].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\Lambda_3)_{ac}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\partial_{T_c}(v_3)_a],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Pi_3=\\\\xi_3+\\\\Lambda_3\\\\phi(S),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\xi_3\\\\sim N(0,M_3),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith \\\\(S\\\\sim N(0,Q_1)\\\\) independent of \\\\(\\\\xi_3\\\\), and define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nv_2=\\\\phi'(S)\\\\odot\\\\Pi_3,\\n\\\\qquad M_2=\\\\mathbb E[v_2v_2^\\\\top].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nNext let\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\Lambda_2)_{ac}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\partial_{S_c}(v_2)_a],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Pi_2=\\\\xi_2+\\\\Lambda_2\\\\phi(U),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\xi_2\\\\sim N(0,M_2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith \\\\(U\\\\sim N(0,R)\\\\), and define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nv_1=\\\\phi'(U)\\\\odot\\\\Pi_2,\\n\\\\qquad M_1=\\\\mathbb E[v_1v_1^\\\\top].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAnalyticity and full support give\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM_3>0,\\\\qquad M_2>0,\\\\qquad M_1>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe last assertion remains true when \\\\(R\\\\) is singular because every marginal \\\\(U_a\\\\sim N(0,1)\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) has only discrete zeros.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\nE_A=\\\\operatorname{tr}(Q_2M_3),\\\\qquad\\nE_B=\\\\operatorname{tr}(Q_1M_2),\\\\qquad\\nE_w=\\\\operatorname{tr}(RM_1).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll three are strictly positive. Integral-remainder expansions of the already-constructed limiting flow give\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta^{(3)}(t)y\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2E_A t^2+O(t^3),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta^{(2)}(t)y\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2E_B t^2+O(t^3),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta^{(1)}(t)y\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2E_w t^2+O(t^3),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top[\\\\Theta(t)-\\\\Theta(0)]y\\n=\\n2\\\\gamma^2(E_w+E_B+E_A)t^2+O(t^3)>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore there is no label-dependent cancellation. All hidden blocks activate, all hidden preactivations move, and the total kernel is nonconstant. Moreover,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot{\\\\mathcal L}(0)\\n=\\n-\\\\frac4{p^2}y^\\\\top Q_3y<0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n## External-theorem audit\\n\\nThe exact theorem used is Han’s fixed-stage asymmetric GFOM state-evolution theorem in [Entrywise Dynamics and Universality of General First Order Methods](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.19061). Its hypotheses match:\\n\\n- one \\\\(n\\\\times2n\\\\) independent Gaussian matrix scaled by \\\\(n^{-1/2}\\\\);\\n- proportional dimensions;\\n- fixed stage count;\\n- deterministic row-separate maps;\\n- \\\\(C^3\\\\) maps with globally bounded derivatives after smooth cutoffs;\\n- no nonsingularity assumption on the Gaussian covariance.\\n\\nWe do not use it for \\\\(h=h_n\\\\to0\\\\), and we do not use its averaged theorem.\\n\\nHowever, Han’s proof of the entrywise result itself invokes a full-history asymmetric AMP extension rather tersely. The underlying memory-one asymmetric AMP and leave-one-out results are proved in [Bao–Han–Xu, A Leave-One-Out Approach to Approximate Message Passing](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05911), but that paper does not state Han’s full-history extension verbatim.\\n\\nTherefore:\\n\\n- Under the normal mathematical convention that a formally stated theorem may be cited after checking its precise hypotheses, the corrected proof passes.\\n- If “fully rigorous” requires re-proving every cited theorem rather than accepting its formal statement, one external source-level gap remains, and the result should be called conditional on Han’s fixed-stage entrywise theorem.\\n\\nNo project files were inspected during this audit.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a04476-e59d-7fe1-b4aa-cf7d9d311ce4\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1787855037,\"completedAt\":1787856160,\"durationMs\":1122748,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1394\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"can you extend this to L=3 hidden layers with a rigorous proof ? the goal is exactly the same as before (joint mean field continuous gradient flow limit, feature learning, changes in loss, all as before, you can change the hyper parameters as long as you dont' deviate from these set rules, as well as keeping the nonlinearity active (don't converge to a linear net in the limit), make sure your proof is fully rigorous and fully and indepdnently audited&#x20;\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1395\",\"summary\":[\"**Creating new goal for audits**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1396\",\"text\":\"I’m treating this as a new proof obligation, not as a formal “add one more matrix” extrapolation. I’ll use the rigorous-math workflow to isolate the two genuinely new issues at \\\\(L=3\\\\): closure of two reused forward/adjoint operators in continuous time, and simultaneous nondegeneracy/activity of all four parameter blocks. I’ll also run independent constructive and adversarial audits, while reading no project files.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_YtBvwvNoowYgt6jHye2g7JLJ\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04477-239c-7a20-98db-ae6e84be4d9b\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_scaling_activity\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_MZ8HM3Sa4MbHndwR7FbgWqzo\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04477-3738-7f01-a915-6881b4cdb38c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_two_operator_limit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_l52TnuME2GdAXgBAq6wU7AGN\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04377-4bdd-7033-8198-8243e66bccc0\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/random_operator_limit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1397\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving forward pass scaling for 3-layer network**\",\"**Deriving forward pass scaling for 3-layer network**\",\"**Deriving normalized gradient mobilities and dynamics**\",\"**Deriving forward pass scaling for 3-layer network**\",\"**Deriving normalized gradient mobilities and dynamics**\",\"**Deriving forward pass scaling for 3-layer network**\",\"**Deriving normalized gradient mobilities and dynamics**\",\"**Proving nonzero gradients and positive covariance structure**\",\"**Deriving forward pass scaling for 3-layer network**\",\"**Deriving normalized gradient mobilities and dynamics**\",\"**Proving nonzero gradients and positive covariance structure**\",\"**Verifying symmetric block matrix properties**\",\"**Verifying symmetric block matrix properties**\",\"**Analyzing query output dependencies and stability risks**\",\"**Verifying symmetric block matrix properties**\",\"**Analyzing query output dependencies and stability risks**\",\"**Designing cutoff scheme for stability in responses**\",\"**Verifying symmetric block matrix properties**\",\"**Analyzing query output dependencies and stability risks**\",\"**Designing cutoff scheme for stability in responses**\",\"**Formulating uniform tail lemma for two-operator response**\",\"**Verifying symmetric block matrix properties**\",\"**Analyzing query output dependencies and stability risks**\",\"**Designing cutoff scheme for stability in responses**\",\"**Formulating uniform tail lemma for two-operator response**\",\"**Verifying symmetric block matrix properties**\",\"**Analyzing query output dependencies and stability risks**\",\"**Designing cutoff scheme for stability in responses**\",\"**Formulating uniform tail lemma for two-operator response**\",\"**Defining recursive constants for response stability lemma**\",\"**Verifying symmetric block matrix properties**\",\"**Analyzing query output dependencies and stability risks**\",\"**Designing cutoff scheme for stability in responses**\",\"**Formulating uniform tail lemma for two-operator response**\",\"**Defining recursive constants for response stability lemma**\",\"**Outlining symmetric GFOM state evolution and dynamics**\",\"**Verifying symmetric block matrix properties**\",\"**Analyzing query output dependencies and stability risks**\",\"**Designing cutoff scheme for stability in responses**\",\"**Formulating uniform tail lemma for two-operator response**\",\"**Defining recursive constants for response stability lemma**\",\"**Outlining symmetric GFOM state evolution and dynamics**\",\"**Analyzing asymmetric matrix mapping properties**\",\"**Analyzing asymmetric matrix mapping properties**\",\"**Deriving combined operator and update scaling**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_qysLgwZqR2VL6Ih1IypTQ3Ud\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04377-4bdd-7033-8198-8243e66bccc0\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/random_operator_limit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_jGYHYEzSwuHFRKPVuZcmqQTf\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04477-3738-7f01-a915-6881b4cdb38c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_two_operator_limit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_rgO2ChbSjNkX4zDDZgyuX8FY\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04477-239c-7a20-98db-ae6e84be4d9b\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_scaling_activity\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1398\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving combined operator dynamics**\",\"**Deriving combined operator dynamics**\",\"**Formulating operator update memory overlaps**\",\"**Deriving combined operator dynamics**\",\"**Formulating operator update memory overlaps**\",\"**Deriving combined operator dynamics**\",\"**Formulating operator update memory overlaps**\",\"**Analyzing response means and innovation variances**\",\"**Defining rigorous constants and bounds**\",\"**Defining rigorous constants and bounds**\",\"**Analyzing Lipschitz coefficients and cutoff impact**\",\"**Defining rigorous constants and bounds**\",\"**Analyzing Lipschitz coefficients and cutoff impact**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1399\",\"text\":\"The \\\\(L=3\\\\) scaling is already pinned down. With three hidden preactivations \\\\(U,S,T\\\\), the viable normalization is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(W^{(1)})=d^{-1},\\\\quad\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(B)=\\\\operatorname{Var}(A)=n^{-1},\\\\quad\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(c)=n^{-4},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand mobilities\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\lambda_1,\\\\lambda_B,\\\\lambda_A,\\\\lambda_c)\\n=\\n(n/d,1,1,n^{-1}).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe main structural simplification is that the two independent reused matrices can be embedded into one rectangular Gaussian operator\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_0=[\\\\,B_0,\\\\ A_0^\\\\top\\\\,]:\\n\\\\mathcal H_1\\\\oplus\\\\mathcal H_3\\\\to\\\\mathcal H_2.\\n\\\\]\\nOne forward/adjoint query pair produces \\\\(S\\\\to T\\\\), and a second produces \\\\(P_2\\\\to P_1\\\\). This makes the fixed-mesh dynamics an exact asymmetric GFOM rather than requiring a new two-matrix theorem. The audits are now concentrated on the new nested response-tail estimate, where \\\\(D_2=\\\\phi'(S)P_2\\\\) is unbounded before cutoff.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1400\",\"summary\":[\"**Formalizing response lemma with bounded derivatives**\",\"**Analyzing bootstrap radius and beta dependencies**\",\"**Analyzing stage2 beta matrix block effects**\",\"**Detailing inequality constants and operator bounds**\",\"**Detailing inequality constants and operator bounds**\",\"**Verifying covariance positivity and SPD conditions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_Uu7wcqgnj6snkbeIoV5IDFc9\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04477-239c-7a20-98db-ae6e84be4d9b\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_scaling_activity\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_BXsGTuzfdW3LfmQuHpnAhVdB\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04477-3738-7f01-a915-6881b4cdb38c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_two_operator_limit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1401\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing response inequality and memory coefficients**\",\"**Verifying subgaussian bounds and tail estimates**\",\"**Analyzing response inequality and memory coefficients**\",\"**Verifying subgaussian bounds and tail estimates**\",\"**Analyzing response inequality and memory coefficients**\",\"**Verifying subgaussian bounds and tail estimates**\",\"**Detailing gradient scaling and memory overlaps**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04477-7788-7362-913d-94adbf569f09\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04377-4bdd-7033-8198-8243e66bccc0\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/random_operator_limit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04477-26b8-7360-aebb-0e3b6bbad601\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04477-239c-7a20-98db-ae6e84be4d9b\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_scaling_activity\"},{\"type\":\"contextCompaction\",\"id\":\"item-1402\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1403\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning rigorous theorem verification**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1404\",\"text\":\"I’m using the rigorous-math workflow here. The \\\\(L=3\\\\) extension has one genuinely new bridge: two independently reused Gaussian connectors must be reduced to a theorem that actually covers the resulting adaptive forward/adjoint queries. I’m checking that bridge from the primary theorem text and separately auditing the scaling, continuous-time passage, and all three activity claims.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_9TWWmLutX3P6ac1vfs9Iizcq\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04477-3738-7f01-a915-6881b4cdb38c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_two_operator_limit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_ko9NrtBPOWrF8y1BkpfN91gC\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0447f-263e-74b1-88bf-cf8112b2d336\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_final_adversary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1405\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing boundedness requirements for maps**\",\"**Assessing boundedness requirements for maps**\",\"**Verifying initial variance scaling and update dynamics**\",\"**Assessing boundedness requirements for maps**\",\"**Verifying initial variance scaling and update dynamics**\",\"**Tracing gradient flow derivatives and scaling consistency**\",\"**Assessing boundedness requirements for maps**\",\"**Verifying initial variance scaling and update dynamics**\",\"**Tracing gradient flow derivatives and scaling consistency**\",\"**Establishing uniform operator norm bounds for gradient flow**\",\"**Assessing boundedness requirements for maps**\",\"**Verifying initial variance scaling and update dynamics**\",\"**Tracing gradient flow derivatives and scaling consistency**\",\"**Establishing uniform operator norm bounds for gradient flow**\",\"**Assessing boundedness requirements for maps**\",\"**Verifying initial variance scaling and update dynamics**\",\"**Tracing gradient flow derivatives and scaling consistency**\",\"**Establishing uniform operator norm bounds for gradient flow**\",\"**Deriving small-time energy expansions for parameter blocks**\",\"**Assessing boundedness requirements for maps**\",\"**Verifying initial variance scaling and update dynamics**\",\"**Tracing gradient flow derivatives and scaling consistency**\",\"**Establishing uniform operator norm bounds for gradient flow**\",\"**Deriving small-time energy expansions for parameter blocks**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04477-3a60-7e22-8ed2-6437d6bbe5bf\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04477-3738-7f01-a915-6881b4cdb38c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/l3_two_operator_limit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1406\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing boundedness requirements for maps**\",\"**Verifying initial variance scaling and update dynamics**\",\"**Tracing gradient flow derivatives and scaling consistency**\",\"**Establishing uniform operator norm bounds for gradient flow**\",\"**Deriving small-time energy expansions for parameter blocks**\",\"**Confirming positive parameter energy and kernel evolution**\",\"**Analyzing covariance in AMP formula**\",\"**Analyzing covariance in AMP formula**\",\"**Verifying covariance and mean decomp