*Summarizing derivative order magnitude**\",\"**Defining smooth cutoff functions and saturations**\",\"**Summarizing derivative order magnitude**\",\"**Defining smooth cutoff functions and saturations**\",\"**Estimating Jacobian norms and learning rate scaling**\",\"**Verifying uniqueness without tail assumption**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04389-7790-7c52-9d74-388a149a8dbc\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04389-746c-7bb0-bec0-d65a202bb735\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/response_weighted_norm\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04389-8eab-7492-94a4-aa08e4b83896\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a04389-8b7f-7e72-a68d-f7caebbc56e3\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/alternative_continuum_bridge\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1392\",\"summary\":[\"**Refining uniqueness proof constants**\",\"**Verifying response lemma constants**\",\"**Verifying response lemma constants**\",\"**Confirming lemma parameter independence**\",\"**Verifying response lemma constants**\",\"**Confirming lemma parameter independence**\",\"**Structuring final convergence theorem**\",\"**Verifying response lemma constants**\",\"**Confirming lemma parameter independence**\",\"**Structuring final convergence theorem**\",\"**Verifying response lemma constants**\",\"**Confirming lemma parameter independence**\",\"**Structuring final convergence theorem**\",\"**Verifying kernel gradient positivity and cavity lemma**\",\"**Verifying response lemma constants**\",\"**Confirming lemma parameter independence**\",\"**Structuring final convergence theorem**\",\"**Verifying kernel gradient positivity and cavity lemma**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1393\",\"text\":\"Yes. The statement is affirmative once “arbitrary data” means every fixed finite dataset of pairwise-distinct RMS-normalized inputs. A single fixed nonlinear activation and one scaling work for all such datasets, including antipodal inputs and arbitrarily close opposite-label pairs.\\n\\n## Theorem\\n\\nFix \\\\(p,d<\\\\infty\\\\). Let\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_1,\\\\ldots,x_p\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|x_a\\\\|^2=d,\\\\qquad x_a\\\\neq x_b\\\\ (a\\\\neq b),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand arbitrary labels \\\\(y_a\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\).\\n\\nUse\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\\\phi(u)=\\\\sin u+\\\\cos u.}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor width \\\\(n\\\\), take\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_i^a=w_i^\\\\top x_a,\\\\qquad\\nH_i^a=\\\\phi(U_i^a),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS_j^a=\\\\sum_{i=1}^n B_{ji}H_i^a,\\\\qquad\\nG_j^a=\\\\phi(S_j^a),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^n c_jG_j^a,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathcal L_n=\\\\frac1p\\\\sum_{a=1}^p(f_{n,a}-y_a)^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nInitialize all entries independently by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nw_{ir}(0)\\\\sim N(0,d^{-1}),\\\\qquad\\nB_{ji}(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-1}),\\\\qquad\\nc_j(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-4}).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nChoose\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\lambda_{1,n}=\\\\frac nd,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_{2,n}=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_{3,n}=\\\\frac1n,\\n}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand, for definiteness,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\\\eta_{0,n}=e^{-n^{10}}.}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\lambda_{\\\\ell,n}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nfor all three layers.\\n\\nThere exists \\\\(T_{\\\\mathcal D}>0\\\\), possibly depending on the fixed dataset but not on \\\\(n\\\\), such that the exact GD interpolation on every \\\\([0,T]\\\\), \\\\(0<T<T_{\\\\mathcal D}\\\\), converges in probability along the full sequence \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) to a unique, autonomous, restartable nonlinear random-operator mean-field flow. This flow satisfies the multi-sample versions of all seven requested properties.\\n\\nNo lower bound on the separation of the distinct inputs is required.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Exact normalized dynamics\\n\\nPut\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC=nc,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\langle u,v\\\\rangle_n=\\\\frac1n u^\\\\top v,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nR_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d},\\n\\\\qquad\\nD^a=C\\\\odot\\\\phi'(S^a),\\n\\\\qquad\\nP^a=B^\\\\top D^a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_a=\\\\langle C,G^a\\\\rangle_n,\\\\qquad\\ne_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\gamma=\\\\frac2p.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDirect differentiation gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_{w_i}f_a\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\phi'(U_i^a)P_i^a x_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_{B_{ji}}f_a\\n=\\n\\\\frac1nD_j^aH_i^a,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\nabla_{c_j}f_a=G_j^a.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently, the layer-scaled finite-width gradient flow is exactly\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\dot U^a\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_{b=1}^p\\nR_{ab}e_b\\\\,\\n\\\\phi'(U^b)\\\\odot P^b,\\n}\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\dot B\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_{b=1}^p\\ne_bD^b\\\\otimes_n H^b,\\n}\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\dot C\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_{b=1}^p e_bG^b,\\n}\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(u\\\\otimes_n v)z=u\\\\langle v,z\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe corresponding NTK blocks are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta^{(3)}_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\langle G^a,G^b\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta^{(2)}_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\langle H^a,H^b\\\\rangle_n\\n\\\\langle D^a,D^b\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta^{(1)}_{ab}\\n=\\nR_{ab}\\n\\\\left\\\\langle\\n\\\\phi'(U^a)P^a,\\\\,\\n\\\\phi'(U^b)P^b\\n\\\\right\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f=-\\\\frac2p\\\\Theta e,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot{\\\\mathcal L}\\n=\\n-\\\\frac4{p^2}e^\\\\top\\\\Theta e.\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese are identities at finite width.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. A priori bounds\\n\\nFor \\\\(\\\\phi=\\\\sin+\\\\cos\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{r\\\\ge0}\\\\|\\\\phi^{(r)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\sqrt2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIf initially \\\\(\\\\|C(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le1\\\\), an event whose probability tends to one, then on a sufficiently short numerical interval,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|e_a(t)|\\\\le E,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|C(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le C_*,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith deterministic numerical constants. Indeed,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|e_a|\\\\le1+\\\\sqrt2\\\\,\\\\|C\\\\|_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac d{dt}\\\\|C\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le\\n2\\\\sqrt2\\\\,\\\\max_a|e_a|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFurthermore,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\gamma\\\\sum_a\\n|e_a|\\\\|D^a\\\\|_n\\\\|H^a\\\\|_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso \\\\(\\\\|B(t)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\) stays bounded. An elementary Gaussian-net estimate gives, for example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Pr\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\|B(0)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}>20\\\\right)\\n\\\\le e^{-c n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt follows that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|P^a(t)\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|B(t)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\|D^a(t)\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le C_T,\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nuniformly in \\\\(n\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Fixed-mesh width identification\\n\\nThe sole external result used is the asymmetric GFOM state-evolution theorem in [Q. Han, *Entrywise dynamics and universality of general first order methods*, arXiv:2406.19061v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.19061).\\n\\nI checked its full statement and surrounding definitions. Its relevant specialization says:\\n\\n- \\\\(A=A_0/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with independent centered sub-Gaussian entries;\\n- the left and right dimensions are proportional;\\n- the iteration has the form\\n  \\\\[\\n  u^{(r)}\\n  =\\n  AF_r^{1}(v^{[0:r-1]})+G_r^{1}(u^{[0:r-1]}),\\n  \\\\]\\n  \\\\[\\n  v^{(r)}\\n  =\\n  A^\\\\top G_r^{2}(u^{[0:r]})+F_r^{2}(v^{[0:r-1]}),\\n  \\\\]\\n  with row-separate maps;\\n- for globally Lipschitz row maps, empirical pseudo-Lipschitz observables converge to the canonical Gaussian state evolution;\\n- no nonsingularity assumption is imposed on any Gaussian covariance.\\n\\nFor a fixed number \\\\(N\\\\) of iterations, its quantitative error is bounded by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left[\\nK\\\\Lambda\\\\Lambda_\\\\psi\\\\log n\\n\\\\bigl(1+\\\\|u^{(0)}\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\\\|v^{(0)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\bigr)\\n\\\\right]^{C_0N^5}\\nn^{-1/C_0^N},\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhich tends to zero for fixed \\\\(N\\\\).\\n\\nWe do not extrapolate (7) directly to continuous time.\\n\\n### Exact serialization\\n\\nFor a coarse Euler mesh \\\\(h\\\\), integrate (2):\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_k\\n=\\nB_0-\\\\gamma h\\n\\\\sum_{\\\\ell<k,b}\\ne_{\\\\ell b}D_{\\\\ell b}\\\\otimes_n H_{\\\\ell b}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS_{ka}\\n=\\nB_0H_{ka}\\n-\\\\gamma h\\n\\\\sum_{\\\\ell<k,b}\\ne_{\\\\ell b}D_{\\\\ell b}\\n\\\\langle H_{\\\\ell b},H_{ka}\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_{ka}\\n=\\nB_0^\\\\top D_{ka}\\n-\\\\gamma h\\n\\\\sum_{\\\\ell<k,b}\\ne_{\\\\ell b}H_{\\\\ell b}\\n\\\\langle D_{\\\\ell b},D_{ka}\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSerialize the pair \\\\((k,a)\\\\) as one asymmetric GFOM stage:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF_{ka}^{1}=H_{ka},\\\\qquad\\nG_{ka}^{1}=\\\\text{the historical term in (8)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nG_{ka}^{2}=D_{ka},\\\\qquad\\nF_{ka}^{2}=\\\\text{the historical term in (9)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHan’s causal ordering allows \\\\(D_{ka}\\\\) to depend on the current forward query \\\\(S_{ka}\\\\) before the reused \\\\(B_0^\\\\top\\\\) query.\\n\\nConditioning on the independent marks\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\{U_{0,i}^{1:p},C_{0,j}\\\\}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nmakes them deterministic coordinate parameters while preserving independence from \\\\(B_0\\\\). The theorem therefore applies conditionally and then unconditionally.\\n\\nOutputs, errors and all \\\\(H\\\\)- and \\\\(D\\\\)-overlaps are first replaced by their deterministic state-evolution values. For fixed \\\\(h\\\\), Han’s averaged theorem makes every corresponding empirical error tend to zero. If \\\\(d_k\\\\) denotes the normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\)-plus-operator distance between the actual and oracle coarse iterations, then\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd_{k+1}\\n\\\\le\\n(1+C_Rh)d_k+C_Rh\\\\,\\\\zeta_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\zeta_n\\\\to0\\\\) in probability. Thus\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{kh\\\\le T}d_k\\n\\\\le\\nC_RT e^{C_RT}\\\\zeta_n\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThere is no \\\\(\\\\zeta_n/h\\\\) term: every learned-memory occurrence carries the factor \\\\(h\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Uniform response and Gaussian-tail lemma\\n\\nIntroduce a smooth odd cutoff \\\\(\\\\tau_R\\\\) satisfying\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\tau_R(x)=x\\\\quad(|x|\\\\le R),\\\\qquad\\n|\\\\tau_R(x)|\\\\le|x|,\\\\qquad\\n|\\\\tau_R'(x)|\\\\le2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOnly \\\\(P\\\\) in (1) is replaced by \\\\(\\\\tau_R(P)\\\\).\\n\\nLet \\\\(b=2\\\\), so that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{0\\\\le r\\\\le4}\\\\|\\\\phi^{(r)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le b.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTake\\n\\n\\\\[\\nT_0=\\\\frac1{256b^2}.\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIn the exact canonical state evolution, index sources lexicographically by \\\\(q=(k,a)\\\\). It has the form\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS_q\\n=\\nZ_q+L_q+\\\\sum_{r<q}\\\\alpha_{qr}D_r,\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_q\\n=\\nY_q+M_q+\\\\sum_{r\\\\le q}\\\\beta_{qr}H_r,\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Cov}(Z_q,Z_r)=\\\\mathbb E[H_qH_r],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_q,Y_r)=\\\\mathbb E[D_qD_r],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_{qr}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\frac{\\\\partial H_q}{\\\\partial Y_r},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\beta_{qr}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\frac{\\\\partial D_q}{\\\\partial Z_r}.\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese formulas are obtained by direct substitution into Han’s state-evolution definition.\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A\\n=\\n\\\\sup_q\\\\sum_{r<q}|\\\\alpha_{qr}|,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathcal B\\n=\\n\\\\sup_q\\\\sum_{r\\\\le q}|\\\\beta_{qr}|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBecause \\\\(C_0=0\\\\) in the limiting state,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|C_k\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\nc_T:=\\\\frac{e^{2b^2T}-1}{b},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|e_{ka}|\\\\le E_T:=e^{2b^2T},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|D_{ka}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le d_T:=bc_T.\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor \\\\(T\\\\le T_0\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nE_T<2,\\\\qquad\\nc_T\\\\le4bT,\\\\qquad\\nd_T\\\\le4b^2T.\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n### Left-source derivatives\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\ns_*=\\\\sup_q\\\\sum_r\\n\\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\partial S_q}{\\\\partial Z_r}\\\\right|,\\n\\\\quad\\nc_*=\\\\sup_k\\\\sum_r\\n\\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\partial C_k}{\\\\partial Z_r}\\\\right|,\\n\\\\quad\\nd_*=\\\\sup_q\\\\sum_r\\n\\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\partial D_q}{\\\\partial Z_r}\\\\right|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe learned term in \\\\(S\\\\) has total coefficient mass at most\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\ell_H\\\\le 2TE_Tb^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDifferentiating (12), the \\\\(C\\\\)-update and \\\\(D=C\\\\phi'(S)\\\\) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\ns_*\\\\le1+(\\\\mathcal A+\\\\ell_H)d_*,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nc_*\\\\le2TE_Tb\\\\,s_*,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd_*\\n\\\\le bc_*+c_Tb\\\\,s_*\\n=\\nb(2TE_Tb+c_T)s_*.\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUnder \\\\(\\\\mathcal A\\\\le1\\\\), (16)–(17) imply\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd_*<\\\\frac1{16}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_r|\\\\beta_{qr}|\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\sum_r\\n\\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\partial D_q}{\\\\partial Z_r}\\\\right|,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwe obtain\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal B<\\\\frac1{16}.\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis includes the reused-adjoint same-source response\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial D_q}{\\\\partial Z_q}\\n=\\nC_k\\\\phi''(S_q);\\n\\\\]\\n\\nit is not discarded.\\n\\n### Right-source derivatives\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nu_k\\n=\\n\\\\max_a\\n\\\\sum_r\\n\\\\left|\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial U_k^a}{\\\\partial Y_r}\\n\\\\right|,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\overline P_k=\\\\frac1p\\\\sum_a|P_{ka}|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe learned term in \\\\(P\\\\) has coefficient mass\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\ell_D\\\\le2TE_Td_T^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDifferentiating (13) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_r\\n\\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\partial P_{ka}}{\\\\partial Y_r}\\\\right|\\n\\\\le\\n1+b(\\\\mathcal B+\\\\ell_D)u_k.\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDifferentiating the end-of-cycle \\\\(U\\\\)-update then gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nu_{k+1}\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\left[\\n1+2hE_Tb\\\\,\\\\overline P_k\\n+4hE_Tb^2(\\\\mathcal B+\\\\ell_D)\\n\\\\right]u_k\\n+4hE_Tb.\\n\\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Lambda_k\\n=\\n2E_Tb\\\\,h\\\\sum_{\\\\ell<k}\\\\overline P_\\\\ell\\n+\\n4E_Tb^2(\\\\mathcal B+\\\\ell_D)kh.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIteration of (20) yields\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{k\\\\le K}e^{-\\\\Lambda_k}u_k\\n\\\\le4E_TbT.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUnder the bootstrap (18), (13) can be written\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_q=Y_q+B_q,\\n\\\\qquad\\n|B_q|\\\\le b(\\\\mathcal B+\\\\ell_D),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(Y_q)\\\\le d_T^2.\\n\\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor every \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\ge0\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E e^{\\\\lambda|P_q|}\\n\\\\le\\n2\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left[\\n\\\\lambda b(\\\\mathcal B+\\\\ell_D)\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\lambda^2d_T^2}{2}\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nCorrelation-free Jensen gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\n\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\n2E_Tb\\\\,h\\\\sum_{k<K}\\\\overline P_k\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\sup_q\\\\mathbb E e^{2E_TbT|P_q|}.\\n\\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nCombining (21)–(24),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A\\n\\\\le\\n4E_Tb^2T\\ne^{4E_Tb^2(\\\\mathcal B+\\\\ell_D)T}\\n\\\\sup_q\\\\mathbb E e^{2E_TbT|P_q|}\\n<\\\\frac18\\n\\\\]\\n\\nfor \\\\(T\\\\le T_0\\\\). A first-exit argument closes the simultaneous bootstrap.\\n\\nTherefore, uniformly in the mesh, cutoff, number of steps and covariance rank,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\sup_q\\\\|P_q\\\\|_{\\\\psi_2}\\\\le4b.\\n}\\n\\\\tag{25}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIn particular,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\nP_q^2\\\\mathbf1_{\\\\{|P_q|>r\\\\}}\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\le C_b e^{-c_b r^2}.\\n\\\\tag{26}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nNo covariance inverse, Price derivative or covariance lower bound was used. Singular Gaussian covariances are allowed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Continuous time and the current operator state\\n\\nFor a fixed cutoff, enumerate all finite typed probe expressions generated from\\n\\n- current right marks \\\\(U^1,\\\\ldots,U^p\\\\);\\n- current left mark \\\\(C\\\\);\\n- \\\\(B\\\\) and \\\\(B^*\\\\);\\n- bounded smooth coordinate maps;\\n- normalized inner products.\\n\\nThe limiting empirical inner products define positive semidefinite forms on the right and left probe spans. Quotient their null spaces and complete them to Hilbert spaces\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal H_R,\\\\qquad \\\\mathcal H_L.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe finite-width identities\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle Bv,u\\\\rangle_n=\\\\langle v,B^*u\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|Bv\\\\|_n\\\\le M_T\\\\|v\\\\|_n\\n\\\\]\\n\\npass to the limit. Hence the limiting state contains a genuine bounded operator\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal B:\\\\mathcal H_R\\\\to\\\\mathcal H_L\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand its genuine adjoint.\\n\\nThe current state is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathfrak S(t)\\n=\\n\\\\bigl(U^1(t),\\\\ldots,U^p(t),C(t),\\\\mathcal B(t)\\\\bigr).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIts equations are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS^a=\\\\mathcal B\\\\phi(U^a),\\\\qquad\\nD^a=C\\\\phi'(S^a),\\\\qquad\\nP^a=\\\\mathcal B^*D^a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\ntogether with (1)–(3), replacing \\\\(B\\\\) by \\\\(\\\\mathcal B\\\\).\\n\\nNo \\\\(B_0\\\\), finite array, response history or externally prescribed trajectory remains in this state.\\n\\nFor fixed \\\\(R\\\\), use a smooth saturation of \\\\(C\\\\) outside the a priori bound (15); the saturation is inactive on the actual trajectory. On the invariant class \\\\(\\\\|C\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le c_T\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\delta H\\\\|_2\\\\le b\\\\|\\\\delta U\\\\|_2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\delta S\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\nb\\\\|\\\\delta\\\\mathcal B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n+M_Tb\\\\|\\\\delta U\\\\|_2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\delta D\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\nb\\\\|\\\\delta C\\\\|_2+c_Tb\\\\|\\\\delta S\\\\|_2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\delta P\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\nd_T\\\\|\\\\delta\\\\mathcal B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n+M_T\\\\|\\\\delta D\\\\|_2.\\n\\\\tag{27}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus two cutoff Euler schemes satisfy\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d(\\\\mathfrak S_R^h(t),\\\\mathfrak S_R^{h'}(t))\\n\\\\le\\nC_R(h+h').\\n\\\\tag{28}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is a direct Euler-Cauchy construction; it does not incorrectly invoke Picard–Lindelöf on an open \\\\(L^2\\\\) ball.\\n\\nRun the two meshes on the same finite-width initialization, apply (28), and only then take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) using the fixed-step theorem. The limiting fixed-mesh action laws are Cauchy as \\\\(h\\\\downarrow0\\\\). This produces the unique fixed-cutoff continuous action flow.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Removing the cutoff and uniqueness\\n\\nLet \\\\(X_n\\\\) be the uncut finite-width ODE and \\\\(X_{n,R}\\\\) its cutoff version. With\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd_{n,R}\\n=\\n\\\\sum_a\\\\|U_n^a-U_{n,R}^a\\\\|_n\\n+\\\\|C_n-C_{n,R}\\\\|_n\\n+\\\\|B_n-B_{n,R}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nuse the exact decomposition\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\phi'(U)P-\\\\phi'(U_R)\\\\tau_R(P_R)\\n={}&\\n\\\\phi'(U)(P-P_R)\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\phi'(U)\\\\bigl(P_R-\\\\tau_R(P_R)\\\\bigr)\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\bigl(\\\\phi'(U)-\\\\phi'(U_R)\\\\bigr)\\\\tau_R(P_R).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEquations (27) and \\\\(|\\\\tau_R(P_R)|\\\\le2R\\\\) give\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd_{n,R}'(t)\\n\\\\le\\nC(1+R)d_{n,R}(t)\\n+\\nC\\\\|P_{n,R}-\\\\tau_R(P_{n,R})\\\\|_n.\\n\\\\tag{29}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe source in (29) involves only the cutoff process.\\n\\nFor fixed \\\\(R\\\\), a smooth pseudo-Lipschitz majorant of\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx^2\\\\mathbf1_{\\\\{|x|>R\\\\}}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the fixed-mesh identification transfer (26) to the cutoff flow. Consequently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\limsup_{n\\\\to\\\\infty}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\n\\\\int_0^T\\n\\\\|P_{n,R}-\\\\tau_R(P_{n,R})\\\\|_n^2\\\\,dt\\n\\\\le\\nC_Te^{-cR^2}.\\n\\\\tag{30}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nGronwall gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\limsup_{n\\\\to\\\\infty}\\nd_{n,R}(T)\\n\\\\le\\nC_Te^{CRT}e^{-cR^2/2}\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{31}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe quadratic Gaussian tail beats the linear exponential in \\\\(R\\\\).\\n\\nThe same comparison proves that two cutoff limits with thresholds \\\\(R<Q\\\\) are Cauchy. Their limit solves the uncut equations.\\n\\nIt also proves uniqueness without assuming tails for an arbitrary competing solution: compare each uncut solution with the same cutoff solution using (29), then let \\\\(R\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Thus the current-state flow is well posed and restartable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Exact GD, jointly with width\\n\\nIn normalized variables, exact GD is Euler discretization of the uncut finite-width ODE with step \\\\(\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\).\\n\\nOn the bounded region above, direct differentiation of the finite-dimensional vector field gives crude bounds\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|DF_n\\\\|\\\\le A_Tn^4,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|DF_n\\\\,F_n\\\\|\\\\le A_Tn^8.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDiscrete Grönwall therefore gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{k\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\le T}\\nd\\\\bigl(X^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}_{n,k},X_n(k\\\\eta_{0,n})\\\\bigr)\\n\\\\le\\nA_Tn^8\\\\eta_{0,n}e^{A_Tn^4T}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWith \\\\(\\\\eta_{0,n}=e^{-n^{10}}\\\\), the right-hand side tends to zero.\\n\\nTherefore this is a genuine diagonal limit:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(n,\\\\eta_{0,n})\\\\longrightarrow(\\\\infty,0),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nnot a finite-step expansion and not an unproved interchange of a width limit with a subsequent learning-rate limit.\\n\\nThe auxiliary mesh \\\\(h\\\\) was only a proof device for identifying the finite-width ODE.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Why every distinct dataset is nondegenerate\\n\\nAt initialization,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU=(U^1,\\\\ldots,U^p)\\\\sim N(0,R).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor jointly standard Gaussian \\\\(X,Y\\\\) with correlation \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(X)\\\\phi(Y)]\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi'(X)\\\\phi'(Y)]\\n=\\ne^{-1+\\\\rho}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{ab}\\n=\\ne^{-1+R_{ab}}\\n=\\n\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\n-\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a-x_b\\\\|^2}{2d}\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{32}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor every nonzero \\\\(v\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nv^\\\\top Qv\\n=\\nc_d\\n\\\\int_{\\\\mathbb R^d}\\n\\\\left|\\n\\\\sum_a v_ae^{i\\\\omega^\\\\top x_a}\\n\\\\right|^2\\ne^{-d\\\\|\\\\omega\\\\|^2/2}\\\\,d\\\\omega\\n>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe final inequality follows because an exponential polynomial supported on distinct points cannot vanish identically. Thus \\\\(Q\\\\) is strictly positive definite for every distinct dataset.\\n\\nThe initial second-layer vector is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS\\\\sim N(0,Q).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIts output Gram is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nT_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(S_a)\\\\phi(S_b)]\\n=\\ne^{-1+Q_{ab}}.\\n\\\\tag{33}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince \\\\(Q\\\\) is a Gram matrix of distinct unit feature vectors, (33) is another Gaussian RBF Gram and is strictly positive definite.\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top Ty>0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nfor every nonzero label vector, including arbitrarily close opposite-label pairs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Feature learning and kernel motion\\n\\nAt initialization, \\\\(C(0)=0\\\\) in the limit and \\\\(e(0)=-y\\\\). Define\\n\\n\\\\[\\ng(S)=\\\\sum_{a=1}^p y_a\\\\phi(S_a),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Psi=\\\\frac12\\\\mathbb E[g(S)^2].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC(t)=\\\\gamma t\\\\,g(S)+O_{L^2}(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{34}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\nV_a(S)=y_ag(S)\\\\phi'(S_a),\\n\\\\qquad\\nM_{ab}=\\\\mathbb E[V_a(S)V_b(S)].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe matrix \\\\(M\\\\) is strictly positive definite. Indeed, if \\\\(v^\\\\top Mv=0\\\\), then\\n\\n\\\\[\\ng(s)\\\\sum_a v_ay_a\\\\phi'(s_a)=0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nfor almost every \\\\(s\\\\). Since \\\\(S\\\\) has a full-support Gaussian law and the expression is analytic, it vanishes everywhere. The function \\\\(g\\\\) is not identically zero, so\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_a v_ay_a\\\\phi'(s_a)\\\\equiv0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDifferentiating in \\\\(s_c\\\\) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nv_cy_c\\\\phi''(s_c)\\\\equiv0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBecause \\\\(\\\\phi''=-\\\\phi\\\\not\\\\equiv0\\\\), \\\\(v_c=0\\\\) for every \\\\(c\\\\).\\n\\n### Reused-adjoint innovation\\n\\nDeleting one column of \\\\(B_0\\\\), Taylor expanding \\\\(V(S)\\\\), and applying the Gaussian triangular-array CLT gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_0^*V\\n=\\n\\\\xi+\\\\sum_cH_c\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[\\\\partial_{S_c}V]+o_{L^2}(1),\\n\\\\tag{35}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\xi\\\\sim N(0,M)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis independent of the representative right mark \\\\(U\\\\).\\n\\nThe first term in (35) comes from the deleted-column-independent summands. The second comes from\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_jB_{ji}^2\\\\longrightarrow1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe Taylor remainder vanishes because the deleted-column contribution is \\\\(O(n^{-1/2})\\\\) and all activation derivatives are bounded.\\n\\nSince \\\\(M>0\\\\), the independent innovation in (35) proves that the first-layer gradient of \\\\(\\\\Psi\\\\) is nonzero even if \\\\(R\\\\) is singular.\\n\\nFor the middle layer,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\nabla_B\\\\Psi\\\\|^2\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{a,b}Q_{ab}M_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbf1^\\\\top(Q\\\\circ M)\\\\mathbf1>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nbecause the Schur product of two strictly positive definite matrices is strictly positive definite.\\n\\nLet \\\\(h=(w,B)\\\\) denote both hidden parameter blocks with their mobility metric. From (34),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot h(t)\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2t\\\\,M_h\\\\nabla_h\\\\Psi+O(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand therefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh(t)\\n=\\nh(0)+\\\\frac{\\\\gamma^2t^2}{2}\\nM_h\\\\nabla_h\\\\Psi+O(t^3).\\n\\\\tag{36}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBoth the \\\\(w\\\\)- and \\\\(B\\\\)-components of \\\\(\\\\nabla_h\\\\Psi\\\\) are nonzero.\\n\\nFor the second preactivation map \\\\(S=S(h)\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\langle\\n\\\\nabla_S\\\\Psi,\\\\,\\n\\\\lim_{t\\\\downarrow0}\\\\frac{\\\\dot S(t)}t\\n\\\\right\\\\rangle\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2\\n\\\\|M_h^{1/2}\\\\nabla_h\\\\Psi\\\\|^2>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus neither hidden preactivation velocity vanishes.\\n\\nConsequently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot U(t)=t\\\\,V^{(1)}_*+o_{L^2}(t),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot S(t)=t\\\\,V^{(2)}_*+o_{L^2}(t),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|V^{(1)}_*\\\\|_2>0,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|V^{(2)}_*\\\\|_2>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence, for every sufficiently small \\\\(T>0\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n0<\\n\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\dot U(t)\\\\|_2^2\\\\,dt\\n<\\\\infty,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n0<\\n\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\dot S(t)\\\\|_2^2\\\\,dt\\n<\\\\infty,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand both hidden layers have nonzero macroscopic displacement.\\n\\n### Every NTK block is active\\n\\nAt \\\\(t=0\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta^{(1)}(0)=\\\\Theta^{(2)}(0)=0,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Theta^{(3)}(0)=T>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFrom (34)–(36),\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta^{(1)}(t)y\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2t^2\\n\\\\|M_w^{1/2}\\\\nabla_w\\\\Psi\\\\|^2\\n+o(t^2)>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta^{(2)}(t)y\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2t^2\\n\\\\|M_B^{1/2}\\\\nabla_B\\\\Psi\\\\|^2\\n+o(t^2)>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus every PSD block has positive trace and positive time integral.\\n\\nMoreover,\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta^{(3)}(t)y\\n=\\n2\\\\Psi(h(t))\\n=\\n2\\\\Psi(h_0)\\n+\\n\\\\gamma^2t^2\\n\\\\|M_h^{1/2}\\\\nabla_h\\\\Psi\\\\|^2\\n+o(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\ny^\\\\top[\\\\Theta(t)-\\\\Theta(0)]y\\n=\\n2\\\\gamma^2t^2\\n\\\\|M_h^{1/2}\\\\nabla_h\\\\Psi\\\\|^2\\n+o(t^2)>0.\\n}\\n\\\\tag{37}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe limiting NTK is therefore genuinely nonconstant.\\n\\nFinally,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot{\\\\mathcal L}(0)\\n=\\n-\\\\frac4{p^2}y^\\\\top Ty<0.\\n\\\\tag{38}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus \\\\(\\\\mathcal L(T)<\\\\mathcal L(0)\\\\) for every sufficiently small \\\\(T>0\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Hidden laws and surviving nonlinearity\\n\\nDefine the joint path laws\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\overline\\\\mu_n^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\n\\\\delta_{(U_i^1(\\\\cdot),\\\\ldots,U_i^p(\\\\cdot))},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\overline\\\\mu_n^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_j\\n\\\\delta_{(S_j^1(\\\\cdot),\\\\ldots,S_j^p(\\\\cdot))}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe operator-action convergence, velocity bounds and\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\n\\\\sup_{s,t\\\\in I}|U_i(t)-U_i(s)|^2\\n\\\\le\\n|I|\\\\int_I V_n^{(1)}(t)^2\\\\,dt\\n\\\\]\\n\\ngive convergence in \\\\(W_2(C([0,T];\\\\mathbb R^p))\\\\); the same argument applies to \\\\(S\\\\). The Gaussian-tail bound for \\\\(P\\\\) supplies the uniform integrability needed for kernels and squared velocities.\\n\\nInitially every sample marginal of both \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(S\\\\) is \\\\(N(0,1)\\\\). Their variances therefore remain strictly positive for sufficiently small time.\\n\\nFor a nondegenerate Gaussian \\\\(Z\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\inf_{a,b}\\n\\\\mathbb E|\\\\phi(Z)-aZ-b|^2>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nbecause equality would make the analytic function \\\\(\\\\sin z+\\\\cos z\\\\) affine everywhere. The regression residual depends continuously on\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb EZ,\\\\quad\\n\\\\mathbb EZ^2,\\\\quad\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\phi(Z),\\\\quad\\n\\\\mathbb EZ\\\\phi(Z),\\\\quad\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\phi(Z)^2\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhile the variance stays bounded below. Hence the nonlinear residual remains positive in both hidden layers on a short interval.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Resolution and audit verdict\\n\\nThe distinct-input version of the conjecture is true.\\n\\nThe construction works for every fixed finite pairwise-distinct RMS-normalized dataset and every \\\\(\\\\{\\\\pm1\\\\}\\\\)-labeling. Arbitrarily close opposite-label inputs merely make some positive activity constants small; they do not destroy the limit.\\n\\nThe adversarial audits specifically checked:\\n\\n- singular input and Gaussian covariances;\\n- reused \\\\(B\\\\) and \\\\(B^\\\\top\\\\), including the same-query response;\\n- adaptive empirical outputs and overlaps;\\n- fixed-mesh versus continuous-time logic;\\n- the \\\\(L^\\\\infty\\\\)-control of \\\\(C\\\\) inside an \\\\(L^2\\\\) state topology;\\n- cutoff-uniform response bounds;\\n- cutoff removal for the actual uncut finite-width ODE;\\n- exact-GD diagonal error;\\n- autonomy and restartability of the current operator state;\\n- positivity of both hidden feature velocities and all three NTK blocks.\\n\\nNo project files were read.\\n\\nGoal run completed in about 3 hours.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a04235-fa47-7792-8849-d2dbea295417\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1787817228,\"completedAt\":1787818133,\"durationMs\":904261,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1169\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"extend this result to having multiple samples with arbitrary configuration (input and labels) ? try very hard until you can resolve this completely (to true or false). Meaning, the same conjecture as before for L=2 an existence of any nonlinear configuration that has a joint limit with gradient flow mean field limit that has feature learning and non-lazy NTK and a true nonlinear activation, but instead with arbitrary configuration of input and arbirtrary +1 or -1 labels. you either have to construct a case that passes this, or you prove that no such configuration exists , some limitationon arbitrary is that the inputs are RMS normalized to 1 (each input vector), and labrels are also let's say +1 or -1, [$solve-math-rigorously](/etc/codex/skills/solve-math-rigorously/SKILL.md) to make sure the proof is fully audited and verified indepdnently and adversarilly\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1170\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing impact of duplicate inputs on feature learning**\",\"**Evaluating nonzero initialization enabling transient feature learning**\",\"**Assessing impact of duplicate inputs on feature learning**\",\"**Evaluating nonzero initialization enabling transient feature learning**\",\"**Analyzing initialization scaling effects on output randomness**\",\"**Assessing impact of duplicate inputs on feature learning**\",\"**Evaluating nonzero initialization enabling transient feature learning**\",\"**Analyzing initialization scaling effects on output randomness**\",\"**Assessing impact of duplicate inputs on feature learning**\",\"**Evaluating nonzero initialization enabling transient feature learning**\",\"**Analyzing initialization scaling effects on output randomness**\",\"**Proving deterministic output convergence excludes feature learning**\",\"**Assessing impact of duplicate inputs on feature learning**\",\"**Evaluating nonzero initialization enabling transient feature learning**\",\"**Analyzing initialization scaling effects on output randomness**\",\"**Proving deterministic output convergence excludes feature learning**\",\"**Identifying duplicates as fundamental universal obstruction**\",\"**Assessing impact of duplicate inputs on feature learning**\",\"**Evaluating nonzero initialization enabling transient feature learning**\",\"**Analyzing initialization scaling effects on output randomness**\",\"**Proving deterministic output convergence excludes feature learning**\",\"**Identifying duplicates as fundamental universal obstruction**\",\"**Formalizing output variance constraints for deterministic convergence**\",\"**Assessing impact of duplicate inputs on feature learning**\",\"**Evaluating nonzero initialization enabling transient feature learning**\",\"**Analyzing initialization scaling effects on output randomness**\",\"**Proving deterministic output convergence excludes feature learning**\",\"**Identifying duplicates as fundamental universal obstruction**\",\"**Formalizing output variance constraints for deterministic convergence**\",\"**Assessing impact of duplicate inputs on feature learning**\",\"**Evaluating nonzero initialization enabling transient feature learning**\",\"**Analyzing initialization scaling effects on output randomness**\",\"**Proving deterministic output convergence excludes feature learning**\",\"**Identifying duplicates as fundamental universal obstruction**\",\"**Formalizing output variance constraints for deterministic convergence**\",\"**Confirming vanishing residuals preclude true feature motion**\",\"**Assessing impact of duplicate inputs on feature learning**\",\"**Evaluating nonzero initialization enabling transient feature learning**\",\"**Analyzing initialization scaling effects on output randomness**\",\"**Proving deterministic output convergence excludes feature learning**\",\"**Identifying duplicates as fundamental universal obstruction**\",\"**Formalizing output variance constraints for deterministic convergence**\",\"**Confirming vanishing residuals preclude true feature motion**\",\"**Examining coupling feasibility for random initial outputs**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1171\",\"text\":\"With the clarified universal quantifier, the central candidate counterexample is no longer the zero input: take the same RMS-normalized input twice with labels \\\\(+1\\\\) and \\\\(-1\\\\). I’m now stress-testing whether any admissible output scaling, random initial-output limit, or diverging layer mobility can evade that cancellation while still qualifying as the same autonomous mean-field gradient flow rather than a hidden tangent-mark limit.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_mjtTCrcAZjxbQkXcokce9l2x\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_liD27Gk34iEAjUTkU3ZsH9le\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-8946-7371-a674-18ae21e4402e\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/tp_scope_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_N9XSXhZcS8BS69vNta5RhVBG\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-a4af-7b22-8235-af93474c086c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/uniform_lift_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1172\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving gradient flow dynamics**\",\"**Deriving gradient flow dynamics**\",\"**Confirming stationary limit at zero output**\",\"**Deriving gradient flow dynamics**\",\"**Confirming stationary limit at zero output**\",\"**Analyzing kernel mobility and convergence assumptions**\",\"**Deriving gradient flow dynamics**\",\"**Confirming stationary limit at zero output**\",\"**Analyzing kernel mobility and convergence assumptions**\",\"**Formulating universal negative theorem assumptions**\",\"**Confirming loss decrease failure with finite kernel**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04237-bf0c-7013-a513-f2ebf8807391\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04237-e4ee-7a52-bc81-d5d5eb78f9fe\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-a4af-7b22-8235-af93474c086c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/uniform_lift_audit\"},{\"type\":\"contextCompaction\",\"id\":\"item-1173\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1174\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing convergence under arbitrary configurations**\",\"**Evaluating broader counterexample constraints**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1175\",\"text\":\"I’m using the rigorous-math skill and three independent audits here. I’ll keep this self-contained and will not inspect any project files. The key issue I’m pressure-testing is the quantifier “arbitrary”: a single admissible labeled configuration can refute the universal claim, but only if the counterexample also survives possible random-output and singular-scaling loopholes.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_ch9yVp9w7FownnRAn9bXlxrl\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_7dmsui6cbTSGL4YxFV4lJTvb\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-8946-7371-a674-18ae21e4402e\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/tp_scope_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_2634z5y79Xkmbwr5R8rMNOog\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-a4af-7b22-8235-af93474c086c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/uniform_lift_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04237-d5e8-7d70-a2f6-80729a9dd318\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-8946-7371-a674-18ae21e4402e\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/tp_scope_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1176\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying convergence requirements in nonlinear configurations**\",\"**Assessing universal impossibility with random initialization**\",\"**Verifying joint distribution independence conditions**\",\"**Analyzing dataset symmetries for zero gradient**\",\"**Verifying joint distribution independence conditions**\",\"**Analyzing dataset symmetries for zero gradient**\",\"**Evaluating activation symmetries for gradient cancellation**\",\"**Verifying joint distribution independence conditions**\",\"**Analyzing dataset symmetries for zero gradient**\",\"**Evaluating activation symmetries for gradient cancellation**\",\"**Clarifying deterministic mean-field law-level convergence**\",\"**Verifying joint distribution independence conditions**\",\"**Analyzing dataset symmetries for zero gradient**\",\"**Evaluating activation symmetries for gradient cancellation**\",\"**Clarifying deterministic mean-field law-level convergence**\",\"**Confirming negative universal statement with duplicates**\",\"**Verifying joint distribution independence conditions**\",\"**Analyzing dataset symmetries for zero gradient**\",\"**Evaluating activation symmetries for gradient cancellation**\",\"**Clarifying deterministic mean-field law-level convergence**\",\"**Confirming negative universal statement with duplicates**\",\"**Verifying joint distribution independence conditions**\",\"**Analyzing dataset symmetries for zero gradient**\",\"**Evaluating activation symmetries for gradient cancellation**\",\"**Clarifying deterministic mean-field law-level convergence**\",\"**Confirming negative universal statement with duplicates**\",\"**Deriving multi-sample gradient flow dynamics and kernel structure**\",\"**Verifying joint distribution independence conditions**\",\"**Analyzing dataset symmetries for zero gradient**\",\"**Evaluating activation symmetries for gradient cancellation**\",\"**Clarifying deterministic mean-field law-level convergence**\",\"**Confirming negative universal statement with duplicates**\",\"**Deriving multi-sample gradient flow dynamics and kernel structure**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1177\",\"text\":\"The first decisive case is al