",\"**Analyzing gradient flow and initialization convergence properties**\",\"**Formulating strong no-go theorem for balanced duplicates**\",\"**Detailing gradient norm limit with concentration via spectral bounds**\",\"**Planning rigorous backpropagation concentration proof**\",\"**Deriving layerwise variance and moment limits**\",\"**Establishing gradient norm concentration and variance limits**\",\"**Analyzing gradient flow and initialization convergence properties**\",\"**Formulating strong no-go theorem for balanced duplicates**\",\"**Detailing gradient norm limit with concentration via spectral bounds**\",\"**Proving spectral norm bound and validating random regime scaling**\",\"**Planning rigorous backpropagation concentration proof**\",\"**Deriving layerwise variance and moment limits**\",\"**Establishing gradient norm concentration and variance limits**\",\"**Analyzing gradient flow and initialization convergence properties**\",\"**Formulating strong no-go theorem for balanced duplicates**\",\"**Detailing gradient norm limit with concentration via spectral bounds**\",\"**Proving spectral norm bound and validating random regime scaling**\",\"**Formulating rigorous theorem for gradient flow contradiction**\",\"**Planning rigorous backpropagation concentration proof**\",\"**Deriving layerwise variance and moment limits**\",\"**Establishing gradient norm concentration and variance limits**\",\"**Analyzing gradient flow and initialization convergence properties**\",\"**Formulating strong no-go theorem for balanced duplicates**\",\"**Detailing gradient norm limit with concentration via spectral bounds**\",\"**Proving spectral norm bound and validating random regime scaling**\",\"**Formulating rigorous theorem for gradient flow contradiction**\",\"**Planning rigorous backpropagation concentration proof**\",\"**Deriving layerwise variance and moment limits**\",\"**Establishing gradient norm concentration and variance limits**\",\"**Analyzing gradient flow and initialization convergence properties**\",\"**Formulating strong no-go theorem for balanced duplicates**\",\"**Detailing gradient norm limit with concentration via spectral bounds**\",\"**Proving spectral norm bound and validating random regime scaling**\",\"**Formulating rigorous theorem for gradient flow contradiction**\",\"**Planning rigorous backpropagation concentration proof**\",\"**Deriving layerwise variance and moment limits**\",\"**Establishing gradient norm concentration and variance limits**\",\"**Analyzing gradient flow and initialization convergence properties**\",\"**Formulating strong no-go theorem for balanced duplicates**\",\"**Detailing gradient norm limit with concentration via spectral bounds**\",\"**Proving spectral norm bound and validating random regime scaling**\",\"**Formulating rigorous theorem for gradient flow contradiction**\",\"**Proving initialization moment convergence and gradient norm limit**\",\"**Planning rigorous backpropagation concentration proof**\",\"**Deriving layerwise variance and moment limits**\",\"**Establishing gradient norm concentration and variance limits**\",\"**Analyzing gradient flow and initialization convergence properties**\",\"**Formulating strong no-go theorem for balanced duplicates**\",\"**Detailing gradient norm limit with concentration via spectral bounds**\",\"**Proving spectral norm bound and validating random regime scaling**\",\"**Formulating rigorous theorem for gradient flow contradiction**\",\"**Proving initialization moment convergence and gradient norm limit**\",\"**Planning rigorous backpropagation concentration proof**\",\"**Deriving layerwise variance and moment limits**\",\"**Establishing gradient norm concentration and variance limits**\",\"**Analyzing gradient flow and initialization convergence properties**\",\"**Formulating strong no-go theorem for balanced duplicates**\",\"**Detailing gradient norm limit with concentration via spectral bounds**\",\"**Proving spectral norm bound and validating random regime scaling**\",\"**Formulating rigorous theorem for gradient flow contradiction**\",\"**Proving initialization moment convergence and gradient norm limit**\",\"**Completing gradient norm concentration and integral contradiction proof**\",\"**Planning rigorous backpropagation concentration proof**\",\"**Deriving layerwise variance and moment limits**\",\"**Establishing gradient norm concentration and variance limits**\",\"**Analyzing gradient flow and initialization convergence properties**\",\"**Formulating strong no-go theorem for balanced duplicates**\",\"**Detailing gradient norm limit with concentration via spectral bounds**\",\"**Proving spectral norm bound and validating random regime scaling**\",\"**Formulating rigorous theorem for gradient flow contradiction**\",\"**Proving initialization moment convergence and gradient norm limit**\",\"**Completing gradient norm concentration and integral contradiction proof**\",\"**Confirming universal failure for arbitrary normalized inputs and activations**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_oCGrDGP2YSE9z5y06fMpiexZ\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_cQEGZN6glXbF2vIqCy0kJCLa\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-8946-7371-a674-18ae21e4402e\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/tp_scope_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_F5zZuQ5e8b4330tRL4dUl4h7\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-a4af-7b22-8235-af93474c086c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/uniform_lift_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1182\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing gradient quadratic distribution behavior**\",\"**Verifying gradient norm concentration conditions**\",\"**Analyzing gradient quadratic distribution behavior**\",\"**Verifying gradient norm concentration conditions**\",\"**Analyzing gradient quadratic distribution behavior**\",\"**Verifying gradient norm concentration conditions**\",\"**Establishing loss bounds for exact gradient descent**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1183\",\"text\":\"A stronger obstruction closes the random-limit loophole too. At CLT readout scale, the initialization first-layer output-gradient norm is asymptotically a strictly positive constant for every admissible non-affine activation. Finite \\\\(K^{(1)}\\\\) then forces the first-layer mobility to be \\\\(O(1)\\\\), while an exact discrete-GD identity forces its empirical feature speed to vanish like \\\\(1/n\\\\). I’m checking the reused-\\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) dependence in that gradient calculation explicitly before stating the theorem.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1184\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving conditional variance formula**\",\"**Deriving conditional variance formula**\",\"**Bounding conditional moments and variance decay**\",\"**Deriving conditional variance formula**\",\"**Bounding conditional moments and variance decay**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04240-6ce6-73c2-bdb0-15dbf3332b83\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-8946-7371-a674-18ae21e4402e\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/tp_scope_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1185\",\"summary\":[\"**Evaluating conjecture on nonlinear configuration implications**\",\"**Analyzing feature learning under varying output scales**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04240-5eac-7910-83e7-9189b3bbc526\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1186\",\"summary\":[\"**Outlining final proof structure**\",\"**Assessing convergence and limit distributions**\",\"**Deriving gradient flow integral relation**\",\"**Assessing convergence and limit distributions**\",\"**Deriving gradient flow integral relation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a04240-7cca-7dd2-9a79-eb5df95c6372\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-a4af-7b22-8235-af93474c086c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/uniform_lift_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1187\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing convergence and limit distributions**\",\"**Deriving gradient flow integral relation**\",\"**Establishing concentration bounds and final theorem scope**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1188\",\"text\":\"No. Under the stated quantifier “arbitrary input and label configuration,” the \\\\(L=2\\\\) conjecture is false.\\n\\nIn fact, one admissible two-sample dataset rules it out for every fixed non-affine \\\\(C^2\\\\) activation and every allowed choice of variances, layerwise learning rates, and base step sizes. The argument also closes the possible nondegenerate random-output loophole.\\n\\n## Theorem\\n\\nLet \\\\(x\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\) satisfy\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand consider the two samples\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(x_1,y_1)=(x,+1),\\n\\\\qquad\\n(x_2,y_2)=(x,-1).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUse the averaged loss. Then no admissible activation and hyperparameter sequence can satisfy all the previously required properties, including:\\n\\n- finite nondegenerate hidden states;\\n- finite uniformly convergent kernel;\\n- feature learning in both hidden layers;\\n- a joint exact-GD/gradient-flow limit;\\n- strict leading-order loss decrease.\\n\\nThis remains true even if the limiting initial output is allowed to be random.\\n\\n## Proof\\n\\nBecause the two inputs are identical, their network outputs are identical. Write this common output as \\\\(f_n\\\\). Then, exactly at every width,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nL_n\\n=\\n\\\\frac12\\\\left[(f_n-1)^2+(f_n+1)^2\\\\right]\\n=\\n1+f_n^2.\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_{W^{(\\\\ell)}}L_n\\n=\\n2f_n\\\\nabla_{W^{(\\\\ell)}}f_n.\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK_n^{(\\\\ell)}\\n=\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(\\\\ell)}\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\\\nabla_{W^{(\\\\ell)}}f_n\\\\right\\\\|^2,\\n\\\\qquad\\nK_n=\\\\sum_{\\\\ell=1}^3K_n^{(\\\\ell)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor continuous layer-scaled gradient flow,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f_n=-2f_nK_n.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWe now split according to the only possible initialization scales.\\n\\n### 1. Vanishing initial output\\n\\nAt initialization, conditionally on the first two layers,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_n(0)\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{j=1}^n W_j^{(3)}\\\\phi(S_j)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis centered Gaussian, with conditional variance\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_n\\n=\\n\\\\sigma_{3,n}^2\\n\\\\sum_{j=1}^n\\\\phi(S_j)^2.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIf \\\\(Q_n\\\\to0\\\\), then\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_n(0)\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nin probability. More generally, symmetry shows that every deterministic finite probability limit of \\\\(f_n(0)\\\\) must be zero.\\n\\nUnder the claimed joint gradient-flow limit, (3) passes to\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f(t)=-2K(t)f(t),\\n\\\\qquad f(0)=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince the required finite kernel satisfies \\\\(K\\\\in L^1_{\\\\mathrm{loc}}\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf(t)\\n=\\nf(0)\\\\exp\\\\left(-2\\\\int_0^tK(s)\\\\,ds\\\\right)\\n=\\n0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\nL(t)=1+f(t)^2=1\\n\\\\]\\n\\nfor every \\\\(t\\\\). Hence\\n\\n\\\\[\\nL(T)=L(0),\\n\\\\]\\n\\ncontradicting the required strict loss decrease.\\n\\nA singular discrete boundary layer cannot evade this conclusion while retaining the required vanishing discrete-GD error and convergence to a genuine gradient flow.\\n\\n### 2. Nondegenerate random initial output\\n\\nIt remains to rule out an \\\\(O(1)\\\\) random Gaussian output.\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_i=W_i^{(1)}\\\\cdot x,\\\\qquad\\nH_i=\\\\phi(U_i),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS_j=\\\\sum_iW_{ji}^{(2)}H_i,\\n\\\\qquad\\nG_j=\\\\phi(S_j).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe required positive finite hidden variances imply\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_i\\\\Rightarrow U\\\\sim N(0,v_1),\\n\\\\qquad\\nS_j\\\\Rightarrow S\\\\sim N(0,v_2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith \\\\(v_1,v_2\\\\in(0,\\\\infty)\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) has polynomial growth,\\n\\n\\\\[\\na_0:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\phi(U)^2\\\\in(0,\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditionally on the first layer,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(S_j\\\\mid U)\\n=\\n\\\\sigma_{2,n}^2\\\\sum_i\\\\phi(U_i)^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe law of large numbers and the positive limit \\\\(v_2\\\\) therefore give\\n\\n\\\\[\\nn\\\\sigma_{2,n}^2\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\nc_2:=\\\\frac{v_2}{a_0}\\\\in(0,\\\\infty).\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSimilarly,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_jG_j^2\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\nb_0:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\phi(S)^2\\\\in(0,\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus a finite nondegenerate random limit of \\\\(f_n(0)\\\\) is possible only if\\n\\n\\\\[\\nn\\\\sigma_{3,n}^2\\\\longrightarrow c_3\\\\in(0,\\\\infty).\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWe next compute the initial first-layer output-gradient norm.\\n\\nSet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_i\\n=\\n\\\\phi'(U_i)\\n\\\\sum_j\\nW_j^{(3)}\\\\phi'(S_j)W_{ji}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_{W_i^{(1)}}f_n=B_ix,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\\\nabla_{W^{(1)}}f_n\\\\right\\\\|_F^2\\n=\\n\\\\|x\\\\|^2\\\\sum_iB_i^2.\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe dependence between \\\\(W_{ji}^{(2)}\\\\) and \\\\(S_j\\\\) must be retained. For one row \\\\(w\\\\) of \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\), define\\n\\n\\\\[\\na_i=\\\\phi(U_i),\\\\qquad r_i=\\\\phi'(U_i),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA=\\\\sum_i a_i^2,\\\\qquad\\nB=\\\\sum_i r_i^2,\\\\qquad\\nC=\\\\sum_i a_i^2r_i^2,\\n\\\\qquad\\nS=w\\\\cdot a.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditionally on \\\\(U\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nw=\\\\frac{S}{A}a+\\\\xi,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\) is independent of \\\\(S\\\\) and\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Cov}(\\\\xi)\\n=\\n\\\\sigma_{2,n}^2\\n\\\\left(I-\\\\frac{aa^\\\\top}{A}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently, with \\\\(h=\\\\phi'\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\nh(S)^2\\nw^\\\\top\\\\operatorname{diag}(r^2)w\\n\\\\mid U\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&=\\n\\\\sigma_{2,n}^2\\n\\\\left(B-\\\\frac CA\\\\right)\\n\\\\mathbb E h(S)^2\\n+\\n\\\\frac{C}{A^2}\\n\\\\mathbb E[S^2h(S)^2].\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nNow\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac Bn\\\\longrightarrow\\na_1:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\phi'(U)^2,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac An\\\\longrightarrow a_0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhile \\\\(C/A=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\). From (5), the second term and the \\\\(C/A\\\\) correction in (8) are \\\\(O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\). Therefore the leading row contribution is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nc_2a_1b_1,\\n\\\\qquad\\nb_1:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\phi'(S)^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditioning next on \\\\(U,W^{(2)}\\\\), \\\\(\\\\sum_iB_i^2\\\\) is a Gaussian quadratic form in \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Row averaging and Gaussian quadratic-form concentration give\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_iB_i^2\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\nc_2c_3a_1b_1\\n\\\\qquad\\\\text{in probability}.\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor completeness, concentration follows because the quadratic-form trace is \\\\(\\\\Theta_{\\\\mathbb P}(n)\\\\), while its operator norm is at most polylogarithmic in \\\\(n\\\\): polynomial growth gives Gaussian-max bounds for the diagonal derivative matrices, and an elementary sphere-net estimate gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nunder (5). Hence the conditional variance in (9) is \\\\(O((\\\\log n)^q/n)\\\\) for some finite \\\\(q\\\\).\\n\\nBecause \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) is non-affine and \\\\(U,S\\\\) are full-support nondegenerate Gaussians,\\n\\n\\\\[\\na_1>0,\\\\qquad b_1>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIndeed, either expectation could vanish only if the continuous function \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) vanished everywhere.\\n\\nCombining (7) and (9),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\\\nabla_{W^{(1)}}f_n(0)\\\\right\\\\|_F^2\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\gamma_1\\n:=\\n\\\\|x\\\\|^2c_2c_3a_1b_1\\n>0.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUniform finite convergence of the total kernel implies\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK_n^{(1)}(0)\\n=\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\\\nabla_{W^{(1)}}f_n(0)\\\\right\\\\|_F^2\\n\\\\le K_n(0)\\n=\\nO_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEquation (10) therefore forces\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}=O(1).\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nNow consider the polygonal interpolation of exact GD. On every step interval, let\\n\\n\\\\[\\nb_i=\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial U_i}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince \\\\(\\\\nabla_{W_i^{(1)}}f_n=b_ix\\\\), equations (1)–(2) give exactly\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot U_i\\n=\\n-2f_n\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}\\\\|x\\\\|^2b_i.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nV_n^{(1)}(t)^2\\n&=\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i|\\\\dot U_i|^2\\\\\\\\\\n&=\\n4f_n(t)^2\\n\\\\frac{\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}\\\\|x\\\\|^2}{n}\\nK_n^{(1)}(t).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is an exact discrete-interpolation identity, not a Taylor or continuous-time approximation.\\n\\nUsing (11) and uniform compact-time boundedness of \\\\(f_n\\\\) and \\\\(K_n\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\int_0^T V_n^{(1)}(t)^2\\\\,dt\\n&\\\\le\\n4\\\\|x\\\\|^2\\n\\\\frac{\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}}n\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}|f_n(t)|^2\\n\\\\int_0^TK_n^{(1)}(t)\\\\,dt\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\xrightarrow{\\\\mathbb P}0.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus the first hidden layer is lazy, contradicting the required strictly positive feature-energy limit.\\n\\n### 3. Remaining scales\\n\\nIf the initial output is neither vanishing nor finite nondegenerate, then it is non-tight or nonconvergent, contradicting full joint output convergence. The preceding two cases are therefore exhaustive.\\n\\n## Conclusion\\n\\nFor the admissible normalized dataset\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\{(x,+1),(x,-1)\\\\},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nevery possible scaling fails:\\n\\n- a self-averaging output gives constant limiting loss;\\n- a nondegenerate random output forces the first hidden layer to be lazy under a finite kernel;\\n- any other output scaling violates finite joint convergence.\\n\\nTherefore:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\\\text{No single nonlinear \\\\(L=2\\\\) construction can work for every normalized finite dataset and every \\\\(\\\\{\\\\pm1\\\\}\\\\)-labeling.}}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis does not say that every multi-sample dataset fails. Orthogonal inputs or repeated inputs with a nonzero average label can inherit variants of the one-sample arctan construction. But the universal “arbitrary configuration” statement is definitively false. If contradictory duplicate inputs were intended to be excluded, that is a materially different conjecture requiring an explicit consistency or separation assumption.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a03fe8-946d-7331-8e04-0201fad2a2cd\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1787778602,\"completedAt\":1787779282,\"durationMs\":679890,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"contextCompaction\",\"id\":\"item-1124\"},{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1125\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"can you then generate the fully rigourous and comleted proof by fixing all issues you found? after that, run many diverse and independent and adversarial audits to make sure it's fully valid, checking all the steps and invocations and theorems used \\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1126\",\"text\":\"I’m using the rigorous-math skill because this now requires closing the proof—not just refining the architecture. I’ll keep the task self-contained: I will not inspect any project file. I’ll first formalize the repaired state space and uniform-in-width lift, while three isolated agents attack the probability/operator construction, the diagonal GD limit, and an adversarial counter-audit.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_VnodqENILPEzlSdVl9GlDF3v\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-8946-7371-a674-18ae21e4402e\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/tp_scope_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_aKMpRFnvdtJQCf9WVc9bRPv7\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_wl6iF0ylNyoC3QwSPOz8VPJE\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-a4af-7b22-8235-af93474c086c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/uniform_lift_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1127\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing convergence of interpolated dynamics**\",\"**Assessing convergence of interpolated dynamics**\",\"**Defining topology and verifying gradient scaling**\",\"**Assessing convergence of interpolated dynamics**\",\"**Defining topology and verifying gradient scaling**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1128\",\"text\":\"The exact finite-width algebra survives the first check: with \\\\(C=nW^{(3)}\\\\), every normalized increment is indeed of order \\\\(\\\\eta\\\\), and the three kernel blocks are exactly the squared \\\\(L^2\\\\)/rank-one quantities claimed. The difficult bridge is narrower now: realizing the reused Gaussian matrix as a law-level operator and lifting fixed-mesh convergence uniformly to continuous time without secretly invoking a growing-depth tensor program. I’m verifying that bridge against the primary theorem statement, not memory.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-60408c12-6b7e-4013-8f7b-870059d63236\",\"query\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/yang21c/yang21c-supp.pdf\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/yang21c/yang21c-supp.pdf\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-20898f16-dc6a-4f22-97c8-515d73ebfd9e\",\"query\":\"'Theorem G.4'\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"findInPage\",\"url\":null,\"pattern\":\"Theorem G.4\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1129\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning Gaussian operator norm proof**\",\"**Planning Gaussian operator norm proof**\",\"**Establishing operator norm and flow bounds**\",\"**Planning Gaussian operator norm proof**\",\"**Establishing operator norm and flow bounds**\",\"**Analyzing Lipschitz vector field and flow convergen