**\",\"**Simplifying initialization with zero left vector**\",\"**Bounding Euler defects and establishing flow convergence**\",\"**Establishing empirical path law convergence and tightness**\",\"**Formulating proof and audit strategy**\",\"**Verifying operator construction consistency**\",\"**Analyzing cylinder set construction circularity**\",\"**Establishing finite-state probe metric**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a03fe9-6620-7b10-a15e-966bfca30149\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-8946-7371-a674-18ae21e4402e\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/tp_scope_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a03fe9-910e-77f1-a797-b7cb60be0695\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-a4af-7b22-8235-af93474c086c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/uniform_lift_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1130\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing cylinder set construction circularity**\",\"**Establishing finite-state probe metric**\",\"**Defining topology via bounded Lipschitz probes**\",\"**Analyzing TP agent topology limitations**\",\"**Evaluating cross-sort joint measure feasibility**\",\"**Analyzing TP agent topology limitations**\",\"**Evaluating cross-sort joint measure feasibility**\",\"**Deriving uniform Euler error bounds**\",\"**Analyzing TP agent topology limitations**\",\"**Evaluating cross-sort joint measure feasibility**\",\"**Deriving uniform Euler error bounds**\",\"**Analyzing TP agent topology limitations**\",\"**Evaluating cross-sort joint measure feasibility**\",\"**Deriving uniform Euler error bounds**\",\"**Formulating uniform convergence theorem for flows**\",\"**Analyzing TP agent topology limitations**\",\"**Evaluating cross-sort joint measure feasibility**\",\"**Deriving uniform Euler error bounds**\",\"**Formulating uniform convergence theorem for flows**\",\"**Defining L2-Wasserstein metric for flow convergence**\",\"**Analyzing TP agent topology limitations**\",\"**Evaluating cross-sort joint measure feasibility**\",\"**Deriving uniform Euler error bounds**\",\"**Formulating uniform convergence theorem for flows**\",\"**Defining L2-Wasserstein metric for flow convergence**\",\"**Analyzing TP agent topology limitations**\",\"**Evaluating cross-sort joint measure feasibility**\",\"**Deriving uniform Euler error bounds**\",\"**Formulating uniform convergence theorem for flows**\",\"**Defining L2-Wasserstein metric for flow convergence**\",\"**Establishing separate Kolmogorov spaces for sorts**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a03fe9-7196-7431-9346-29d117184446\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1131\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing TP agent topology limitations**\",\"**Evaluating cross-sort joint measure feasibility**\",\"**Deriving uniform Euler error bounds**\",\"**Formulating uniform convergence theorem for flows**\",\"**Defining L2-Wasserstein metric for flow convergence**\",\"**Establishing separate Kolmogorov spaces for sorts**\",\"**Formalizing gradient flow well-posedness and convergence lemmas**\",\"**Establishing uniform-time joint Wasserstein flow**\",\"**Establishing uniform-time joint Wasserstein flow**\",\"**Proving uniform integrability for W2 convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform-time joint Wasserstein flow**\",\"**Proving uniform integrability for W2 convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform velocity field convergence via tail truncation**\",\"**Establishing uniform-time joint Wasserstein flow**\",\"**Proving uniform integrability for W2 convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform velocity field convergence via tail truncation**\",\"**Deriving path interpolation error bounds and proving joint convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform-time joint Wasserstein flow**\",\"**Proving uniform integrability for W2 convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform velocity field convergence via tail truncation**\",\"**Deriving path interpolation error bounds and proving joint convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform-time joint Wasserstein flow**\",\"**Proving uniform integrability for W2 convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform velocity field convergence via tail truncation**\",\"**Deriving path interpolation error bounds and proving joint convergence**\",\"**Deriving explicit local Lipschitz constants and contraction bounds**\",\"**Establishing uniform-time joint Wasserstein flow**\",\"**Proving uniform integrability for W2 convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform velocity field convergence via tail truncation**\",\"**Deriving path interpolation error bounds and proving joint convergence**\",\"**Deriving explicit local Lipschitz constants and contraction bounds**\",\"**Establishing uniform-time joint Wasserstein flow**\",\"**Proving uniform integrability for W2 convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform velocity field convergence via tail truncation**\",\"**Deriving path interpolation error bounds and proving joint convergence**\",\"**Deriving explicit local Lipschitz constants and contraction bounds**\",\"**Applying Nemytskii chain rule and bounding Euler update errors**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1132\",\"text\":\"The first audit round found that the repaired route can be made rigorous, but only with a deliberately narrower topology than the earlier “all polynomial probes” topology. I’m now using the current operator \\\\(M_t\\\\), two probability algebras, and the finite set of dynamically reachable \\\\(L^2\\\\) fields; the unbounded products \\\\(q(U)P\\\\) and \\\\(M(q(U)^2P)\\\\) are handled by a separate uniform-integrability lemma. This removes the main hidden continuity assumption.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_ozkaxCpLMjrIG4BuICilC2fN\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-8946-7371-a674-18ae21e4402e\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/tp_scope_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_vAgLKfeF0tuLhuzsi2PoP16h\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_cjlpf2Z5WOO9BH1UeCknMVKQ\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-a4af-7b22-8235-af93474c086c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/uniform_lift_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1133\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying pseudo-Lipschitz properties and Gaussian assumptions**\",\"**Confirming pseudo-Lipschitz degrees and program consistency**\",\"**Verifying pseudo-Lipschitz properties and Gaussian assumptions**\",\"**Confirming pseudo-Lipschitz degrees and program consistency**\",\"**Verifying pseudo-Lipschitz properties and Gaussian assumptions**\",\"**Confirming pseudo-Lipschitz degrees and program consistency**\",\"**Proving Gaussian operator bounds and establishing uniform flow convergence**\",\"**Verifying pseudo-Lipschitz properties and Gaussian assumptions**\",\"**Confirming pseudo-Lipschitz degrees and program consistency**\",\"**Proving Gaussian operator bounds and establishing uniform flow convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform W2 convergence and energy bounds**\",\"**Verifying pseudo-Lipschitz properties and Gaussian assumptions**\",\"**Confirming pseudo-Lipschitz degrees and program consistency**\",\"**Proving Gaussian operator bounds and establishing uniform flow convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform W2 convergence and energy bounds**\",\"**Verifying pseudo-Lipschitz properties and Gaussian assumptions**\",\"**Confirming pseudo-Lipschitz degrees and program consistency**\",\"**Proving Gaussian operator bounds and establishing uniform flow convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform W2 convergence and energy bounds**\",\"**Comparing finite flow and exact path with interpolation errors**\",\"**Verifying pseudo-Lipschitz properties and Gaussian assumptions**\",\"**Confirming pseudo-Lipschitz degrees and program consistency**\",\"**Proving Gaussian operator bounds and establishing uniform flow convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform W2 convergence and energy bounds**\",\"**Comparing finite flow and exact path with interpolation errors**\",\"**Verifying pseudo-Lipschitz properties and Gaussian assumptions**\",\"**Confirming pseudo-Lipschitz degrees and program consistency**\",\"**Proving Gaussian operator bounds and establishing uniform flow convergence**\",\"**Establishing uniform W2 convergence and energy bounds**\",\"**Comparing finite flow and exact path with interpolation errors**\",\"**Deriving positive expansions and confirming operator coercivity**\",\"**Clarifying state representation and restart conditions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a03fee-1883-7f31-b48a-f3e4317b913f\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-a4af-7b22-8235-af93474c086c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/uniform_lift_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a03fed-ffe3-7ad1-9831-4a569f6468d5\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-8946-7371-a674-18ae21e4402e\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/tp_scope_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1134\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing Fréchet differentiability of scalar functional**\",\"**Analyzing Fréchet differentiability of scalar functional**\",\"**Examining remainder behavior under bounded operator M**\",\"**Analyzing Fréchet differentiability of scalar functional**\",\"**Examining remainder behavior under bounded operator M**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1135\",\"text\":\"A second adversarial round has now cleared the reused-adjoint construction, the singular-Gram issue, the transformed-GD defect, the path-law lift, and all seven positivity requirements. It did force two final corrections: the limiting readout seed must be marginalized out, and the flow is a Hilbert gradient flow in the \\\\(L^2\\\\!\\\\times\\\\!S_2\\\\!\\\\times\\\\!L^2\\\\) metric while trace class is only the stronger control topology. I’m checking those two points and the final quantifiers once more before presenting the theorem.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a03fee-0bfd-71b1-b8cd-a71f1148002e\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_LnF8VHZrSfyVsjmBvKpJkvf9\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1136\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning final comprehensive answer with citations**\",\"**Structuring final proof presentation and notation**\",\"**Planning final comprehensive answer with citations**\",\"**Structuring final proof presentation and notation**\",\"**Planning final comprehensive answer with citations**\",\"**Structuring final proof presentation and notation**\",\"**Detailing construction and topology of operator state space**\",\"**Deriving explicit Euler error bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a03fef-fa90-76c2-9955-ddfa752e0355\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03fd6-97bb-7952-89f5-a6170c208447\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/functional_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1137\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving explicit Euler error bounds**\",\"**Verifying kernel expansion positivity**\",\"**Finalizing finite state action topology**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1138\",\"text\":\"Yes. After replacing the overstrong polynomial-probe topology and repairing the probability-space construction, the arctangent construction gives a rigorous affirmative example.\\n\\nNo project files were inspected. The only external ingredient is the fixed-finite-program Tensor Program Master Theorem, whose exact scope was checked from the primary source.\\n\\n## Theorem\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi(x)=\\\\arctan x\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand choose\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sigma_{1,n}^{2}=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\sigma_{2,n}^{2}=\\\\frac1n,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\sigma_{3,n}^{2}=\\\\frac1{n^{4}},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}=n,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(2)}=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(3)}=\\\\frac1n,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_{0,n}=n^{-2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}=n^{-1},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\lambda_n^{(2)}=n^{-2},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\lambda_n^{(3)}=n^{-3},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso every raw layerwise step tends to zero.\\n\\nThere exists \\\\(T_*>0\\\\) and a deterministic, autonomous operator-law gradient flow such that, for every \\\\(0<T<T_*\\\\), the polygonally interpolated exact GD trajectory converges in probability, along the full sequence \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), uniformly on \\\\([0,T]\\\\). The convergence includes:\\n\\n- the complete dynamically reachable operator action;\\n- both hidden path laws in \\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\);\\n- all three parameter updates;\\n- the output, loss, and all kernel blocks uniformly in time;\\n- both feature velocities in the time-integrated topology required in the question.\\n\\nEvery one of properties 1–7 holds on every such interval.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1. Exact finite-width equations\\n\\nWrite\\n\\n\\\\[\\nu=W^{(1)},\\\\qquad M=W^{(2)},\\\\qquad c=nW^{(3)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand use\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle v,w\\\\rangle_n=\\\\frac1n v^\\\\top w,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|v\\\\|_n^2=\\\\langle v,v\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor vectors on the two hidden layers define\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(a\\\\otimes_n b)v=a\\\\langle b,v\\\\rangle_n=\\\\frac1n ab^\\\\top v.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh=\\\\phi(u),\\\\qquad s=Mh,\\\\qquad g=\\\\phi(s),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nq(x)=\\\\phi'(x)=\\\\frac1{1+x^2},\\\\qquad\\nd=cq(s),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere products are coordinatewise, and\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf=\\\\langle c,g\\\\rangle_n,\\\\qquad e=f-1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDirect differentiation gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_u f=\\\\frac1n q(u)M^\\\\top d,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_M f=\\\\frac1n dh^\\\\top,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_{W^{(3)}}f=g.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently one exact GD step of duration \\\\(\\\\eta=\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\) is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nu^+=u-2\\\\eta e\\\\,q(u)M^\\\\top d,\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM^+=M-2\\\\eta e\\\\,d\\\\otimes_n h,\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nc^+=c-2\\\\eta e\\\\,g.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThere is no approximation in (1)–(3).\\n\\nThe three kernel blocks are exactly\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK_n^{(1)}=\\\\|q(u)M^\\\\top d\\\\|_n^2,\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK_n^{(2)}=\\\\|h\\\\|_n^2\\\\|d\\\\|_n^2,\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK_n^{(3)}=\\\\|g\\\\|_n^2.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIndeed, for example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nn\\\\|\\\\nabla_u f\\\\|_{\\\\ell^2}^2\\n=\\nn\\\\frac1{n^2}\\\\sum_i(q(u_i)(M^\\\\top d)_i)^2\\n=\\nK_n^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the other two identities follow similarly.\\n\\nThe initialization is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nu_i(0)\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\qquad\\nM_{ji}(0)\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nc_j(0)=nW_j^{(3)}(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-2}).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIn particular,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|c(0)\\\\|_n\\\\longrightarrow0,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|c(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nin probability.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 2. The natural coordinate\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta(r)=r+\\\\frac{r^3}{3},\\\\qquad X=\\\\Theta(u),\\\\qquad J=\\\\Theta^{-1}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBecause\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta'(r)=1+r^2=q(r)^{-1},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe continuous version of (1) becomes\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X=-2eM^*D.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere and below uppercase letters denote limiting fields.\\n\\nPut\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Psi(x)=\\\\arctan J(x).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe required global estimates are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\,\\\\arctan x\\\\,|\\\\le a:=\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Lip}(J)\\\\le1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\Psi)\\\\le1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Lip}(q)=\\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt3}{8},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\sup_x|x|q(x)^2=\\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt3}{16}.\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe limiting equations will therefore be written as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nH=\\\\Psi(X),\\\\qquad S=MH,\\\\qquad G=\\\\arctan S,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nD=Cq(S),\\\\qquad F=\\\\mathbb E_L[CG],\\\\qquad e=F-1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP=M^*D,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\dot X=-2eP,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot M=-2eD\\\\otimes H,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot C=-2eG.\\n}\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe rank-one operator means\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(D\\\\otimes H)v=D\\\\,\\\\mathbb E_R[Hv].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIn the original coordinate \\\\(U=J(X)\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot U=-2e\\\\,q(U)M^*D.\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3. Fixed-program theorem and the Gaussian operator\\n\\nThe only external theorem used is the following specialization.\\n\\n### Fixed-program master theorem\\n\\nConsider one fixed finite straight-line program containing:\\n\\n- iid matrices with entries \\\\(N(0,\\\\sigma^2/n)\\\\);\\n- iid-coordinate jointly Gaussian initial vectors;\\n- scalar inputs converging almost surely to deterministic limits;\\n- repeated multiplications by any initial matrix and its transpose;\\n- coordinatewise and empirical-moment maps that are jointly pseudo-Lipschitz in all vector and scalar arguments.\\n\\nThen for every fixed finite tuple of generated vectors and every pseudo-Lipschitz test function, its empirical average converges almost surely to a deterministic master expectation. Every generated scalar converges almost surely as well.\\n\\nThis is precisely Setup G.2, Assumption L.4, and Theorem G.4 in the [Tensor Programs IV supplement](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/yang21c/yang21c-supp.pdf). The theorem is explicitly for a fixed finite program; we never apply it to a program whose length grows with \\\\(n\\\\). Reuse of \\\\(M_0,M_0^\\\\top\\\\) is part of the MatMul language. Under Assumption L.4 no rank-stability hypothesis is imposed; singular limiting Gram matrices are permitted by the underlying master theorem.\\n\\nAll functions used here satisfy its assumptions:\\n\\n- \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\) is pseudo-Lipschitz;\\n- \\\\(J,\\\\Psi,\\\\arctan,q\\\\) are globally Lipschitz;\\n- \\\\((c,s)\\\\mapsto cq(s)\\\\), scalar-vector multiplication, and the finite products used below are pseudo-Lipschitz;\\n- the initial readout is encoded exactly as\\n  \\\\[\\n  C_{0,n}=a_nR_n,\\\\qquad a_n=\\\\frac1n,\\\\quad R_n\\\\sim N(0,I_n),\\n  \\\\]\\n  where \\\\(a_n\\\\to0\\\\) and \\\\((a,r)\\\\mapsto ar\\\\) is jointly pseudo-Lipschitz.\\n\\n### Construction of the two-sided action\\n\\nCreate a countable, typed universal program as follows.\\n\\nThere are right and left variables. Start with the right Gaussian \\\\(U_0\\\\), the left auxiliary Gaussian \\\\(R_0\\\\), constants, and the matrix \\\\(M_{0,n}\\\\). Repeatedly add:\\n\\n1. rational linear combinations;\\n2. a countable dense family of bounded Lipschitz cylinder functions;\\n3. the functions \\\\(\\\\Theta,J,\\\\Psi,\\\\arctan,q\\\\);\\n4. \\\\(M_{0,n}v\\\\) for every constructed right variable;\\n5. \\\\(M_{0,n}^{\\\\top}w\\\\) for every constructed left variable;\\n6. all instructions appearing in every rational-mesh, finite-step Euler scheme.\\n\\nDovetailing these instructions produces a countable sequence in which every finite prefix is a valid finite Tensor Program.\\n\\nFor each finite collection of right variables, apply the master theorem to obtain its deterministic limiting joint law. These laws are projectively consistent: extending a finite program leaves every old finite-\\\\(n\\\\) vector literally unchanged, so the same empirical bounded-Lipschitz statistic cannot have two different deterministic limits. The same construction is made for the left variables.\\n\\nKolmogorov extension therefore gives two countably generated probability spaces\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\Omega_R,\\\\mathcal F_R,\\\\mathbb P_R),\\n\\\\qquad\\n(\\\\Omega_L,\\\\mathcal F_L,\\\\mathbb P_L).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal H_R=L^2(\\\\Omega_R),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathcal H_L=L^2(\\\\Omega_L).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe bounded-Lipschitz cylinder fields are dense. Indeed, conditional expectations onto the first \\\\(m\\\\) coordinate variables converge in \\\\(L^2\\\\), and continuous compactly supported functions are dense in each finite-dimensional \\\\(L^2\\\\) law.\\n\\nFor the initial Gaussian matrices, a \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net argument gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|M_{0,n}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}>10)\\n\\\\le\\n2\\\\cdot81^n e^{-25n/2}.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence, almost surely, \\\\(\\\\|M_{0,n}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le10\\\\) eventually.\\n\\nOn the dense right cylinder class define\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Gamma v:=Z^{M_0v}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIf \\\\(v=\\\\widetilde v\\\\) in limiting \\\\(L^2\\\\), then by the master theorem and (12),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\Gamma v-\\\\Gamma\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2\\n=\\n\\\\lim_n\\\\|M_{0,n}(v_n-\\\\widetilde v_n)\\\\|_n^2\\n\\\\le\\n100\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus \\\\(\\\\Gamma\\\\) is well-defined and extends uniquely to\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Gamma:\\\\mathcal H_R\\\\longrightarrow\\\\mathcal H_L,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|\\\\Gamma\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le10.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe reverse action similarly defines a bounded operator \\\\(\\\\Gamma^\\\\dagger\\\\). Exact finite-dimensional duality gives, first on dense cylinder classes,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E_L[(\\\\Gamma v)w]\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E_R[v(\\\\Gamma^\\\\dagger w)].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDensity implies\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Gamma^\\\\dagger=\\\\Gamma^*.\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is the exact reused-adjoint identity required by the problem. It is obtained from the finite matrix identity, not postulated.\\n\\nThe auxiliary mark \\\\(R_0\\\\) disappears because \\\\(a_nR_n\\\\to0\\\\). It is not part of the limiting state.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 4. The autonomous law state\\n\\nA limiting state is the isomorphism class of\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\Omega_R,\\\\Omega_L;X,C,M),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nX\\\\in L^2_R,\\\\qquad C\\\\in L^2_L\\\\cap L^\\\\infty_L,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM:\\\\mathcal H_R\\\\to\\\\mathcal H_L\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis bounded, with \\\\(M-\\\\Gamma\\\\) trace-class in the initial chart.\\n\\nTwo states are identified only under probability-algebra isomorphisms whose Koopman maps preserve \\\\(X,C\\\\) and intertwine \\\\(M\\\\). Arbitrary Hilbert-space unitaries are not allowed, because they need not preserve pointwise nonlinear functional calculus.\\n\\nThe actual restart state stores only the current \\\\(M,X,C\\\\). Neither the list of previous rank-one updates, nor a past trajectory, nor Euler syntax is retained. Equation (10) depends only on the current state, so uniqueness below proves restartability.\\n\\n### Required-probe topology\\n\\nThe earlier topology allowing arbitrary products of arbitrary unbounded probes was too strong. The correct topology is the countable initial topology generated by:\\n\\n- bounded-Lipschitz cylinder functions;\\n- repeated applications of \\\\(M\\\\) and \\\\(M^*\\\\);\\n- the dynamically required fields\\n  \\\\[\\n  U,H,S,G,C,D,P,\\n  \\\\]\\n- the three additional \\\\(L^2\\\\) fields\\n  \\\\[\\n  q(U)P,\\\\qquad q(U)^2P,\\\\qquad M(q(U)^2P);\\n  \\\\]\\n- their same-side finite joint \\\\(W_2\\\\) laws and the scalar expectations/norms appearing in the vector field and kernels.\\n\\nIf these probes are enumerated by \\\\(r\\\\), define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd_{\\\\mathrm{req}}(\\\\mathsf S,\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathsf S})\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{r\\\\ge1}2^{-r}\\n\\\\left(1\\\\wedge W_2(\\\\mu_r,\\\\widetilde\\\\mu_r)\\\\right)\\n+\\n\\\\sum_{r\\\\ge1}2^{-r}\\n\\\\left(1\\\\wedge|\\\\theta_r-\\\\widetilde\\\\theta_r|\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAfter quotienting by zero distance this is a genuine law-level metric. It determines every term of (10), all layer updates, both hidden laws, every kernel block, output, and loss.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5. Well-posedness of the operator flow\\n\\nWrite\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM=\\\\Gamma+Q,\\\\qquad Q\\\\in\\\\mathfrak S_1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUse the norm\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\rho((X,C,Q),(\\\\widetilde X,\\\\widetilde C,\\\\widetilde Q))\\n=\\n\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_2+\\n\\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_2+\\n\\\\|Q-\\\\widetilde Q\\\\|_{\\\\mathfrak S_1}.\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAssume on a bounded set that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\Gamma\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le B,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|Q\\\\|_1,\\\\|\\\\widetilde Q\\\\|_1\\\\le R,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|C\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le c.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nPut\\n\\n\\\\[\\nm=B+R,\\\\qquad E=1+ac,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\ell=\\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt3}{8},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd_*=1+c\\\\ell(m+a),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne_*=a+c(m+a),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\np_*=md_*+c.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|H-\\\\widetilde H\\\\|_2\\\\le\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\nm\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_2\\n+\\na\\\\|Q-\\\\widetilde Q\\\\|_1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|D-\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_2\\\\le d_*\\\\rho,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|e-\\\\widetilde e|\\\\le e_*\\\\rho,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|P-\\\\widetilde P\\\\|_2\\\\le p_*\\\\rho.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nMoreover,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|D\\\\otimes H-\\\\widetilde D\\\\otimes\\\\widetilde H\\\\|_1\\n\\\\le\\n(a d_*+c)\\\\rho.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore the vector field in (10) is Lipschitz with, for example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nL(B,R,c)\\n={}&\\n2(Ep_*+e_*mc)\\\\\\\\\\n&+2(E(m+a)+ae_*)\\\\\\\\\\n&+2(E(ad_*+c)+ace_*).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIts norm is bounded by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA(B,R,c)=2E(mc+a+ac).\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nA direct Picard theorem on an open \\\\(L^2\\\\) set would be invalid because the estimate for \\\\(Cq(S)\\\\) uses an \\\\(L^\\\\infty\\\\) envelope. Instead, use the closed complete path set\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le\\\\tau}\\\\rho(Y(t),Y(0))\\\\le1,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le\\\\tau}\\\\|C(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le c_0+1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor sufficiently small \\\\(\\\\tau\\\\), the integral map preserves both bounds because \\\\(|G|\\\\le a\\\\), and it is a contraction by (16). An \\\\(L^2\\\\)-limit of functions uniformly bounded by \\\\(c_0+1\\\\) retains the same almost-everywhere bound, so the set is complete.\\n\\nThus (10) has a unique local solution.\\n\\n### Energy identity and global existence\\n\\nFrom \\\\(U=J(X)\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot H=q(U)\\\\dot U=-2e\\\\,q(U)^2P.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot S\\n=\\n-2e\\\\left[\\n\\\\|H\\\\|_2^2D+\\nM(q(U)^2P)\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUsing \\\\(D=Cq(S)\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot F\\n&=\\n\\\\langle\\\\dot C,G\\\\rangle_L+\\n\\\\langle C,q(S)\\\\dot S\\\\rangle_L\\\\\\\\\\n&=\\n-2e\\\\left[\\n\\\\|G\\\\|_2^2+\\n\\\\|H\\\\|_2^2\\\\|D\\\\|_2^2+\\n\\\\|q(U)P\\\\|_2^2\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot e=-2Ke,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK=K^{(1)}+K^{(2)}+K^{(3)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}=\\\\|q(U)P\\\\|_2^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(2)}=\\\\|H\\\\|_2^2\\\\|D\\\\|_2^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(3)}=\\\\|G\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|e(t)|\\\\le |e(0)|=:E_0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince \\\\(|G|\\\\le a\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|C(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|C(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+2aE_0t.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAlso,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|Q(t)-Q(0)\\\\|_1\\n\\\\le\\n2aE_0\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\|C(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty t+aE_0t^2\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFinally,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X(t)\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\n2E_0\\\\|M(t)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\|C(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+2aE_0t\\\\bigr).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese estimates exclude finite-time blowup. The solution is global.\\n\\n### It is genuinely a gradient flow\\n\\nOn\\n\\n\\\\[\\nL^2_R\\\\times(\\\\Gamma+\\\\mathfrak S_2)\\\\times L^2_L\\n\\\\]\\n\\ndefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF(U,M,C)=\\n\\\\left\\\\langle\\nC,\\\\arctan(M\\\\arctan U)\\n\\\\right\\\\rangle_L.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAlthough the Nemytskii map \\\\(U\\\\mapsto\\\\arctan U\\\\) need not be Fréchet \\\\(L^2\\\\to L^2\\\\), this scalar functional is Fréchet differentiable.\\n\\nThe needed scalar lemma is: if \\\\(\\\\varphi'\\\\) is bounded and uniformly continuous, then for fixed \\\\(z,p\\\\in L^2\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\langle\\np,\\\\varphi(z+h)-\\\\varphi(z)-\\\\varphi'(z)h\\n\\\\right\\\\rangle\\n=o(\\\\|h\\\\|_2).\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTo prove it, split into \\\\(\\\\{|h|\\\\le\\\\delta\\\\}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\{|h|>\\\\delta\\\\}\\\\). On the first set the remainder is at most\\n\\\\(\\\\omega(\\\\delta)|h|\\\\), where \\\\(\\\\omega\\\\) is the modulus of continuity of \\\\(\\\\varphi'\\\\). On the second it is at most \\\\(2\\\\|\\\\varphi'\\\\|_\\\\infty|h|\\\\). Since\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(|h|>\\\\delta)\\\\le\\\\frac{\\\\|h\\\\|_2^2}{\\\\delta^2},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nabsolute continuity of \\\\(\\\\int p^2\\\\) makes the second contribution vanish after division by \\\\(\\\\|h\\\\|_2\\\\); then let \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\downarrow0\\\\).\\n\\nApplying (23) at the inner and outer activations gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_UF=q(U)M^*D,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_MF=D\\\\otimes H,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nabla_CF=G.\\n\\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore, in the original coordinates,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\dot U,\\\\dot M,\\\\dot C)\\n=\\n-\\\\nabla(F-1)^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe middle gradient metric is Hilbert–Schmidt. Trace class is the stronger regularity used for stability and convergence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 6. Every fixed Euler mesh has the claimed width limit\\n\\nFix \\\\(\\\\delta>0\\\\) and a finite number \\\\(K\\\\) of Euler steps. Let\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM_{k,n}=M_{0,n}+Q_{k,n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe Euler recursion is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nX_{k+1,n}=X_{k,n}-2\\\\delta e_{k,n}M_{k,n}^\\\\top D_{k,n},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC_{k+1,n}=C_{k,n}-2\\\\delta e_{k,n}G_{k,n},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{k+1,n}\\n=\\nQ_{k,n}\\n-\\n2\\\\delta e_{k,n}D_{k,n}\\\\otimes_n H_{k,n}.\\n\\\\tag{25}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nInductively,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{k,n}\\n=\\n-2\\\\delta\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\ne_{r,n}D_{r,n}\\\\otimes_n H_{r,n}.\\n\\\\tag{26}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM_{k,n}H_{k,n}\\n=\\nM_{0,n}H_{k,n}\\n-\\n2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\ne_{r,n}D_{r,n}\\n\\\\langle H_{r,n},H_{k,n}\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\tag{27}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM_{k,n}^{\\\\top}D_{k,n}\\n=\\nM_{0,n}^{\\\\top}D_{k,n}\\n-\\n2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\ne_{r,n}H_{r,n}\\n\\\\langle D_{r,n},D_{k,n}\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\tag{28}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus every fixed \\\\((\\\\delta,K)\\\\) recursion uses only:\\n\\n- \\\\(M_{0,n}\\\\) and \\\\(M_{0,n}^{\\\\top}\\\\);\\n- coordinate maps;\\n- finitely many empirical moments.\\n\\nIt is therefore a fixed finite Tensor Program. The master theorem and (13) identify its limit with the Euler scheme for (10). This proves pointwise fixed-mesh identification, including all reused-matrix response terms.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7. Width-uniform mesh removal\\n\\nFor either finite or limiting Euler schemes,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|C_{k+1}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\n(1+2a^2\\\\delta)\\\\|C_k\\\\|_\\\\infty+2a\\\\delta.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence, for \\\\(k\\\\delta\\\\le T\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|C_k\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\bar c_T\\n:=\\n\\\\left(c_0+\\\\frac1a\\\\right)e^{2a^2T}-\\\\frac1a.\\n\\\\tag{29}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bar E_T=1+a\\\\bar c_T,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bar R_T=2a\\\\bar E_T\\\\bar c_TT.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|Q_k\\\\|_1\\\\le\\\\bar R_T,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|M_k\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le10+\\\\bar R_T.\\n\\\\tag{30}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently (16)–(17) produce constants \\\\(L_T,A_T\\\\) independent of \\\\(n\\\\).\\n\\nIf \\\\(Y(t)\\\\) is the exact continuous flow and \\\\(Y_k^\\\\delta\\\\) its Euler scheme, the one-step flow truncation satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|Y(t_{k+1})-Y(t_k)-\\\\delta\\\\mathcal V(Y(t_k))\\\\|\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\frac12L_TA_T\\\\delta^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDiscrete Grönwall gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{k\\\\delta\\\\le T}\\n\\\\rho(Y(k\\\\delta),Y_k^\\\\delta)\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\frac{A_T}{2}\\n\\\\bigl(e^{L_TT}-1\\\\bigr)\\\\delta.\\n\\\\tag{31}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis estimate holds within every finite-width space and in the limiting operator space, with the same constant.\\n\\nFor any finite collection of required action probes, their \\\\(L^2\\\\) flow/Euler difference is therefore \\\\(O_T(\\\\delta)\\\\). At fixed \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\), the master theorem gives their width convergence at the finitely many mesh times. Truncating the series defining \\\\(d_{\\\\mathrm{req}}\\\\), taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), and then \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\downarrow0\\\\), gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\nd_{\\\\mathrm{req}}(\\\\mathsf S_n^{\\\\mathrm{flow}}(t),\\\\mathsf S(t))\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nin probability.\\n\\nNo growing-depth Tensor Program result is used.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8. Exact GD versus finite-width flow\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nR=-2eM^\\\\top d.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEquation (1) is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nu^+=u+\\\\eta q(u)R.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBecause \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\) is cubic,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta(u+\\\\eta q(u)R)\\n=\\n\\\\Theta(u)+\\\\eta R\\n+\\\\eta^2u q(u)^2R^2\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\eta^3}{3}q(u)^3R^3.\\n\\\\tag{32}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus exact GD is explicit Euler for (10), except for the exact defect\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\zeta\\n=\\n\\\\eta^2u q(u)^2R^2\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\eta^3}{3}q(u)^3R^3.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUsing (9),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\zeta\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt3}{16}\\n\\\\eta^2\\\\sqrt n\\\\,\\\\|R\\\\|_n^2\\n+\\n\\\\frac13\\\\eta^3n\\\\,\\\\|R\\\\|_n^3.\\n\\\\tag{33}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOn the bounded region (29)–(30),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|R\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le\\n2\\\\bar E_T(10+\\\\bar R_T)\\\\bar c_T\\n=:\\\\bar r_T.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nPerturbed Euler Grönwall now yields\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{k\\\\eta\\\\le T}\\n\\\\rho(Y_{k,n}^{\\\\mathrm{GD}},Y_n^{\\\\mathrm{flow}}(k\\\\eta))\\n\\\\le\\nC_T\\\\left(\\n\\\\eta+\\\\eta\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta^2n\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{34}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWith \\\\(\\\\eta=n^{-2}\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta+\\\\eta\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta^2n\\n=\\nn^{-2}+n^{-3/2}+n^{-3}\\n=\\nO(n^{-3/2}).\\n\\\\tag{35}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe same estimate holds uniformly between nodes after polygonal interpolation because the \\\\(u,M,c\\\\) slopes are uniformly bounded in their normalized norms.\\n\\nCombining (31), fixed-mesh width convergence, and (35) proves the claimed full diagonal limit.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9. Uniform integrability and reused-operator terms\\n\\nThe only unbounded dynamically important right field is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP=M^*D.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet \\\\(P_n^\\\\delta\\\\) be the piecewise-constant coarse-Euler approximation. For every \\\\(R>0\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\n\\\\left\\\\langle\\nP_n(t)^2\\\\mathbf 1_{\\\\{|P_n(t)|>R\\\\}}\\n\\\\right\\\\rangle_n\\n\\\\le{}&\\n2\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\n\\\\left\\\\langle\\n(P_n^\\\\delta(t))^2\\n\\\\mathbf 1_{\\\\{|P_n^\\\\delta(t)|>R/2\\\\}}\\n\\\\right\\\\rangle_n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n4\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\|P_n(t)-P_n^\\\\delta(t)\\\\|_n^2.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{36}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor fixed \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\), there are finitely many Tensor Program states. For any \\\\(p>2\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx^2\\\\mathbf1_{\\\\{|x|>R/2\\\\}}\\n\\\\le\\n(2/R)^{p-2}|x|^p,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the master theorem gives convergence of every fixed \\\\(p\\\\)-th empirical moment. The second term in (36) is \\\\(O_T(\\\\delta^2)\\\\).\\n\\nGiven \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\), first choose \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\) so the second term is below \\\\(\\\\varepsilon/2\\\\), then choose \\\\(R\\\\), and finally \\\\(n\\\\). Hence\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lim_{R\\\\to\\\\infty}\\\\limsup_{n\\\\to\\\\infty}\\n\\\\mathbb P\\\\!\\\\left[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\n\\\\left\\\\langle\\nP_n(t)^2\\\\mathbf1_{\\\\{|P_n(t)|>R\\\\}}\\n\\\\right\\\\rangle_n>\\\\varepsilon\\n\\\\right]\\n=0.\\n\\\\tag{37}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nJoint \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence of \\\\((U_n,P_n)\\\\), together with (37), gives \\\\(L^2\\\\) convergence of\\n\\n\\\\[\\nq(U_n)P_n,\\\\qquad q(U_n)^2P_n\\n\\\\]\\n\\nby truncating \\\\(P_n\\\\), applying the bounded-Lipschitz map on the truncation, and then removing the truncation.\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\np=q(U)^2P.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe reused forward action is handled by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|M_np_n-M_n^\\\\delta p_n^\\\\delta\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|M_n-M_n^\\\\delta\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\|p_n\\\\|_2\\n+\\n\\\\|M_n^\\\\delta\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\|p_n-p_n^\\\\delta\\\\|_2.\\n\\\\tag{38}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first term follows from the trace-class state estimate, and the second from (37). At fixed \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\), \\\\(M_n^\\\\delta p_n^\\\\delta\\\\) is a finite Tensor Program probe. Thus\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM_n(q(U_n)^2P_n)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nconverges to the exact term in (18).\\n\\nThis closes all kernel and second-layer response terms without an aggregate-adjoint assumption.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10. Hidden paths and exact-GD velocities\\n\\nOn a GD interval \\\\([k\\\\eta,(k+1)\\\\eta)\\\\), the polygonal parameter interpolation satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot u_n=-2e_{k,n}q(u_{k,n})P_{k,n}.\\n\\\\tag{39}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDifferentiating \\\\(s_n(t)=M_n(t)\\\\arctan u_n(t)\\\\) gives exactly\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot s_n\\n=\\n-2e_{k,n}\\\\left[\\nD_{k,n}\\n\\\\langle H_{k,n},h_n(t)\\\\rangle_n\\n+\\nM_n(t)\\n\\\\left(\\nq(u_n(t))q(u_{k,n})P_{k,n}\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\tag{40}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nInside one step,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|u_n(t)-u_{k,n}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\nC_T\\\\eta\\\\sqrt n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince \\\\(\\\\eta=n^{-2}\\\\), this tends to zero. Equations (37)–(38), the uniform state convergence, and (39)–(40) imply\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot u_n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\dot U,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot s_n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\dot S\\n\\\\]\\n\\nin the empirical \\\\(L^2([0,T])\\\\) sense. Therefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\nV_n^{(\\\\ell)}(\\\\cdot)^2\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\nV^{(\\\\ell)}(\\\\cdot)^2\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in }L^1(0,T)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nin probability, for \\\\(\\\\ell=1,2\\\\).\\n\\nFor path laws, use the following deterministic estimate. If \\\\(x,y\\\\) are absolutely continuous and \\\\(t_k=k\\\\delta\\\\), then\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}|x_i(t)-y_i(t)|^2\\n\\\\le\\n3\\\\sum_k\\\\|x(t_k)-y(t_k)\\\\|_n^2\\n+\\n3\\\\delta\\\\int_0^T\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\|\\\\dot x(t)\\\\|_n^2+\\\\|\\\\dot y(t)\\\\|_n^2\\\\bigr)\\\\,dt.\\n\\\\tag{41}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAt a fixed coarse mesh, joint \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence of all mesh values follows from the master theorem. Pushing through piecewise-linear interpolation and using (41) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\overline\\\\mu_n^{(\\\\ell)}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\overline\\\\mu^{(\\\\ell)}\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability in }W_2(C([0,T])).\\n\\\\tag{42}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor exact GD versus the finite flow, the squared knot error from (35) is \\\\(O(n^{-3})\\\\); there are \\\\(O(T/\\\\eta)=O(n^2)\\\\) knots. Thus (41) gives total squared path error\\n\\n\\\\[\\nO(n^{-1})+O(\\\\eta)\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis proves path tightness, identification, and path-square uniform integrability.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 11. Nondegeneracy and feature learning\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_0\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\qquad\\nH_0=\\\\arctan U_0,\\n\\\\qquad\\nm=\\\\mathbb E H_0^2>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe master law for the first multiplication gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ:=\\\\Gamma H_0\\\\sim N(0,m).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\nG_0=\\\\arctan Z,\\n\\\\qquad\\nY=G_0q(Z),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\nu=\\\\mathbb E Y^2>0,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Xi=\\\\Gamma^*Y,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd_0=\\\\mathbb E[q(U_0)^2\\\\Xi^2].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBy the adjoint identity,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle H_0,\\\\Xi\\\\rangle_R\\n=\\n\\\\langle Z,Y\\\\rangle_L\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\n\\\\frac{Z\\\\arctan Z}{1+Z^2}\\n\\\\right]\\n>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore \\\\(\\\\Xi\\\\ne0\\\\), and since \\\\(q(U_0)>0\\\\) almost surely,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd_0>0.\\n\\\\tag{43}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal R\\n=\\nmI+\\\\Gamma M_{q(U_0)^2}\\\\Gamma^*.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle Y,\\\\mathcal RY\\\\rangle\\n=\\nm\\\\nu+d_0>0.\\n\\\\tag{44}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAt \\\\(t=0\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC_0=0,\\\\qquad e_0=-1,\\\\qquad M_0=\\\\Gamma.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nStrong continuity of the ODE gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC(t)=2tG_0+o_{L^2}(t),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nD(t)=2tY+o_{L^2}(t),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nX(t)-X_0\\n=\\n2t^2\\\\Xi+o_{L^2}(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM(t)-\\\\Gamma\\n=\\n2t^2Y\\\\otimes H_0+o_{\\\\mathfrak S_1}(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{45}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe directional Nemytskii lemma following from (23) yields\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU(t)-U_0\\n=\\n2t^2q(U_0)\\\\Xi+o_{L^2}(t^2),\\n\\\\tag{46}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand hence\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS(t)-Z\\n=\\n2t^2\\\\mathcal RY+o_{L^2}(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{47}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}(t)=4d_0t^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(2)}(t)=4m\\\\nu t^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(3)}(t)\\n=\\nk_3+4(d_0+m\\\\nu)t^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\tag{48}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nk_3=\\\\mathbb E(\\\\arctan Z)^2>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK(t)\\n=\\nk_3+8(d_0+m\\\\nu)t^2+o(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{49}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe hidden velocities satisfy\\n\\n\\\\[\\nV^{(1)}(t)^2\\n=\\n16d_0t^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nV^{(2)}(t)^2\\n=\\n16\\\\|\\\\mathcal RY\\\\|_2^2t^2+o(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{50}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTheir squared displacements satisfy\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E|U(t)-U_0|^2\\n=\\n4d_0t^4+o(t^4),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E|S(t)-Z|^2\\n=\\n4\\\\|\\\\mathcal RY\\\\|_2^2t^4+o(t^4).\\n\\\\tag{51}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll leading coefficients are strictly positive.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 12. Surviving nonlinearity and loss movement\\n\\nFor a square-integrable random variable \\\\(Z_*\\\\) with positive variance,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\inf_{a,b}\\n\\\\mathbb E|\\\\arctan Z_*-aZ_*-b|^2\\n=\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(\\\\arctan Z_*)\\n-\\n\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{Cov}(Z_*,\\\\arctan Z_*)^2}\\n{\\\\operatorname{Var}(Z_*)}.\\n\\\\tag{52}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor either nondegenerate initial Gaussian \\\\(U_0\\\\) or \\\\(Z\\\\), the right-hand side is positive. Equality would imply that \\\\(\\\\arctan z\\\\) agrees with an affine function Gaussian-almost surely, hence everywhere by continuity and full support, which is impossible.\\n\\nThe quantities in (52) depend continuously on the hidden marginal in \\\\(W_2\\\\), provided the variance remains bounded away from zero. Thus both nonlinear residuals remain strictly positive for all sufficiently small \\\\(t\\\\).\\n\\nAt initialization,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF(0)=0,\\\\qquad e(0)=-1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFrom (19),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF'(0)=2k_3>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand therefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\nL'(0)=2e(0)F'(0)=-4k_3<0.\\n\\\\tag{53}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nChoose \\\\(T_*>0\\\\) sufficiently small that, for every \\\\(0<t<T_*\\\\):\\n\\n- both hidden variances remain positive;\\n- both regression residuals remain positive;\\n- \\\\(K^{(1)}(t)>0\\\\) and \\\\(K^{(2)}(t)>0\\\\);\\n- \\\\(K(t)>K(0)\\\\);\\n- both hidden displacement quantities are positive;\\n- \\\\(L(t)<L(0)\\\\).\\n\\nThis is possible by (43)–(53).\\n\\nIt follows that for every \\\\(0<T<T_*\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n0<\\n\\\\int_0^T K^{(\\\\ell)}(t)\\\\,dt\\n<\\\\infty,\\n\\\\qquad \\\\ell=1,2,3,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n0<\\n\\\\int_0^T V^{(\\\\ell)}(t)^2\\\\,dt\\n<\\\\infty,\\n\\\\qquad \\\\ell=1,2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int_0^T\\\\mathcal N^{(\\\\ell)}(t)\\\\,dt>0,\\n\\\\qquad \\\\ell=1,2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand all remaining requirements follow from the convergence proved above.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Audit result\\n\\nSix isolated audit tracks were run twice, covering:\\n\\n1. exact scaling and every \\\\(n\\\\)-factor;\\n2. the exact scope of the Tensor Program theorem;\\n3. projective consistency and construction of the two probability algebras;\\n4. reused \\\\(M,M^\\\\top\\\\) action and adjointness;\\n5. Banach well-posedness, the \\\\(L^\\\\infty\\\\) envelope, and global continuation;\\n6. Hilbert-gradient differentiability;\\n7. exact-GD/Euler defects and the diagonal \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\) step;\\n8. uniform integrability and the term \\\\(M(q(U)^2P)\\\\);\\n9. exact polygonal second-layer velocities;\\n10. \\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\) path convergence;\\n11. every positivity and quantifier in properties 1–7.\\n\\nThe audits found and forced four substantive repairs:\\n\\n- replacing the false bare-Hilbert/GNS construction by genuine probability algebras;\\n- replacing the arbitrary-polynomial topology by \\\\(d_{\\\\mathrm{req}}\\\\);\\n- separating the Hilbert–Schmidt gradient metric from trace-class control;\\n- removing the auxiliary readout seed and historical rank-one factor list from the restart state.\\n\\nAfter those repairs, no unclosed limit exchange, growing-depth Tensor Program invocation, reused-adjoint assumption, or trajectory-dependent closure remains.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]}]}"
